window.EPPP_TESTS["practice-exam-8"] = {"name": "Practice Exam 8", "slug": "practice-exam-8", "category": "Full Tests", "questionCount": 225, "questions": [{"question": "One difference between Generalized Anxiety Disorder and nonpathological anxiety is that Generalized Anxiety Disorder is more likely to be accompanied by physical symptoms. Additionally, a person with Generalized Anxiety Disorder:", "options": {"A": "feels he or she caused the problems underlying current worries.", "B": "makes repeated, but unsuccessful, attempts to avoid anxiety-arousing stimuli.", "C": "experiences anxiety that is unrelated to speci\u0000c events or actions.", "D": "\u0000nds it di\u0000cult to control his or her worrying."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) involves excessive anxiety and worry about various events or activities. One of the diagnostic criteria for\nGAD is that the person \u0000nds it di\u0000cult to control his or her anxiety and worry.\nAnswer A: This could potentially be true about both GAD and nonpathological anxiety.\nAnswer B: This could potentially be true about both GAD and nonpathological anxiety.\nAnswer C: GAD involves excessive anxiety and worry about various events or activities."}, {"question": "When treating a client with paraphilia, which of the following techniques would be most useful for replacing the inappropriate stimulus that currently elicits sexual arousal with a more appropriate stimulus?", "options": {"A": "Covert sensitization", "B": "Flooding (in vivo exposure)", "C": "Habit reversal training", "D": "Orgasmic reconditioning"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This question is asking about the technique that would change the cue (stimulus) that produces sexual arousal for a client with paraphilia.\nWhen using orgasmic reconditioning, the client is \"reconditioned\" to masturbate to a more appropriate stimulus. This is accomplished by\nhaving the client begin to masturbate while fantasizing about the inappropriate stimulus, switching the fantasy to a more appropriate stimulus\nbefore orgasm.\nAnswer A: When using covert sensitization to eliminate the undesirable behavior, the individual repeatedly imagines engaging in that behavior\n(or a stimulus associated with it) and then imagines an aversive consequence of engaging in the behavior. Eventually, thinking about the\nundesirable behavior produces an aversive response.\nAnswer B: Flooding is used primarily to reduce anxiety responses, and research has not demonstrated that it is effective for the treatment of\nparaphilias.\nAnswer C: Habit reversal training is used to reduce habits such as nail-biting, thumb-sucking, and hair pulling, and research has not supported\nits use as a treatment for paraphilias. Habit reversal training is used to increase the individual's awareness of the habit and replace the habit\nwith alternative behaviors."}, {"question": "A 33-year-old man tells his therapist that, since his involvement in a serious car accident two months ago, he has been feeling sad every day; has had no appetite; feels tired all of the time, and sleeps for 10 to 12 hours each night; isn't interested in spending time with his friends and family; and feels guilty about the accident, even though it wasn't his fault. He also says that because he is having trouble concentrating at work and getting to work on time, he's afraid he's going to be \u0000red. Based on these symptoms, the most likely diagnosis is:", "options": {"A": "bipolar II disorder", "B": "major depressive disorder.", "C": "adjustment disorder.", "D": "acute stress disorder."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The nature, number, and duration of the man's symptoms are consistent with a diagnosis of Major Depressive Disorder. This diagnosis requires\n\u0000ve or more criteria. The man's symptoms include: depressed mood, change in appetite, hypersomnia, diminished interest in activities, and\ninappropriate guilt; his symptoms have lasted for more than two weeks, and he is experiencing impaired occupational functioning as the result\nof his symptoms.\nAnswers A, C, and D: The nature, number, and duration of the man's symptoms are most consistent with a diagnosis of Major Depressive\nDisorder."}, {"question": "Research examining the relationship between expressed emotion and Schizophrenia has demonstrated that:", "options": {"A": "negative expressed emotion is often an early sign of the disorder", "B": "expressed emotion is related more to the negative than the positive symptoms of the disorder", "C": "a high degree of negative expressed emotion by family members is associated with a high risk for relapse for a person diagnosed with this disorder", "D": "an extremely high or extremely low level of expressed emotion by family members is an environmental factor that increases the risk for the development of this disorder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "In the context of Schizophrenia, negative expressed emotion refers to excessive criticism or hostility or emotional overinvolvement and\noverprotectiveness. The research has consistently linked negative expressed emotion by family members to a high risk of relapse and\nrehospitalization for individuals diagnosed with Schizophrenia.\nAnswers A, B, and D: Research examining the relationship between expressed emotion and Schizophrenia has demonstrated that a high degree\nof negative expressed emotion by family members is associated with a high risk for relapse for a person diagnosed with this disorder."}, {"question": "When a clinician does not currently have su\u0000cient information for a \u0000rm diagnosis but believes that the client will eventually meet the full criteria for the diagnosis, the clinician should assign the following:", "options": {"A": "other speci\u0000ed disorder", "B": "unspeci\u0000ed disorder", "C": "disorder NOS", "D": "provisional"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The speci\u0000er provisional may be used when the clinician does not currently have su\u0000cient information for a \u0000rm diagnosis but believes that\nthe client will eventually meet the full criteria for the diagnosis.\nAnswer A: A clinician may code other speci\u0000ed disorders when she wants to indicate the reason why the client's symptoms do not meet the\ncriteria for a speci\u0000c diagnosis.\nAnswer B: When the clinician does not want to indicate the reason that the client's symptoms do not meet the criteria for a speci\u0000c diagnosis,\nhe may code unspeci\u0000ed disorder.\nAnswer C: Disorder NOS is not a DSM-5 diagnosis, code, or speci\u0000er."}, {"question": "Studies have linked the ApoE4 gene on chromosome 19 to an increased risk for:", "options": {"A": "Alzheimer's disease", "B": "Huntington's disease", "C": "diabetes mellitus", "D": "multiple sclerosis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Researchers have identi\u0000ed a link between Alzheimer's disease and speci\u0000c genes on several chromosomes, including chromosome 19. The\nApoE4 gene on chromosome 19 has been associated with an increased risk for both heart disease and late-onset Alzheimer's disease.\nAnswer B: Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant genetic disorder. It has not been linked to the ApoE4 gene.\nAnswer C: Diabetes mellitus is due to insu\u0000cient insulin or the inability of cells to use insulin. It has not been linked to the ApoE4 gene.\nAnswer D: The cause of multiple sclerosis is unknown but may entail a combination of biological and environmental factors."}, {"question": "Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) has high comorbidity rates with other psychological disorders and, according to the results of the National Comorbidity Survey Replication, the most common co-diagnosis is:", "options": {"A": "Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder", "B": "Posttraumatic Stress Disorder", "C": "Social Phobia", "D": "Separation Anxiety Disorder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The National Comorbidity Survey Replication (NCS-R) demonstrated that GAD frequently co-occurs with Mood Disorders, Substance-Related\nDisorders, and other Anxiety Disorders (Kessler, R. C., Chiu, W. T., Demler, O., Merikangas, K. R., & Walters, E. E. (2005). Prevalence, severity, and\ncomorbidity of 12-month DSM-IV disorders in the National Comorbidity Survey Replication. (Archives of general psychiatry, 62(6), 617627).\nAlthough reported comorbidity rates for GAD vary somewhat from study to study, in the NCS-R, the highest correlation (.47) was found between\nGAD and Social Phobia.\nAnswer A: The NCS-R reported a correlation of .33 between GAD and OCD.\nAnswer B: The NCS-R reported a correlation of .44 between GAD and PTSD.\nAnswer D: The NCS-R reported a correlation of .36 between GAD and Separation Anxiety Disorder."}, {"question": "The reduction of parasuicidal behaviors is a primary and explicit target of which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Stress inoculation training", "B": "Self-control therapy", "C": "Eye movement desensitization and reprocessing", "D": "Dialectical Behavior Therapy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Parasuicide refers to deliberate burning, cutting, and other self-injurious behaviors and suicidal gestures without the intent to cause death. DBT\nwas developed as a treatment for Borderline Personality Disorder and explicitly targeted parasuicidal behaviors, a common feature of this\ndisorder. For example, skills training is one component of DBT and includes teaching the client strategies to help regulate emotions and better\ntolerate distress, which decreases the likelihood of parasuicidal behaviors.\nAnswer A: As its name implies, stress inoculation training focuses on helping individuals acquire the skills they need to manage stress more\neffectively. Although some of the skills addressed in stress inoculation training might be useful for reducing parasuicidal behaviors, these\nbehaviors are not \"a primary and explicit target\" of treatment.\nAnswer B: Self-control therapy is a behavioral intervention that is used primarily to reverse undesirable habits (e.g., overeating, smoking) and is\nused to treat depression. However, parasuicidal behaviors are not a primary target of self-control therapy.\nAnswer C: EMDR was initially developed as a treatment for PTSD. Parasuicidal behaviors are not a primary target of EMDR."}, {"question": "Adults with ADHD are most likely to:", "options": {"A": "be excessively perfectionistic in their work", "B": "prefer jobs that involve routine, repetitive tasks", "C": "avoid relationships, especially sexual relationships", "D": "frequently change jobs and intimate partners"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The research has demonstrated that adults diagnosed with ADHD change jobs and intimate partners more often than do individuals without\nthe disorder.\nAnswer A: Due to symptoms of impulsivity, a tendency to make decisions quickly, and attention de\u0000cits, adults diagnosed with ADHD are not\nmost likely to be perfectionistic. Newer research has found that perfectionism/unrelenting high standards are among some common\ndistortions found in the adult ADHD population, but they are more likely to change jobs or intimate partners than those undiagnosed.\nAnswer B: A person with symptoms of ADHD tends to feel bored easily and would not prefer routine, repetitive tasks.\nAnswer C: Adults diagnosed with ADHD often have trouble maintaining relationships but do not avoid them. Regarding sexual relationships,\nadults with ADHD tend to have more sexual partners than adults without ADHD and may engage in more impulsive or risky sexual behaviors."}, {"question": "In children, Generalized Anxiety Disorder:", "options": {"A": "usually \u0000rst develops after a recent loss such as parental divorce or death of a family member", "B": "usually manifests as autonomic hyperarousal and other prominent physical symptoms", "C": "typically manifests as excessive concern about performance or competence at school", "D": "typically involves crying, tantrums, freezing, and clinging to family members, especially among those under 7 years of age"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The symptoms of Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) are somewhat age-related. Children diagnosed with GAD often worry excessively about\ntheir competence or the quality of their performance, and this worry is frequently related to schoolwork and performance in sports activities.\nAnswer A: This is not characteristic of GAD.\nAnswer B: Autonomic hyperarousal is somewhat less prevalent in diagnoses of GAD than in other Anxiety Disorders.\nAnswer D: This is characteristic of children with Social Phobia."}, {"question": "Borderline Personality Disorder is diagnosed most often in individuals ages:", "options": {"A": "19 through 34", "B": "29 through 44", "C": "39 through 54", "D": "49 through 64"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Knowing that the symptoms of Borderline Personality Disorder tend to dissipate with increasing age would have helped you identify the correct\nanswer to this question. The rates of BPD are highest during the young adult years. Although individuals with this disorder often continue to\nexhibit some symptoms throughout their life, by age 40, many no longer meet the diagnostic criteria for BPD.\nAnswers B, C, and D: BPD is diagnosed most often in individuals ages 19-34."}, {"question": "According to Hamburger et al. (1996), psychopathic personality traits interact with biological sex to produce Antisocial Personality Disorder in males and __________ Personality Disorder in females.", "options": {"A": "Histrionic", "B": "Narcissistic", "C": "Borderline", "D": "Schizoid"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The DSM-5 Cluster B Personality Disorders share many characteristics and sometimes co-occur but differ in terms of gender ratios.\nHamburger et al. proposed that Antisocial Personality Disorder (APD) and Histrionic Personality Disorder (HPD) represent gender-related\nmanifestations of the same biological predisposition toward psychopathy. Note, however, that the research has not been entirely supportive of\nthis hypothesis.\nAnswer B: Like Antisocial and Histrionic Personality Disorders, Narcissistic Personality Disorder is categorized within Cluster B; however, this\ndisorder was not included in Hamburger's research on psychopathic personality traits.\nAnswer C: APD and Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD) also share several symptoms (e.g., impulsivity, manipulativeness, and problems with\nrelationships) and are viewed by some experts as gender-related manifestations of a similar pathology. However, this question is asking about\nHamburger et al.'s hypothesis, which describes APD and HPD (not BPD) as sharing underlying psychopathic personality traits.\nAnswer D: Schizoid Personality Disorder falls within Cluster A Personality Disorders and was not included in Hamburger's research."}, {"question": "During a mental status exam, a 36-year old man takes a great deal of time to answer the examiner's questions because he often focuses on unnecessary details and makes parenthetical remarks. When he is not interrupted, the man usually does get around to answering the examiner's question. The man's speech illustrates which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Circumstantiality", "B": "Loosening of associations", "C": "Confabulation", "D": "Derailment"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The important detail to note in the description of the man's speech is that he does eventually get back to the point. Circumstantiality refers to\nspeech that is indirect and delayed in reaching the point because of unnecessary, tedious details and parenthetical remarks.\nAnswer B: Loosening of associations involves a complete lack of connectedness between utterances and the loss of the original point.\nAnswer C: Confabulation involves fabrication of facts or events to compensate for memory loss.\nAnswer D: Derailment is another term for loosening associations."}, {"question": "The use of functional brain imaging techniques to study ADHD has linked its symptoms to lower-than-normal levels of metabolic activity in the:", "options": {"A": "prefrontal cortex and basal ganglia", "B": "prefrontal cortex and ARAS", "C": "entorhinal cortex and SCN", "D": "entorhinal cortex and thalamus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Knowing the functions of the brain areas listed in the responses and the core symptoms of ADHD would have helped you choose the correct\nanswer to this question, even if you are unfamiliar with brain-imaging research. The prefrontal cortex is involved in executive functions (e.g.,\nplanning, decision-making, attention), while the basal ganglia are involved in the regulation of movement and consist of several structures,\nincluding the caudate nucleus and globus pallidus. Researchers have demonstrated a link between abnormalities in these structures and\nADHD.\nAnswers B, C, and D: The use of functional brain imaging techniques to study ADHD has linked its symptoms to lower-than-normal levels of\nmetabolic activity in the prefrontal cortex and basal ganglia."}, {"question": "When an individual presents with one or more motor tics and/or vocal tics that have been present for less than one year and began before age 18, the best diagnosis is:", "options": {"A": "Provisional Tic Disorder", "B": "Persistent Motor or Vocal Tic Disorder", "C": "Tourette's Disorder", "D": "Unspeci\u0000ed Tic Disorder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Provisional Tic Disorder involves one or more motor and/or vocal tics that have been present for less than one year and began before age 18.\nAnswer B: This diagnosis involves one or more motor or vocal tics that have persisted for more than one year and began before age 18.\nAnswer C: A diagnosis of Tourette's Disorder requires the presence of at least one vocal tic and multiple motor tics for more than one year, with\nan onset before 18 years of age.\nAnswer D: This category applies to symptoms that do not meet the full criteria for other tic disorders when the clinician chooses not to specify\nthe reason that the criteria are not met."}, {"question": "A drug that ____________ would be most useful for treating Bulimia Nervosa.", "options": {"A": "increases sensitivity to cortisol", "B": "reduces glutamate levels", "C": "decreases sensitivity to acetylcholine", "D": "increases serotonin levels"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Bulimia Nervosa has been linked to lower-than-normal levels of serotonin. The symptoms of this disorder have been responsive to drugs that\nincrease serotonin levels, such as SSRIs.\nAnswers A, B, and C: A drug that increases serotonin levels would be most useful for treating Bulimia Nervosa."}, {"question": "Studies demonstrate that the ______________ of Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder are similar across cultures.", "options": {"A": "prevalence rates", "B": "prevalence rates and age of onset", "C": "prevalence rates, age of onset, and gender distribution", "D": "prevalence rates, age of onset, gender distribution, and comorbidity"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Studies have generally demonstrated that OCD does not differ signi\u0000cantly across different cultures. The DSM-5 notes that prevalence rates,\nage of onset, gender distribution, comorbidity, and symptom structure of OCD are similar across cultures (APA, 2013, p. 140). However, the\ncontent of obsessions and compulsions may be affected by cultural factors.\nAnswers A, B, and C: Studies have generally demonstrated that OCD does not differ signi\u0000cantly across different cultures."}, {"question": "Your client states that, for the past three months, the government has been tapping his phone, reading his emails, and having someone follow him. He indicates that at one point, he was drugged by \"an agent\" and was \"out for a day or two.\" The client works as a computer programmer for a medical records company, has been married for three years, and has one child. He says that, despite the government's interference in his life, everything else seems to be okay. What is the most likely DSM-5 diagnosis for this client?", "options": {"A": "Paranoid Personality Disorder", "B": "Brief Psychotic Disorder", "C": "Delusional Disorder", "D": "Schizophrenia"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The client's symptoms are most suggestive of Delusional Disorder, a persecutory type.\nAnswer A: The client has a single delusional belief that is limited to the government being out to get him, rather than a pervasive tendency to\nbehave, feel, and perceive the world as hostile and persecutory, which would be more indicative of a personality disorder.\nAnswer B: Despite his delusion about the government, the client appears to be functioning well at work and at home. Brief Psychotic Disorder\nwould cause greater impairment in functioning, and its diagnosis requires a duration of one day to less than one month.\nAnswer D: The nature of the client's symptoms and their duration do not meet the diagnostic criteria for Schizophrenia."}, {"question": "The DSM-5 diagnoses of Bipolar I and Bipolar II Disorders both require at least one episode of abnormally persistent elevated, expansive, or irritable mood:", "options": {"A": "and persistently increased goal-directed activity or energy", "B": "with a lack of awareness of his/her illness and the need for treatment", "C": "that is su\u0000ciently severe to cause a marked impairment in functioning or require hospitalization", "D": "and at least one episode involving a depressed mood or a loss of interest or pleasure in all or most activities"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The DSM-5 diagnostic criteria for Bipolar I and Bipolar II disorder include at least one episode involving abnormally persistent elevated,\nexpansive, or irritable mood with the abnormal and persistent increased goal-directed activity or energy.\nAnswer B: A lack of awareness of the illness and the need for treatment is an associated symptom of Bipolar I disorder but is not a diagnostic\ncriterion for either disorder.\nAnswer C: For a diagnosis of Bipolar I Disorder, symptoms must be su\u0000ciently severe to cause marked impairment in functioning or to\nnecessitate hospitalization to prevent harm to self or others, or psychotic features are present. For a diagnosis of Bipolar II Disorder, symptoms\nmust not be severe enough to cause impairment in functioning or necessitate hospitalization.\nAnswer D: The diagnosis of Bipolar II Disorder requires the presence of both hypomanic and major depressive episodes, whereas a diagnosis\nof Bipolar I Disorder requires only the presence of one or more manic episodes (although it may include hypomanic and/or major depressive\nepisodes)."}, {"question": "A DSM-5 diagnosis of Intermittent Explosive Disorder requires the presence of:", "options": {"A": "verbal aggression or physical aggression toward property, animals, or other people that does not cause injury or destruction, or behavioral outbursts that do cause injury or destruction", "B": "physical aggression that is injurious to animals or people or destructive to property", "C": "physically aggressive acts that are precipitated by an irritable mood and that cause injury to people or animals or destruction to property", "D": "aggressive acts that cause injury to people or animals or destruction to property and are accompanied by a lack of remorse or guilt"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The DSM-5 diagnosis of Intermittent Explosive Disorder requires recurrent behavioral outbursts that re\u0000ect a failure to control aggressive\nimpulses as manifested by either of the following: 1. Verbal or physical aggression toward property, animals, or people that DOES NOT result in\ndestruction or injury 2. Behavioral outbursts that DO result in the destruction of property or injury of people or animals.\nAnswer B: A diagnosis of Intermittent Explosive Disorder does not require physical aggression to result in injury or destruction.\nAnswer C: The aggressive acts associated with Intermittent Explosive Disorder are due to a failure to control aggressive impulses but are not\nnecessarily precipitated by an irritable mood. Also, as noted above, the aggression may not result in injury or destruction.\nAnswer D: The diagnosis of Intermittent Explosive Disorder does not require that aggression be accompanied by a lack of remorse or guilt."}, {"question": "When using the DSM-5, a diagnosis of Major Neurocognitive Disorder requires the presence of a signi\u0000cant decline in:", "options": {"A": "social and occupational functioning from premorbid functioning", "B": "cognitive functioning that interferes with independence in everyday activities", "C": "at least three areas of cognitive functioning", "D": "the ability to perform activities of daily living"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The DSM-5 distinguishes between Major and Mild Neurocognitive Disorders. The diagnosis of a Major Neurocognitive Disorder requires a\nsigni\u0000cant decline from a previous level of cognitive functioning that interferes with independence in everyday activities, while the diagnosis of\na Mild Neurocognitive Disorder requires a modest decline from a previous level of cognitive functioning that does not interfere with\nindependence in everyday activities.\nAnswers A, C, and D: A diagnosis of Major Neurocognitive Disorder requires the presence of a signi\u0000cant decline in cognitive functioning that\ninterferes with independence in everyday activities."}, {"question": "When using the DSM-5, the diagnosis of Delusional Disorder requires the presence of:", "options": {"A": "one or more delusions for at least one month", "B": "one or more nonbizarre delusions for at least one month", "C": "one or more bizarre delusions for at least one month", "D": "one or more bizarre or nonbizarre delusions for at least three months"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The DSM-5 diagnosis of Delusional Disorder requires the presence of one or more delusions that last at least one month.\nAnswers B, C, and D: A diagnosis of Delusional Disorder requires the presence of one or more delusions for at least one month."}, {"question": "Anna, age 34, tells you that she believes she is extremely overweight even though her body mass index is within normal limits. She says that she is mostly concerned about her \"fat thighs and stomach,\" which make her feel ugly and that she's afraid to go out in public because she thinks people will ridicule her. If a DSM-5 diagnosis of Body Dysmorphic Disorder is the appropriate diagnosis for Anna, further questioning will con\u0000rm that:", "options": {"A": "her self-evaluations are unduly in\u0000uenced by her body weight and shape", "B": "she performs repetitive behaviors or mental acts related to her concerns about her weight", "C": "she is preoccupied with having or acquiring a serious illness related to her weight", "D": "she exhibits impairments in cognitive functioning"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The performance of repetitive behaviors or mental acts related to the individual's concerns was added to the DSM-5 as a diagnostic criterion\nfor Body Dysmorphic Disorder.\nAnswer A: Self-evaluations that are unduly in\u0000uenced by body weight and shape is a diagnostic criterion for Bulimia Nervosa and would not\ncon\u0000rm a diagnosis of Body Dysmorphic Disorder.\nAnswer C: A preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness is characteristic of Illness Anxiety Disorder and would not con\u0000rm a\ndiagnosis of Body Dysmorphic Disorder.\nAnswer D: Executive dysfunction (impaired higher-order cognitive skills) is an associated symptom of Body Dysmorphic Disorder. Since it is an\nassociated symptom rather than a diagnostic criterion, it would not be as useful as performing repetitive behaviors or mental acts for\ncon\u0000rming a diagnosis of Body Dysmorphic Disorder."}, {"question": "A diagnosis of Persistent Depressive Disorder requires the presence of a depressed mood on most days for at least ________ for adults and at least ________ for children and adolescents.", "options": {"A": "one year; six months", "B": "18 months; one year", "C": "two years; one year", "D": "36 months; 18 months"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "For a diagnosis of Persistent Depressive Disorder in children and adolescents, mood may be depressed or irritable. Diagnoses of Persistent\nDepressive Disorder (Dysthymia) and Cyclothymic Disorder have the same duration requirement: at least two years for adults and one year for\nchildren and adolescents.\nAnswers A, B, and D: A diagnosis of Persistent Depressive Disorder requires the presence of a depressed mood on most days for at least two\nyears for adults and at least one year for children and adolescents."}, {"question": "A strong desire to be of the other gender is indicative of which of the following diagnoses?", "options": {"A": "Transvestic Disorder", "B": "Frotteuristic Disorder", "C": "Gender Identity Disorder", "D": "Gender Dysphoria"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Gender Dysphoria involves an incongruence between one's experienced/expressed gender and assigned gender. Speci\u0000cally, the individual\nmust express a strong desire to be of the other gender or an insistence that he or she is the other gender (or some alternative gender different\nfrom one's assigned gender).\nAnswer A: Transvestic Disorder involves cross-dressing and does not necessarily involve a desire to be of another gender.\nAnswer B: Frotteuristic Disorder involves intense sexual arousal from touching a nonconsenting adult.\nAnswer C: Gender Identity Disorder is not a DSM-5 diagnosis. This was a DSM-IV term that was replaced by Gender Dysphoria in the DSM-5."}, {"question": "Ben G. says he has continued to experience hallucinations (\"\u0000ashbacks\") several months after he stopped using a drug. He also states that he realizes that this is probably due to the drug. Ben most likely used which of the following?", "options": {"A": "An inhalant", "B": "Methamphetamine", "C": "Cocaine", "D": "Mescaline"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Knowing that mescaline is a hallucinogen would have helped you identify the correct answer to this question. The DSM-5 diagnosis of\nHallucinogen Persisting Perception Disorder involves re-experiencing perceptual disturbances that are reminiscent of those experienced during\nearlier Hallucinogen Intoxication.\nAnswers A, B, and C: Of the substances listed in the answers, only mescaline is a type of hallucinogen."}, {"question": "One approach to distinguishing between depression and anxiety involves considering three dimensions of emotion: negative affect, positive affect, and autonomic arousal. From this perspective, compared to depression, anxiety involves:", "options": {"A": "a similar level of negative affect and autonomic arousal but a higher level of positive affect", "B": "a similar level of negative affect but a higher level of both autonomic arousal and positive affect", "C": "a lower level of negative affect, a similar level of autonomic arousal, and a higher level of positive affect", "D": "a similar level of both negative and positive affect but a higher level of autonomic arousal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Considering the characteristics of depression and anxiety might have helped you choose the correct response to this question even if you were\nunfamiliar with the research. As described in the Abnormal Psychology chapter of the written study materials, diagnoses of depression and\nanxiety involve similar levels of negative affect; however, anxiety is associated with higher levels of positive affect and autonomic arousal\n(Clark, L.A., Watson, D., Mineka, S. Temperament, personality, and the mood and anxiety disorders. J Abnormal Psychology. 1994\nFeb;103(1):103-16).\nAnswers A, C, and D: These responses are incorrect; diagnoses of depression and anxiety involve similar levels of negative affect; however,\nanxiety is associated with higher levels of positive affect and autonomic arousal."}, {"question": "Which of the following is true about the difference between Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder and Obsessive- Compulsive Disorder (OCD)?", "options": {"A": "Obsessions and compulsions in Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder are less severe than those in OCD but begin in early adulthood and are present in a variety of contexts.", "B": "Obsessions and compulsions in Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (but not in OCD) are experienced by the individual as egosyntonic.", "C": "The diagnosis of Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (but not OCD) requires the presence of obsessions and compulsions prior to age 21.", "D": "The diagnosis of OCD (but not Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder) requires the presence of obsessions and/or compulsions."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The name Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD) is misleading because the disorder does NOT require the presence of\nobsessions or compulsions. Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder is characterized by a preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism,\nand control.\nAnswers A, B, and C: OCPD does not include the presence of obsessions or compulsions."}, {"question": "About 10 to 15% of new mothers develop postpartum major depression in the \u0000rst few days or weeks following delivery. Additionally, up to _____% of new mothers exhibit \"baby blues,\" a less severe condition that involves mood swings and sleep disturbances.", "options": {"A": "30", "B": "40", "C": "50", "D": "80"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Researchers generally agree that approximately 80% of new mothers experience some degree of minor mood symptoms after giving birth,\ncalled \"baby blues.\"\nAnswers A, B, and C: Up to 80% of new mothers exhibit the \"baby blues.\""}, {"question": "A DSM-5 diagnosis of Bipolar II Disorder requires the presence or history of:", "options": {"A": "at least one major depressive episode and one hypomanic episode", "B": "at least one major depressive episode and one mixed episode", "C": "two or more mixed episodes without a manic, hypomanic, or major depressive episode", "D": "one or more manic episodes"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with the diagnostic criteria for both Bipolar Disorders. The diagnosis of Bipolar II Disorder requires the\npresence or history of major depressive and hypomanic episodes.\nAnswer B: See explanation for answer A.\nAnswer C: See explanation for response A.\nAnswer D: See explanation for answer A."}, {"question": "When using Atkinson, Morten, and Sue's (1993) Racial/Cultural Identity Development Model, a person's stage of identity development is determined by:", "options": {"A": "identifying his/her degree of acceptance of the minority and majority cultures", "B": "assessing the amount of contact he/she normally has with members of the minority and majority cultures", "C": "assessing his/her attitudes toward the minority and majority cultures", "D": "evaluating his/her reaction to racial oppression"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Atkinson et al.'s model consists of \u0000ve stages: conformity, dissonance, resistance/immersion, introspection, and integrative awareness. Each\nstage in this model is characterized by a different combination of attitudes toward one's own minority group, other minority groups, and the\nmajority (dominant) group.\nAnswer A: Acceptance is not identi\u0000ed as the primary determinant between Atkinson et al.'s stages.\nAnswer B: Contact is not identi\u0000ed as a determinant between the \u0000ve stages.\nAnswer D: One's reaction to racial oppression is not identi\u0000ed as a determinate between Atkinson et al.'s stages."}, {"question": "Interpreting the defenses against anxiety that underlie a client's current maladaptive behavior patterns would be of most interest to a practitioner of:", "options": {"A": "solution-focused therapy", "B": "object relations therapy", "C": "reality therapy", "D": "personal construct therapy"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Defenses are of interest to psychodynamic psychotherapists. As a psychodynamic therapy, object relations therapy utilizes many traditional\npsychodynamic strategies. It focuses on the impact of unconscious processes that impact the client's current relationships and involves\nidentifying and interpreting the client's defenses and transferences.\nAnswer A: Solution-focused therapy adopts a here-and-now orientation and focuses on identifying solutions to problems.\nAnswer C: Reality therapy focuses on current issues and current problems.\nAnswer D: Personal construct therapy combines cognitive, behavioral, and humanistic concepts and focuses on the impact of the individual's\nperspective on his/her experience of the world."}, {"question": "The awareness of and responsiveness to each cultures uniqueness is most consistent with _________?", "options": {"A": "an emic approach", "B": "an etic approach", "C": "an etic-emic synthesis", "D": "an endemic approach"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "An emic approach is characterized by an awareness that each culture possesses unique characteristics. An emic approach recognizes cultural\ndifferences and the impact of those differences on behaviors, attitudes, etc.\nAnswer B: An etic approach applies universal principles that are derived from one culture to all cultures.\nAnswer C: This is a made up term.\nAnswer D: This is a medical term."}, {"question": "What is a unique challenge that comes from using Evidence Based Therapies in public mental health services settings?", "options": {"A": "No universal guidelines provided by the government", "B": "No standardized supervision or training literature", "C": "No procedures for selecting supervisors or trainers", "D": "No testing applications to verify licensing authenticity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "A signi\u0000cant portion of the training and supervision literature within the \u0000eld of clinical psychology does not often address the unique\nchallenges that arise in training in the public mental health service settings. A comprehensive model for providing training in EBTs that can\nappraise local and larger-scale implementation and facilitate training partnerships with publicly funded or community-based agencies and\npractitioners of EBTs has been demonstrated to be most effective.\nAnswers A, C & D: Government guidelines, lack of procedures, and lack of testing applications are not related to any unique challenges with\nemploying evidence-based therapies in public mental health-based settings."}, {"question": "Why does the most pervasive modern form of supervision tend to be the triangular model?", "options": {"A": "Because organizational policies and professional knowledge form the foundation, while the relationship with the supervisor forms the core.", "B": "Because the relationship with the supervisor and professional experience form the foundation while care of clients forms the core.", "C": "Because the relationship with the clients and counseling theory form the foundation and the organizational policies form the core.", "D": "Because the relationship with the organization and the supervisor literature form the foundation and personal experience form the core."}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "One of the common modern forms of supervision is a triangular model where the organizational policies and professional knowledge form the\nfoundation, the supervisor relationship is at the core, and the ultimate emphasis on providing service to the clients.\nAnswers B, C & D: These various answer models are not models of supervision based on the triangular model."}, {"question": "Virginia Satir's approach to family therapy emphasizes:", "options": {"A": "styles of communication", "B": "family projection processes", "C": "boundary disturbances", "D": "transferences between family members"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Virginia Satir's emphasis on communication re\u0000ects her a\u0000liation with the Mental Research Institute in Palo Alto. Satir distinguishes between\n\u0000ve communication styles--placater, blamer, super-reasonable, irrelevant, and congruent.\nAnswer B: This sounds more like Bowen's approach to family therapy.\nAnswer C: Boundary disturbances are most closely associated with Gestalt therapy.\nAnswer D: Satir's approach does not emphasize transferences."}, {"question": "Self-in-relation therapy is an approach to:", "options": {"A": "family therapy that emphasizes the multiple transferences that affect current relationships among family members", "B": "group therapy that focuses on interpersonal interpretations and misinterpretations that affect individual functioning", "C": "feminist therapy that emphasizes the role of the mother-son versus mother-daughter relationship in creating gender differences in behavior", "D": "biopsychosocial therapy that recognizes the interacting impact of biological and sociocultural factors on male and female development"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Self-in-relation therapy was developed at the Stone Center for Developmental Services and Studies at Wellesley College. Object relations theory\nwas an important in\u0000uence on self-in-relation theory. However, self-in-relation theory extends the object relations approach by considering the\nimpact of same versus opposite gender in caregiver-infant relationships on development, especially on development of the relational self.\nAnswer A: This is a description of Object Relations Family Therapy.\nAnswer B: Self-in-relation therapy is not a group therapy approach.\nAnswer D: Self-in-relation therapy is rooted in feminist and object relations theory."}, {"question": "According to Sue et al. (1991) which population is likely to yield better treatment outcomes as a result of being matched with a therapist of the same race?", "options": {"A": "Black clients", "B": "Hispanic clients", "C": "Elderly clients", "D": "Clients under the age of 25"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Sue et al. (1991) found a correlation between ethnic matching and treatment outcomes for Hispanic clients.\nAnswer A: Sue et al. (1991) did not \u0000nd a correlation between ethnic matching and treatment outcomes for Black clients.\nAnswer C: No correlation between treatment outcome and ethnic matching for elderly clients was found.\nAnswer D: The researchers did not \u0000nd a correlation between treatment outcome, ethnic matching, and age."}, {"question": "As described by Sue and Sue (1999), \"Uncle Tom Syndrome\" is a:", "options": {"A": "manifestation of \"reverse racism\"", "B": "sign of self-degradation", "C": "betrayal of one's race", "D": "reaction to generational trauma and systemic oppression"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The term \"Uncle Tom\" originated from Harriet Beecher Stowe's novel, Uncle Tom's Cabin. In this novel, Uncle Tom is a heroic character who is\nbeaten to death for refusing to reveal the whereabouts of two women, fellow slaves, who were hiding from their sexually abusive slave master.\nNonetheless, the term Uncle Tom has become synonymous with subservience. For instance, Sue and Sue (1999) coined the phrase \"Uncle Tom\nSyndrome\" and de\u0000ned it as the use of passivity as a defense mechanism. They further described \"Uncle Tom Syndrome\" as a reaction of\nAfrican Americans to long-term racial oppression (Counseling the culturally different, New York, John Wiley & Sons, 1999).\nAnswer A: One might view this \"syndrome\" as a sign of internalized racism; however, it could not be construed to represent \"reverse racism.\"\nAnswer B: Sue and Sue did not describe this as a sign of self-degradation.\nAnswer C: While the term \"Uncle Tom\" is often used in this sense colloquially, Sue and Sue described \"Uncle Tom Syndrome\" as a defense\nmechanism rather than a betrayal of one's race."}, {"question": "A Bowenian therapist would use a genogram to:", "options": {"A": "examine family relationships over several generations", "B": "identify genetic (biological) contributions to maladaptive behavior", "C": "examine interactions between a family and its environment", "D": "evaluate the progress and outcome of therapy"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "As originally described by Bowen, a genogram is a schematic diagram of a family's relationships and usually includes at least three\ngenerations. A primary purpose of a genogram is to identify behavior patterns that recur in the family.\nAnswer B: Genograms do not provide genetic data.\nAnswer C: This describes an ecomap. Although some authors use the terms ecomap and genogram interchangeably, this question is asking\nspeci\u0000cally about the use of a genogram by a Bowenian therapist, so response a is the best answer.\nAnswer D: Genograms are useful in formulating case conceptualizations rather than measuring treatment outcomes."}, {"question": "An African American individual in the immersion substage of the immersion-emersion stage of Cross's (1991) Black Racial Identity Development Model will exhibit which of the following?", "options": {"A": "A lack of interest in race or racial issues", "B": "Identi\u0000cation with the White (dominant) culture", "C": "Functional paranoia", "D": "Intense involvement in African American culture"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Cross's (1991) Black Racial Identity Development Model distinguishes between four stages: pre-encounter, encounter, immersion-emersion,\nand internalization. An African American individual in the immersion substage is immersed in African American culture, which is evident in\nhis/her interests, speech, style of dress, actions, choice of friends, etc. This individual also feels a great deal of rage toward White supremacy.\nDuring the immersion substage, the person's intense emotions subside, but s/he continues to reject White culture and begins to develop a\nmore sophisticated African American identity.\nAnswer A: A lack of interest in race is characteristic of the pre-encounter stage.\nAnswer B: African American individuals in the assimilation substage of the pre-encounter stage have adopted a mainstream (White) identity.\nAnswer C: Ridley (1984) used the term functional paranoia to refer to an unhealthy condition that is characterized by mistrust and suspicion. A\nperson in the immersion-emersion stage is more likely to exhibit cultural paranoia, which Ridley de\u0000ned as a healthy reaction to racism."}, {"question": "Which of the following statements is accurate regarding the dose-dependent effect?", "options": {"A": "It is not supported by research.", "B": "It involves the relationship between medication dose size and therapeutic effectiveness.", "C": "It relates to the relationship between treatment length and outcome.", "D": "It was proposed by Smith, Glass, and Miller in the 1980's."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with Howard et al.'s (1996) phase model and dose-dependent effect. These researchers found that about\n75% of patients showed measurable improvement at 26 sessions and that this number increased to only about 85% at 52 sessions. These\nauthors refer to this as the dose-dependent effect.\nAnswer A: Howard et al. proposed the phase model based on research related to the dose-dependent effect.\nAnswer B. In psychology, the 'dose' in the phrase dose-dependent effect refers to the number of therapy sessions one attends rather than the\namount of medication one takes.\nAnswer D: Smith, Miller, and Glass used meta-analysis to obtain a mean effect size for therapy effectiveness."}, {"question": "The racial classi\u0000cation Asian American does NOT include Americans with origins in:", "options": {"A": "Cambodia and/or Malaysia", "B": "Thailand and/or Vietnam", "C": "Samoa and/or Fiji", "D": "Pakistan and/or India"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The U.S. Census and other federal de\u0000nitions of the Asian racial/ethnic classi\u0000cation include persons having origins \"in any of the original\npeoples of the Far East, Southeast Asia, or the Indian subcontinent.\" One criticism of the construction of this classi\u0000cation is that it includes\nmore than 20 ethnicities with varying geographical, political, and cultural origins. Therefore, any generalizations about individuals in this\nclassi\u0000cation are likely to have extremely limited utility.\nAnswers A, B, & D: The current federal classi\u0000cation for individuals with origins in these countries is Native Hawaiian and Other Paci\u0000c Islander\n(NHOPI). Previously, Asian and Paci\u0000c Island populations were grouped under the same classi\u0000cations."}, {"question": "The transtheoretical model would best be applied for treatment of:", "options": {"A": "posttraumatic stress disorder", "B": "paranoid schizophrenia", "C": "nicotine use disorder", "D": "autism spectrum disorder"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Knowing that the transtheoretical model was originally developed as an intervention for cigarette smoking and other addictive behaviors would\nlikely have helped you answer this question. Since its inception, the transtheoretical model has been applied to a variety of other behaviors,\nincluding weight control, treatment compliance, intimate partner violence, and \u0000nancial management. However, it is generally regarded as most\neffective in treating conditions where concrete, identi\u0000able behavior change is the focus of treatment.\nAnswer A: Numerous treatment studies have established exposure-based treatment as the most effective treatment option for PTSD.\nAnswer B: Medication management is highly regarded as a necessary treatment for individuals diagnosed with schizophrenia spectrum\ndisorders.\nAnswer D: Applied behavioral analysis (ABA therapy) is widely regarded as the best intervention for individuals diagnosed with this condition."}, {"question": "A solution-focused therapist is working with a family whose conversations frequently escalate to intense arguments. The therapist will most likely:", "options": {"A": "reduce tension during therapy sessions by having family members talk directly to her rather than to each other", "B": "appoint one family member to act as \"referee\" whenever the family \u0000ghts at home", "C": "ask the family members if they can remember a time when they were able to talk to each other for an extended period without arguing", "D": "instruct the family members to argue with each other for at least one hour each evening"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "As its name implies, solution-focused therapy focuses on solutions rather than problems. Solution-focused therapists use \"exceptions\" to help\nidentify solutions to a client's problems. An exception is a time when the problem did not exist or existed in a less intense form.\nAnswer A: This sounds more like the approach a Bowenian family therapist would adopt.\nAnswer B: This isn't characteristic of the solution-focused approach.\nAnswer D: This is not characteristic of this approach."}, {"question": "During the \u0000rst session with an American Indian family consisting of a grandmother, mother, father, and three children, an Anglo American therapist would be best advised to:", "options": {"A": "refer the family to an American Indian therapist", "B": "treat the family like any other family unless there is a clear reason to do otherwise", "C": "assess each family member's level of acculturation before developing a treatment plan", "D": "ensure that she establishes a \"value-free\" environment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "You may have been able to identify the correct response to this question through the process of elimination.\nAnswer A: This may be unnecessary if the therapist has appropriate training or seeks consultation. An initial step when working with clients\nfrom culturally diverse groups is to identify the client's stage of racial/ethnic identity development, degree of acculturation, and worldview\n(beliefs, values, attitudes, etc.).\nAnswer B: Utilizing a \"colorblind\" approach would be unethical. Ethical mandates require psychologists to consider the importance of cultural\nfactors impacting the client and the therapeutic process.\nAnswer D: This also would not be culturally competent or ethical. It is impossible to establish a \"value-free\" environment. Psychologists are\ninstead mandated to recognize their values and biases and maintain an awareness of how this impacts the therapeutic process."}, {"question": "A White female client feels that the primary obstacle in her life is sexism and that there is nothing she can do to change the problems she is experiencing. As de\u0000ned by Sue (1978), she has an:", "options": {"A": "internal locus of control and internal locus of responsibility", "B": "external locus of control and internal locus of responsibility", "C": "internal locus of control and external locus of responsibility", "D": "external locus of control and external locus of responsibility"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Even if you are unfamiliar with Sue's conceptualization of worldview, you probably could have identi\u0000ed the correct response by considering the\ninformation provided in the question. This individual has an external locus of responsibility (she can't do anything about her problems) and an\nexternal locus of control (she believes her problems are due to sexism).\nAnswers A, B, & C: As de\u0000ned by Sue this individual is exhibiting an EC-ER worldview."}, {"question": "Meta-analysis of therapist/client racial/ethnic matching suggests that:", "options": {"A": "clients may be more likely to remain in treatment when they are matched with therapists of their same race or ethnicity", "B": "client/therapist matching has no effect", "C": "client/therapist matching greatly improves treatment outcomes", "D": "clients are less likely to remain in treatment when they are matched with therapists of their same race or ethnicity"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Cabral and Smith (2011) found that clients prefer therapists of their own race and perceive therapists of their own race somewhat more\npositively than other therapists. This is consistent with the \u0000ndings of meta-analysis related to therapist/client racial matching. For instance,\nSmith and Trimble (2016) found that clients are more likely to remain in treatment when they are matched with therapists of their same race or\nethnicity. [Smith, Timothy B. & Trimble, Joseph E. (2016). Foundations of multicultural psychology: Research to inform effective practice, (pp.\n115-128). Washington, DC, US: American Psychological Association, viii, 308 pp.]\nAnswer B: This is not true. Numerous studies have found a correlation between length of treatment and client/therapist matching.\nAnswer C: While studies have shown that therapist/client matching increases adherence to treatment, correlations with treatment outcome\nhave not been highlighted.\nAnswer D: The opposite is true."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned by Minuchin, triangulation is a:", "options": {"A": "healthy adaptation by family members to unexpected change", "B": "way for family members to avoid dealing with stress or con\u0000ict", "C": "method used by the therapist to diagnose the family structure", "D": "method used by the therapist to reduce stress in the family"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Minuchin described triangulation as a type of rigid triad. Triangulation occurs when parents are in con\u0000ict, and both try to recruit a child to their\nside to avoid or reduce the con\u0000ict between them.\nAnswer A: Triangulation, as described by Minuchin, is an unhealthy dynamic.\nAnswer C: Triangulation is not a therapist intervention.\nAnswer D: Triangulation relates to a dynamic initiated by a client rather than the therapist."}, {"question": "A practitioner of Minuchin's structural family therapy uses which of the following to alter the hierarchical relationships within a family system or subsystem?", "options": {"A": "Unbalancing", "B": "Tracking", "C": "Reframing", "D": "Mimesis"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with the four techniques listed in the answers to this question. Unbalancing is a restructuring technique\nthat is used by structural family therapists to change hierarchical relationships between family members. It may involve a\u0000liating with a family\nmember, ignoring a family member, or entering into a coalition with a family member against another member.\nAnswer B: Tracking is a joining technique and involves following the verbal and nonverbal content of the family's communications by, for\nexample, asking clarifying questions, showing interest in what they're saying, and connecting things they've said in the past to current\ninteractions.\nAnswer C: Reframing is a paradoxical technique that involves offering an alternative interpretation of the meaning of a behavior or event.\nAnswer D: Mimesis is another joining technique and involves adopting the family's affective and communication style."}, {"question": "When 11-year old Samantha plays with her 5-year old brother Sam, she often assumes the role of \"mother\" and teaches Sam how to do things. Sam cooperates by doing what Samantha instructs him to do. The interaction between Samantha and her brother is best described as:", "options": {"A": "complementary", "B": "symmetrical", "C": "parallel", "D": "disengaged"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "In this situation, Samantha is the \"teacher\" while her younger brother is the \"student.\" Complementary transactions are transactions between\nindividuals who are not equals - e.g., between a dominant and a subordinate person.\nAnswer B: Symmetrical transactions occur between equals.\nAnswer C: Parallel transactions are a combination of complementary and symmetrical transactions.\nAnswer D: The term \"disengaged\" is not used to describe the type of transaction presented in this question."}, {"question": "Providing social skills training to children whose aggressive behaviors have already had a substantial adverse impact on their family and peer relations and school performance is an example of:", "options": {"A": "primary prevention", "B": "secondary prevention", "C": "tertiary prevention", "D": "behavioral prevention"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Three types of prevention are distinguished in the literature - primary, secondary, and tertiary. Tertiary preventions are aimed at individuals who\nhave already developed a disorder. Their goal is to reduce the risk for chronicity and relapse and any other problems related to the disorder.\nAnswer A: Primary preventions are aimed at reducing the development and incidence of mental disorders.\nAnswer B: Secondary preventions target individuals who have just begun to exhibit symptoms of a disorder in order to prevent the disorder\nfrom becoming more serious.\nAnswer D: This is a made up term."}, {"question": "The primary advantage of manual-guided treatment is that the use of manuals:", "options": {"A": "standardizes treatment delivery so that, in research, treatment effects can be more clearly ascertained", "B": "substantially improves treatment outcomes across different clients, diagnoses, and contexts", "C": "reduces the legal liability of clinicians for \"treatment errors\"", "D": "ensures that the procedures used to evaluate treatment effectiveness are valid"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Manual-guided treatments provide manuals that specify the theoretical underpinnings of the treatment and treatment goals and present\nspeci\u0000c therapeutic guidelines and strategies. Treatment manuals were initially developed to standardize treatments so that their effects could\nbe empirically evaluated and to provide training guidelines for therapists.\nAnswer B: The research on this issue is inconsistent but has generally not found manual-guided treatments to have substantially better\noutcomes than treatments that are not guided by the use of a manual.\nAnswer C: This has not been identi\u0000ed as an outcome of the use of treatment manuals.\nAnswer D: This is not one of the bene\u0000ts of treatment manuals."}, {"question": "Gary previously felt confused about his sexual orientation, but now that he is certain that he is gay, he is considering adopting an openly gay life. Gary is currently best categorized as being in which of Troiden's four stages of identity development?", "options": {"A": "Identity Assumption", "B": "Sensitization/ Feeling Different", "C": "Self-Recognition/Identity Confusion", "D": "Commitment/ Identity Integration"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Attention to a few keywords and familiarity with the four stages of Troiden's Homosexual Identity Development Model may have helped you\nidentify the correct answer. Identity Assumption is the third stage of Troiden's model. During this stage, which typically occurs during middle\nchildhood, individuals become more certain of their sexual orientation.\nAnswer B: This is the \u0000rst stage of Troiden's model and is characterized by feeling different from one's peers.\nAnswer C: This is the second stage of Troiden's model. During this stage, which typically begins at the onset of puberty, an individual\nexperiences confusion and turmoil as feelings of same-sex attraction are realized. Gary appears to have recently emerged from this stage.\nAnswer D: This is the fourth and \u0000nal stage of Troiden's model. It appears that Gary may be on the verge of entering this stage as he\ncontemplates publicly disclosing his sexual orientation."}, {"question": "During the fourth session, a therapy client says he \"is already feeling much better.\" Applying Howard and colleagues' (1996) phase model of therapy, which phase is the client most likely in?", "options": {"A": "Remediation", "B": "Rehabilitation", "C": "Remoralization", "D": "Restoration"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Howard and colleagues (1996) proposed a three-phase model of therapy that predicts the bene\u0000ts of therapy by the number of sessions. They\nindicated that during the \u0000rst few sessions of therapy, a client experiences an increase in hopefulness. This stage is referred to as\nremoralization.\nAnswer A: During the second phase of therapy (remediation), the focus is on symptoms. Clients begin experiencing real symptom relief by\nsession 16.\nAnswer B: During the third stage of therapy (rehabilitation), the focus is on unlearning maladaptive behaviors and establishing healthy\nbehavioral patterns.\nAnswer D: This is not one of the phases of therapy proposed by Howard and colleagues."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned by Gerald Caplan, the target of consultee-centered case consultation is the consultee's skills, knowledge, abilities, and/or objectivity. Caplan describes which of the following as a potential cause of a consultee's lack of objectivity?", "options": {"A": "Projective identi\u0000cation", "B": "Diagnostic overshadowing", "C": "Parallel process", "D": "Theme interference"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Caplan distinguishes between four types of mental health consultation: client-centered case consultation, consultee-centered case\nconsultation, program-centered administrative consultation, and consultee-centered administrative consultation. Caplan de\u0000nes theme\ninterference as a type of transference that occurs when a consultee's unresolved con\u0000ict related to a particular type of client or situation\ninterferes with his/her current performance with similar clients or in similar situations.\nAnswer A: This concept is associated with object relations theory.\nAnswer B: Diagnostic overshadowing is a term that captures the bias which leads clinicians to attribute all of a person's psychiatric symptoms\nto his or her intellectual disability.\nAnswer C: Parallel process occurs when a therapist (supervisee) replicates problems and symptoms with the supervisor that are being\nmanifested by the therapist's client."}, {"question": "The Buckley Amendment ___________________________.", "options": {"A": "establishes the right of the school psychologist to determine who should have access to information included in school records", "B": "establishes the right of parents to inspect the school records of their children", "C": "establishes the right of patients or their legal representatives to have access to their hospital records", "D": "requires that parental consent be obtained before a student can be subjected to a psychological or psychiatric examination"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The Buckley Amendment is another name for the Family Education Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA). Among other things, the Buckley\nAmendment states that any school district may be denied federal funds if parents of students (or other legal guardians) or students who have\nreached the age of majority are not given access to their school records. It also prohibits disclosure of school records to unauthorized\nindividuals without parental consent. See the chapter on ethics and professional issues in the written study materials for additional information\non the Buckley Amendment.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. While school psychologists will likely deal with the Buckley Amendment frequently, it does not assure rights\nfor school psychologists.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. The Buckley Amendment applies to records within educational institutions.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The Buckley Amendment does not serve this purpose."}, {"question": "A research psychologist designs a study that involves hospitalized psychiatric patients who have legal guardians. Before beginning the study, he obtains signed consent from the legal guardians. The psychologist has acted _______________________________.", "options": {"A": "unethically since he has breached the patients' con\u0000dentiality", "B": "ethically as long as the patients are allowed to withdraw from the study at any time", "C": "ethically as long as he has also obtained assent from the patients", "D": "ethically, since all a psychologist must obtain in this situation is the informed consent of legal guardians"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This issue is addressed in Standard 3.10 of the APA's Ethics Code. When research participants are not legally capable of giving informed\nconsent, either because they are mentally incompetent or minors, legal guardians must give consent and participants should give assent. This\nanswer is consistent with ethical responsibilities.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Con\u0000dentiality is not the issue of concern in this case.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Standard 8.02(a) dictates that participants have the right to withdraw from research at any time. However,\nthis is not the best answer since it does not address the questions underlying ethical issue, which is the need for psychologists to obtain\nassent from participants who are legally incapable of giving informed consent.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Ethical guidelines require that psychologists obtain assent from potential participants and consent from\nthe patients' legal guardians when participants cannot legally give consent."}, {"question": "The fee for a 23-year-old woman suffering from depression is being paid by her parents. In terms of con\u0000dentiality, in this situation __________________________.", "options": {"A": "the parents are the holders of the privilege since they are paying the therapist's fee", "B": "the parents should be supplied with general information about their daughter's progress since they are paying the therapist's fee and, therefore, are the \"users\" of the therapist's services", "C": "the therapist should relate only the information she believes the parents will understand", "D": "the therapist should, at the onset of treatment, establish a policy about con\u0000dentiality jointly with the daughter and her parents"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This is the most ethical alternative. In a case similar to the one described in this question (APA Casebook, 1987), the parents \u0000led a complaint\nagainst the psychologist when he would not release information to them about their daughter. The Ethics Committee decided that the\npsychologist acted ethically by not releasing the information since he had clari\u0000ed this policy in advance with the parents and the daughter at\nthe onset of treatment.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Payment of fees does not give a party the right to information about a client's progress in therapy.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Again, payment of fees does not mean that the parents need to be given information (speci\u0000c or general)\nunless this has been explicitly decided upon by all parties.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. This response does not address the issue of con\u0000dentiality presented by the question."}, {"question": "A legal guardian has been appointed by the court for a 9-year-old child after his biological parents are found guilty of child abuse. The legal guardian brings the child to you for treatment. You should _________________________.", "options": {"A": "obtain permission from both of the child's biological parents before providing treatment", "B": "obtain permission from one of the child's biological relatives before providing treatment", "C": "obtain permission from the court before providing treatment", "D": "provide the treatment to the child as requested by the guardian"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "In most cases, the biological parents are the legal guardians of a minor child. However, in this case, the court has appointed a legal guardian.\nThe child's court-appointed guardian has the right to request treatment for the child.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Approval from the child's biological parents is not necessary.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect, as permission from a biological relative is not required.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. The court has appointed a legal guardian who has the right to request treatment for the child."}, {"question": "A new client says she wants you to give her an IQ test so that she can use the results to join MENSA. You administer the test, but she obtains an IQ score of only 105. The client asks you to give her the test results so that she can destroy them, and she informs you that she is going to be retested by another psychologist. You should ______________________.", "options": {"A": "give the original record of the test results to the client as requested", "B": "destroy the original record of the test results yourself", "C": "tell the client you will forward the original record to the other psychologist", "D": "give the client a summary of the test results and retain the original record in your \u0000les"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This issue is addressed by R. Woody in \"Legally safe mental health practice: Psycholegal questions and answers,\" Madison, CT, Psychosocial\nPress, 1997. Woody notes that \"No records, be they test results or otherwise, should ever be destroyed simply because one person, be it client\nor therapist, does not like the content\" (p. 68). The client has the right to information about her test results, but the record itself belongs to the\ntherapist, who has a \"limited property right\" in the document.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. The psychologist should keep the original test results.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Records are to protect both the client and the therapist, and destroying a record at the request of the client\nwould not necessarily be in the best interests of either the client or therapist.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. In this case, you would not release the records to another psychologist without the client's permission."}, {"question": "A psychologist in private practice who has been sued once in the past by a client for malpractice and who fears being sued again decides to limit his practice to clients whose problems are not highly associated with higher rates of malpractice claims. This is ___________________.", "options": {"A": "unethical if he is competent to treat these clients", "B": "ethical only if he is willing to see the clients with higher malpractice claims in emergencies and other unusual circumstances", "C": "ethical as long as he makes appropriate referrals", "D": "ethical as long as he makes his policy clear at the outset of treatment"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Although you may question the therapist's decision, therapists are not required to see patients they do not want to see. This issue is addressed\nby R. I. Simon in Clinical psychiatry and the law (American Psychiatric Press, Washington, DC, 1992). Although Simon is discussing the\nobligations of psychiatrists, the same principles apply to psychologists. Self-employed mental health professionals do not have a legal or\nethical duty to enter into professional relationships. While the psychologist's decision may seem unfair to some, he is free to serve the clients\nhe chooses. He should, however, make appropriate referrals and not discriminate on the basis of race, gender, or any other basis proscribed by\nlaw. Litigious clients do not fall into this category, however.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Psychologists are not required to see patients simply because they are quali\u0000ed to work with them.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. There is no requirement for a psychologist to work with non-patients in emergencies or unusual situations.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The psychologist would not want to begin therapy with a patient for whom he is likely to terminate\ntreatment in the near future. If the psychologist wants to avoid taking on litigious clients, it would be better not to begin a therapeutic\nrelationship with them at all."}, {"question": "Research on sexual and nonsexual dual relationships between therapists and their clients has found that ________________________.", "options": {"A": "male therapists engage in more sexual dual relationships, but female therapists engage in more nonsexual dual relationships", "B": "the rates of sexual dual relationships are higher among those with less professional experience", "C": "in a sexual dual relationship, the client is, on average, 10 years younger than the therapist", "D": "therapists who undergo personal therapy are less likely to become sexually involved with their clients"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "As noted in the chapter on ethics and professional issues in the written study materials, several surveys have been conducted to determine\nfactors related to sexual misconduct by mental health professionals.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Male therapists are involved in substantially more sexual and nonsexual dual relationships than female\ntherapists. The average age of therapists who become involved in sexual dual relationships is 42 or 43 (depending on which study you read),\nwhile the average age of the client is 32 or 33. See K. S. Pope, \"Sexual feelings in psychotherapy: Explorations for therapists and therapists-in-\ntraining,\" American Psychological Association, Washington, DC, 1993.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. This answer is not necessarily true. Some research indicates higher rates among the more experienced.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The research is not entirely consistent on this issue. Most studies have found either that there is no effect\nof therapy or that therapy is associated with an increased risk for sexual involvement."}, {"question": "If a psychologist is requested by a defense attorney to evaluate and testify about a defendant for the purpose of establishing the defense of insanity, the psychologist should _____________________.", "options": {"A": "obtain an order from the court before agreeing to evaluate the defendant", "B": "evaluate the defendant and testify as requested but inform the defendant of the purpose of the evaluation and of the limits of con\u0000dentiality before doing so", "C": "evaluate the defendant as requested and not be concerned about informing the defendant of the limits of con\u0000dentiality since privilege is waived in this situation", "D": "refuse to evaluate the defendant because psychologists are not quali\u0000ed to determine if a person is \"insane\""}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Claiming insanity as a defense is one of the times when privilege is waived. However, precautions regarding con\u0000dential information must still\nbe taken. Although the psychologist would want to refrain from concluding whether or not the defendant is \"insane,\" he/she can testify about\nthe client's mental state at the time of the crime. Also, while privilege is waived when a defendant claims insanity, the defendant should be\ninformed of the limits of con\u0000dentiality prior to the evaluation.\nAnswer A: An order from the court is not necessary in this case.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. Psychologists should inform the defendant of the purpose of the evaluation and of the limits of\ncon\u0000dentiality.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Psychologists should not conclude whether or not a person is \"insane,\" but they can draw conclusions\nabout a person's mental status, which the court then uses to make a determination of insanity."}, {"question": "A former therapy client calls you and expresses an interest in becoming friends. The former client suggests that you meet at a local restaurant for lunch. You remember that the client shared many of your interests and that, at the time of therapy, you were aware that he/she was someone you would like to know better. As an ethical psychologist, you ____________________.", "options": {"A": "meet the former client for lunch only if the therapeutic relationship was terminated more than two years ago", "B": "meet the former client for lunch only if the therapeutic relationship was terminated more than two years ago and you believe that a relationship is not clinically contraindicated and is not likely to be exploitative", "C": "meet the client for lunch to clarify and discuss any potential con\u0000icts and then proceed with caution", "D": "do not meet the client for lunch and explain why a friendship would not be in the client's best interests"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Friendships with former clients are covered by the multiple relationship clause in the Ethics Code. In most situations, a psychologist would\nwant to avoid friendships with former clients. Most experts agree that friendships with former clients should be avoided since this sets up an\nunequal situation and precludes the possibility that the former client can return for therapy in the future.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Psychologists should avoid establishing friendships with former clients. The power imbalance inherent in\nsuch relationships and the fact that the client would be unable to return for therapy make this arrangement unwise.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. The two-year limit applies to sexual relationships, which this question is not addressing.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. Establishing a friendship with a former client should be avoided."}, {"question": "Dr. K. has just started working at a community mental health center. Several of his new clients belong to an ethnic minority group that he has not had prior experience working with. Dr. K. should _______________.", "options": {"A": "refuse to see the clients because he has not had adequate experience", "B": "see the clients only if he has completed appropriate coursework on cross-cultural counseling", "C": "ask the clients if they desire a referral and, if so, refer them to another therapist", "D": "see the clients but obtain appropriate supervision or consultation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The ethical guidelines recommend that therapists seek appropriate supervision or consultation when working with clients who belong to an\nunfamiliar group or population. In some situations, a referral would be an appropriate course of action, but supervision or consultation seems\nbest in this case. During the course of his/her career, a therapist will ordinarily want to acquire new skills to expand his/her practice to include\nnew kinds of clients. This is particularly true for Dr. K. since he is working at a clinic where some clients are apparently members of minority\ngroups.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Given Dr. K is working in a location where some of his clients are from a minority group with which he is\nless familiar, he needs to seek consultation and training to serve them.\nAnswer B: Coursework along will likely be insu\u0000cient. Dr. K. should supplement any coursework with additional training and supervision or\nconsultation.\nAnswer C: Given Dr. C. will likely have many diverse clients in his current setting, he should seek additional training and supervision in order to\nserve them."}, {"question": "Dr. Billings, a psychologist, asks a graduate student to help him with a research project. The student does most of the initial library research, administers the tests and interviews to subjects, and writes some of the article that Dr. Billings will submit to a professional journal. When the article is published, Dr. Billings should make sure that the graduate student is __________________________.", "options": {"A": "listed as the \u0000rst author", "B": "listed as a co-author", "C": "acknowledged in a footnote", "D": "acknowledged in an introductory statement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Standard 8.12(b) of the 2002 Ethics Code states that publication credits \"accurately re\u0000ect the relative scienti\u0000c or professional contributions\nof the individuals involved, regardless of their relative status.\" The graduate student has written some of the article and, therefore, should be\nlisted as a co-author.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. First authorship is reserved for the principal contributor, who, in this case, seems to be the psychologist.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Minor contributions are often acknowledged in a footnote or introductory statement. However, the student\nmade a substantial contribution to the research and the journal article.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Given the student's signi\u0000cant contributions to the project, an introductory statement would be an\ninsu\u0000cient acknowledgment."}, {"question": "Dr. Rafael, a licensed psychologist, requires his Psychology 101 students to participate in two research studies conducted by graduate students or professors at the university as part of the course requirements. This requirement ____________.", "options": {"A": "is unethical under any circumstances", "B": "is ethical only if students are aware of the requirement prior to enrolling in the class", "C": "is ethical only if students are given a choice of alternative assignments", "D": "is ethical as long as the research has been approved by the university's Institutional Review Board"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This issue is addressed in Standard 8.04(b) of the APA's Ethics Code, which states that \"when research participation is a course requirement or\nan opportunity for extra credit, the prospective participant is given the choice of equitable alternative activities.\"\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Participation provides students an opportunity to learn about psychological research. However, the Ethics\nCode requires students have an alternative means of earning credit.\nAnswer B: The language of this answer dies not to align with Standard 8.04(b).\nAnswer D: This answer does not address the more central issue, which is that students must have other alternatives."}, {"question": "The Publication Manual of the American Psychological Association states that some abbreviations do not require explanation in text. These include all of the following except ____________.", "options": {"A": "IQ", "B": "HIV", "C": "APA", "D": "ESP"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The Publication Manual states that abbreviations that appear as word entries in the Webster's Collegiate Dictionary (i.e., those without the label\n\"abbr\") do not require explanation in text. You may have been able to recognize this as the correct answer as long as you recalled that \"APA\"\ncould stand for several organizations, including the American Psychological Association and the American Psychiatric Association. Therefore,\nyou would need an explanation the \u0000rst time that it's used. The abbreviations listed in the other answers are provided in the Publication Manual\nas examples of abbreviations that do not require an explanation.\nAnswer A: The Publication Manual lists IQ as an example of an abbreviation that does not require an explanation.\nAnswer B: HIV does not require an explanation per the Publication Manual.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The Publication Manual does not include ESP as an abbreviation that needs additional explanation."}, {"question": "An ongoing debate in the literature centers on a therapist's responsibility when working with a client who is HIV-positive and is engaging in risky sexual behavior. A central issue in this debate is _______.", "options": {"A": "social responsibility", "B": "client abandonment", "C": "foreseeability of harm", "D": "vicarious liability"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "As noted in the chapter on ethics and professional issues in the written study materials, this issue is often discussed within the framework of\nthe Tarasoff decision. In discussing this topic, S. Knapp and L. VandeCreek, for example, note that three issues are involved in the duty to\nprotect: a \u0000duciary relationship, an identi\u0000able victim, and the foreseeability of harm. They note that the \"issue of foreseeability creates the\ngreatest problems with the duty to protect with HIV-positive patients because the research has not identi\u0000ed all the risk factors involved in HIV\ntransmission\" (Application of the duty to protect to HIV-positive patients, Professional Psychology: Research and Practice, 21(3), 161-166,\n1990).\nAnswer A: Social responsibility is not the central issue discussed in the literature.\nAnswer B: Abandonment is not the central issue discussed regarding HIV-positive patients.\nAnswer D: Vicarious liability is an issue in supervision, but not in reference to working with HIV-positive clients."}, {"question": "An employee is referred for counseling through his company's employee assistance program by his employer. In this situation, the psychologist can let the employee's supervisor know that the employee is receiving treatment _________.", "options": {"A": "under no circumstance", "B": "under any circumstance", "C": "as long as the employee has signed an authorization for release of con\u0000dential information", "D": "as long as no other information about the treatment is given to the supervisor"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "EAP is one of the unique exceptions to con\u0000dentiality. When an employee is referred to an EAP by their employer, the employer is allowed to\nknow the attendance record of that employee, even without a release of information. Unless the employee has been referred by the employer\nspeci\u0000cally for a \"\u0000tness for duty evaluation\", all other clinical information would require a release to be shared.\nAnswer A: This is an incorrect statement. Con\u0000dentiality may be breached in the context of EAPs in those situations that it can be breached in\nother contexts (e.g., when a client has signed an authorization or is believed to be a danger to him/herself or to others).\nAnswer B: The source of the referral does not impact con\u0000dentiality.\nAnswer C: When an employee uses an EAP, their employer is allowed to know their attendance record without a release of information."}, {"question": "An insurance company requests that you fax the company con\u0000dential client information. Assuming that the client has signed a release for this information, you should ______________________________.", "options": {"A": "refuse to fax the requested documents", "B": "make sure the documents are clearly marked as con\u0000dential", "C": "make sure that all identifying information is removed or coded", "D": "ask the company to verify that the documents have been received after they are faxed"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Of the available answers, this is the best one. Standard 6.02 of the APA's Ethics Code addresses the con\u0000dentiality of client records. Once\ndocuments are faxed, you do not have control over who sees them. Removing identifying information is one way of assuring client information\nis kept con\u0000dential.\nAnswer A: It would not be necessary to refuse to fax the requested information unless doing so would breach client con\u0000dentiality.\nAnswer B: Marking the material \"con\u0000dential\" would not guarantee that client con\u0000dentiality would be maintained.\nAnswer D: This would not necessarily be adequate for ensuring that someone other than an authorized person has had access to the\ndocuments."}, {"question": "You are working in a correctional facility and are asked to evaluate a prisoner to determine his eligibility for parole. In this situation, you should ____________.", "options": {"A": "conduct the evaluation as requested since it is part of your job", "B": "conduct the evaluation only if you believe it will serve a useful dispositional function", "C": "conduct the evaluation after reminding the prisoner that anything he says can be shared with prison authorities", "D": "refuse to conduct the evaluation unless it has been court-ordered"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Relatively little has been written about ethical considerations in correctional facilities, so few guidelines are available. However, APA published\nrecommendations in a 1978 issue of the American Psychologist that apply to this situation (Report of the task force on the role of the\npsychologist in the criminal justice system, American Psychologist, 33, pp. 1099-1133, 1978). Recommendation 3 of the document states that\n\"Other than for legitimate research purposes, psychological assessments of offenders should be performed only when the psychologist has a\nreasonable expectation that such assessments will serve a useful therapeutic or dispositional function.\"\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Psychologists are required to follow ethical and legal mandates and not simply follow the directives of their\nemployer.\nAnswer C: APA recommends that the \"ideal level of con\u0000dentiality of therapeutic services in criminal justice settings should be the same as the\nlevel of con\u0000dentiality that exists in voluntary noninstitutional settings.\" Although there certainly are exceptions to con\u0000dentiality in prison\nsettings that would not apply elsewhere, it is not true that a prisoner has no right to con\u0000dentiality, which this response implies.\nAnswer D: This answer is too absolute and fails to account for the guidance provided in professional documents."}, {"question": "A psychologist has been seeing a couple in therapy for six months, and they have recently decided to get a divorce. The psychologist is asked by the husband to testify on his behalf in a court custody hearing. The psychologist should _______________.", "options": {"A": "refuse to testify", "B": "agree to testify as long as she does not reveal con\u0000dential information about the wife", "C": "agree to testify only if the wife gives consent for her to do so", "D": "agree to testify only if she will be testifying for both parties"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "When working with a couple, the couple is considered the client, and the welfare of the client must always come \u0000rst. As a general rule, a\ntherapist should not provide services to one member of a couple when the couple has been the client.\nAnswer B: It would be nearly impossible to separate the information the psychologist could potentially share, limiting it to one party or the\nother.\nAnswer C: Even if the wife consents, it is not possible to foresee a situation that might put her welfare in jeopardy if the therapist testi\u0000es on\nbehalf of the husband.\nAnswer D: Incorrect The action described in response d could similarly put the therapist in a very awkward position and jeopardize the welfare\nof one or both of the parties."}, {"question": "With regard to terminating the life of animals used in research studies, the APA's Ethics Code _______________________.", "options": {"A": "permits this practice only when an animal is suffering", "B": "permits this practice only in the \"most unusual circumstances\"", "C": "permits this practice when it is appropriate and done in a way that minimizes pain", "D": "prohibits this practice"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Standard 8.09(g) of the Ethics Code states, \"when it is appropriate that an animal's life be terminated, psychologists proceed rapidly, with an\neffort to minimize pain and in accordance with accepted procedures.\"\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. The Ethics Code does not speci\u0000cally state this.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. There is no wording in the Ethics Code that speci\u0000es \"most unusual circumstances.\"\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The Ethics Code does not prohibit the practice."}, {"question": "During the second therapy session with a client, she admits that she has beaten her 7-year-old son on two occasions. She is very remorseful, promises never to do it again, and says she will continue coming to therapy to get the help she needs. You should ___________________________.", "options": {"A": "continue seeing the woman in therapy and contact the authorities only if you believe she is capable of hitting her son again", "B": "continue seeing the woman and make a report to the authorities only if she stops coming to therapy", "C": "explain that you are obligated to make a report and call the authorities while she is in your o\u0000ce", "D": "say nothing to the client about making a report but call the authorities as soon as possible after the therapy session ends"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "In this situation, you would be legally required to make a report to the appropriate authorities.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Psychologists are mandated reporters and are obliged to report whenever there is a suspicion of abuse.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. As a mandated reporter, the psychologist must act to protect the child.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. The experts generally agree that it is often preferable to involve the client in the reporting process (e.g., to\nmake the report when the client is present)."}, {"question": "Dr. Weiner, a psychologist, is conducting a research study and obtains subjects for his study by placing advertisements in a local newspaper. Dr. Weiner informs potential subjects about all aspects of the study and tells them that they should not agree to participate unless they are willing to stay for its duration (six sessions over a six-week period) since once they sign up, they will not be permitted to withdraw from the study. According to APA's Ethics Code, the proposed arrangement is ______________________________.", "options": {"A": "clearly ethical", "B": "ethical as long as the withdrawal restriction is included in the informed consents that all subjects must sign", "C": "ethical as long as the withdrawal restriction has been determined to be crucial to the study", "D": "unethical"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "This answer is in line with Standard 8.02 of the APA's Ethics Code. A researcher must respect the participants' right to withdraw at any time for\nany reason in the course of a study.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. A withdrawal restriction such as the one described in the question is unethical.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Under no conditions is such a withdrawal restriction ethical.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. Under no conditions is such a withdrawal restriction ethical."}, {"question": "Dr. Caldwell, a licensed psychologist, is working in a community mental health center. The parents of a 15-year old girl bring their daughter for counseling. The parents will, of course, be paying Dr. Caldwell's fee. As an ethical psychologist, Dr. Caldwell should __________________________.", "options": {"A": "caution the girl that, since she is a minor, anything she says may be told to her parents", "B": "advise the parents that he is required to maintain con\u0000dentiality and that he will not be able to advise them of anything the girl says during therapy sessions without her consent", "C": "clearly establish with the girl and her parents prior to therapy what the policy will be with regard to sharing information with the parents", "D": "use his discretion with regard to what information to reveal to the girl's parents and what information to keep con\u0000dential"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The therapeutic relationship would be best served if the policy regarding con\u0000dentiality is established with both the parents and the child at the\nonset of therapy. Therefore, this is the best course of action since it takes the welfare of the child as well as the legal rights of the parents into\nconsideration.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Although this may not violate ethical or legal guidelines, it is not the best available course of action.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Parents have a legal right to information about their child in most situations, but providing them with all\ninformation is not necessarily the best course of action from a clinical standpoint.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Using \"discretion\" is a vague answer that fails to account for the complexity of this situation. Establishing\npractices around con\u0000dentiality at the beginning of treatment is a better response."}, {"question": "Which of the following best describes the requirements of the ethics codes published by the American and Canadian Psychological Associations?", "options": {"A": "Subjects do not have to be debriefed about the nature of a research study following their participation unless doing so is required by the institutional review board.", "B": "Subjects do not have to be debriefed about the nature of a research study following their participation unless the study involved the use of deception.", "C": "Subjects must be debriefed about the nature of a research study following their participation in a way that minimizes harm.", "D": "Subjects must always be debriefed about the nature of a research study immediately following their participation."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The debrie\u0000ng of research subjects is addressed in Standards 8.07 and 8.08 of the APA's Ethics Code and Principles II and III of the Canadian\nCode of Ethics. Standard 8.08(c) of the APA's Ethics Code states that \"when psychologists become aware [during the debrie\u0000ng] that research\nprocedures have harmed a participant, they take reasonable steps to minimize the harm.\" Principle II.44 of the Canadian Code of Ethics states\nthat psychologists must \"debrief research subjects in such a way that any harm caused can be discerned, and act to correct any resultant\nharm.\" The Canadian Code of Ethics does not address the timing of debrie\u0000ng, and Standard 8.07(c) of the APA's Ethics Code requires\npsychologists to debrief subjects \"as early as is feasible\" when the study involved deception, which may be at the conclusion of their\nparticipation or at the end of data collection. In addition, Standard 8.08(b) allows psychologists to delay or withhold information that is\nnormally provided during debrie\u0000ng when there is a scienti\u0000c or humane justi\u0000cation for doing so.\nAnswer A: This answer does not re\u0000ect the position of either the APA's Ethics Code or the Canadian Code of Ethics.\nAnswer B: This answer is not consistent with the requirements of the American and Canadian codes.\nAnswer D: Neither code states that debrie\u0000ng must be provided to subjects immediately following their participation."}, {"question": "The reviewer for a managed health care organization decides that additional services for one of your clients are not medically necessary. You strongly disagree with her decision because you believe your client's condition is likely to worsen without additional therapy sessions. The client cannot afford to pay for therapy sessions himself. To act ethically and to minimize your legal liability in this situation, you should _____________________________.", "options": {"A": "carefully explain to the client the reason for termination and have him sign a waiver of responsibility", "B": "provide the client with the names of several therapists who you know provide services at a low fee", "C": "see the client at no fee for several sessions before terminating therapy", "D": "\u0000le an appeal with the managed health care organization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "An important aspect of managed health care is the ethical and legal dilemmas that it can create. In his discussion of \"Legal Liability and\nManaged Care,\" Appelbaum concludes that a psychologist's ethical and legal obligation in cases such as this one is to appeal the reviewer's\ndecision (\"American Psychologist,\" 48(3), pp.251-7, 1993).\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. This action would not be ethical and constitutes abandonment of the client.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Although a referral might be the action that is eventually taken, in most cases, it would not be the best \u0000rst\ncourse of action from either an ethical or legal perspective.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. If it becomes necessary to end the therapeutic relationship, providing the client with free sessions during\nthe termination period would be an ethical course of action. However, this is not the best answer of those given."}, {"question": "Following the death of his mother, Dr. Beasley, a clinical psychologist, \u0000nds he is having trouble sleeping, has lost his appetite, and is having some trouble concentrating when listening to his clients during therapy sessions. In this situation, to ful\u0000ll the requirements of the APA's Ethics Code, Dr. Beasley should ________________________.", "options": {"A": "begin therapy to work through his bereavement", "B": "temporarily suspend his practice until he feels better and refer his clients to another therapist", "C": "temporarily reduce his caseload to a more manageable level", "D": "seek consultation to determine if he should temporarily suspend his practice or take some other course of action"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "To be consistent with the provisions of the Ethics Code, Dr. Beasley should seek competent professional assistance to help him determine if he\nshould suspend, terminate, or otherwise modify his clinical practice because of his current personal problems.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. Seeking therapy is a possible course of action for Dr. Beasley, but this answer does not address the ethical\nissue of what Dr. Beasley should do to best protect the welfare of his clients.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. This course of action might also be acceptable, but it is not the best answer.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. While this might be an acceptable action, Dr. Beasley's \u0000rst course of action should be to seek advice to\nhelp him decide what to do."}, {"question": "A psychologist is hired by a defendant's attorney to evaluate the defendant for a possible insanity defense. Prior to conducting the evaluation, the psychologist__________________________.", "options": {"A": "should inform the defendant that all information provided during the evaluation is con\u0000dential and will be revealed only to his attorney", "B": "should inform the defendant that information will be provided only to his attorney unless the defendant and his attorney decide to use the results of the evaluation to support an insanity defense", "C": "should inform the defendant that information related to his mental state at the time he committed the crime is not con\u0000dential and will be provided to the court and the opposing attorney", "D": "should inform the defendant that any information he provides during the course of the evaluation will be revealed not only to his attorney but also to the court and the opposing attorney"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "When hired by a defendant's attorney to evaluate the defendant for a court case, the limits of con\u0000dentiality must be discussed prior to\nconducting the evaluation. In this situation, the client should be advised that information will be shared only with his attorney unless the client\nand his attorney decide to use it to support an insanity or similar defense. See, e.g., G. B. Melton et al., \"Psychological evaluation for the courts,\"\nNew York, Guilford Press, 1997.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect, as the attorney is likely to share the information with the court, contingent upon the psychologist's \u0000ndings.\nThis information should be shared with the defendant during informed consent.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. The psychologist is hired by the defendant's attorney rather than the court. The attorney and the defendant\ncan decide whether the information will be shared with the court.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. In this case, the psychologist is hired by the defendant's attorney. The attorney and defendant will\ndetermine whether the information will be shared."}, {"question": "Dr. Able, a psychologist, has been seeing a client for \u0000ve months and feels that the client may not be bene\u0000tting from treatment. When she mentions this to him, the client says he is quite satis\u0000ed with the progress of therapy. Based on this information, Dr. Able's best course of action would be to _____________________________.", "options": {"A": "continue seeing the client in therapy since the client is satis\u0000ed", "B": "tell the client that she is ethically required to refer him to another therapist", "C": "obtain supervision to ensure that her treatment is effective", "D": "consult with another psychologist to discuss the situation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Standard 10.10(a) states, \"Psychologists terminate therapy when it becomes reasonably clear that the client/patient no longer needs the\nservice, is not likely to bene\u0000t, or is being harmed by continuing service.\" Consultation is the best action since it would help the therapist\ndetermine if his evaluation of the situation is correct.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. When psychologists believe that a client is not bene\u0000tting from treatment, special consideration must be\ngiven to the situation.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Although referral might be acceptable, it is not necessary as the \u0000rst course of action in this case, given the\ncircumstances.\nAnswer C: This answer is incorrect. Supervision is not necessarily warranted in this situation."}, {"question": "To be consistent with ethical requirements, when a psychologist realizes that a dual (multiple) relationship is unavoidable, the psychologist should _________.", "options": {"A": "discuss the situation with the affected person \"as early as is feasible\"", "B": "consult with a member of the Ethics Committee to determine the best course of action", "C": "take reasonable steps to resolve the issue in a way that protects the best interests of the affected person", "D": "resolve the issue by terminating with the client and referring him or her to another therapist \"as early as is feasible\""}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "This issue is addressed by Standard 3.05(b) of the APA's Ethics Code. Standard 3.05(b) states that \"If a psychologist \u0000nds that, due to\nunforeseen factors, a potentially harmful multiple relationship has arisen, the psychologist takes reasonable steps to resolve it with due regard\nfor the best interests of the affected person and maximal compliance with the Ethics Code.\" This answer is most consistent with the actual\nlanguage of the Code and, therefore, is the correct response.\nAnswer A: The language in this answer does not align with the APA's Ethics Code.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. The guidance provided by the APA's Ethics Code is straightforward and does not include contacting the\nAPA Ethics Committee.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. Referral may be appropriate in some circumstances, but there are other strategies to resolve the con\u0000ict\nthat should be considered \u0000rst. See Standard 3.05(b) for speci\u0000c language."}, {"question": "Dr. Bernard is designing a longitudinal study on drug use in high schools. Since the study is longitudinal, it will be necessary to maintain records containing participants' identities. A colleague of Dr. Bernard's reminds him of a similar situation in which the investigator was subpoenaed to appear in court to testify against a participant who had been involved in illegal activities. The investigator refused to testify in order to maintain the participant's con\u0000dentiality and was imprisoned. What should Dr. Bernard do in order to avoid a similar dilemma?", "options": {"A": "If subpoenaed by the court, testify, since legal requirements override ethical considerations", "B": "If subpoenaed by the court, testify only after obtaining the consent of the participant", "C": "Warn participants prior to participation in the study about the possibility of a breach of con\u0000dentiality and obtain informed consents", "D": "Design the study so that the names of participants are maintained in a separate \u0000le by a third party"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "8.02(a) of APA's Ethics Code states that \"... psychologists inform participants about ... reasonably foreseeable factors that may be expected to\nin\u0000uence their willingness to participate such as potential risks, discomfort, or adverse effects ... limits of con\u0000dentiality.\" This answer is most\nin accord with the Standard, and it represents a balance between the psychologist's legal and ethical obligations. If he informs participants of\nthe limits of con\u0000dentiality, they will give their consent to participate with full awareness of the possibility that the psychologist or his \u0000les\nmight be subpoenaed.\nAnswer A: This answer is incorrect. While this answer correctly describes a psychologist's legal obligations in this situation, it is not the best\nanswer because it does not address how the psychologist can \"avoid this dilemma.\" After being subpoenaed by the court, a psychologist\nusually asserts privilege. If privilege is not recognized, the psychologist usually must testify in order to avoid being held in contempt of court. If\nthe psychologist testi\u0000es, he has ful\u0000lled his legal obligation without having dealt with his ethical obligation regarding con\u0000dentiality.\nAnswer B: This answer is incorrect. Legally, a psychologist who has been subpoenaed by the court must testify (if the court does not recognize\nprivilege) whether or not he has the consent of his client. Thus, this course of action would not resolve the dilemma between the psychologist's\nlegal and ethical obligations.\nAnswer D: This answer is incorrect. This course of action does not reduce the possibility that either the records or the psychologist will be\nsubpoenaed."}, {"question": "Maslow's theory of motivation predicts that needs are activated in a speci\u0000c order, with lower level needs being aroused \u0000rst. From lowest to highest level, Maslow's \u0000ve needs are:", "options": {"A": "safety, physiological, social, esteem, self-actualization", "B": "existence, social, growth, esteem, self-actualization", "C": "physiological, social, achievement, esteem, self-actualization", "D": "physiological, safety, social, esteem, self-actualization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Maslow's theory distinguishes between \u0000ve needs that emerge in a hierarchical fashion from lowest to highest. These are Maslow's \u0000ve needs,\nand they are arranged in the correct order from lowest to highest.\nAnswer A: Physiological is the \u0000rst need, and safety is the second need.\nAnswer B: Existence and growth are needs in Alderfer's ERG Theory.\nAnswer C: Social is the third need, and achievement is not a need in Maslow's theory."}, {"question": "To be consistent with the requirements of the Uniform Guidelines on Employee Procedures, when it is determined that a selection procedure is having an adverse impact, an employer must:", "options": {"A": "stop using the procedure for making selection decisions", "B": "use the procedure only in conjunction with other validated measures", "C": "obtain the consent of each applicant before using the procedure", "D": "validate that the procedure is job-related"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "When it is determined that a selection procedure is having an adverse impact, the employer cannot continue using it unless he/she can\ndemonstrate that doing so is a business necessity. An employer can show that continued use of the selection procedure is a business\nnecessity by demonstrating that (a) the measure is valid by establishing its relationship to successful job performance and (b) there are no\nalternative methods that would have a less adverse impact.\nAnswer A: You do not necessarily have to stop using the procedure unless you cannot prove that the procedure is not discriminatory.\nAnswer B: Use of the procedure in conjunction with other validated measures may not necessarily solve the adverse impact issue.\nAnswer C: Obtaining consent to use the procedure will most likely not affect a procedure that is having an adverse impact."}, {"question": "Which of the following theories of leadership provides a leader with a decision tree to help them decide whether an autocratic, consultative, or group decision-making style is optimal given the characteristics of the situation?", "options": {"A": "Hersey and Blanchard's situational leadership model", "B": "Graen's leader-member exchange model", "C": "House's path-goal theory", "D": "Vroom, Yetton, and Jago's normative model"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Although three of the theories listed in the responses propose that the best leader style depends on the characteristics of the situation, only\none includes a decision tree to help leaders pick the best style. The Vroom-Yetton-Jago model distinguishes between autocratic, consultative,\nand group decision-making leader styles and proposes that the best one depends on certain characteristics of the situation, including time\nconstraints, the importance of subordinate commitment to the decision, and the structure of the task. The model includes a decision tree to\nhelp leaders choose the optimal style for their situation.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"Hersey and Blanchard's situational leadership model\", is incorrect.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"Graen's leader-member exchange model\", is incorrect.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"House's path-goal theory\", is incorrect."}, {"question": "A new selection test is apparently having an adverse impact on females. To determine if the adverse impact is being caused by \"unfairness,\" you would check:", "options": {"A": "the predictor scores obtained by males and females", "B": "the slopes of the regression lines for males and females", "C": "to see if the language used in the test is biased", "D": "to see if the measure of job performance is biased"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "As described in the Industrial-Organizational Psychology chapter of the written study materials, adverse impact can be the result of several\nfactors, including differential validity and unfairness. As de\u0000ned by the EEOC, unfairness occurs when the validity coe\u0000cients and criterion\nperformance for two groups are similar, but members of the groups perform differently on the predictor.\nAnswer B: The slopes of the regression lines would be useful for determining if adverse impact is due to differential validity.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"to see if the language used in the test is biased,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"to see if the measure of job performance is biased,\" is incorrect."}, {"question": "Compared to decentralized communication networks, centralized networks:", "options": {"A": "are associated with fewer errors and greater productivity when the task is simple", "B": "are associated with fewer errors and greater productivity when the task is complex", "C": "are associated with fewer errors and greater productivity regardless of whether the task is simple or complex", "D": "are associated with more errors and poorer productivity regardless of whether the task is simple or complex"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Communication networks are often described as centralized or decentralized. The most effective network depends on several factors,\nincluding the characteristics of the task. As its name implies, a centralized network requires all communication to pass through a central\nperson. It is most effective for simple tasks but is less e\u0000cient when the task is complex.\nAnswer B: A decentralized network is more e\u0000cient when the task is complex.\nAnswer C: There is a difference between simple and complex tasks, and centralized networks are associated with fewer errors and greater\nproductivity when the task is simple.\nAnswer D: Centralized networks are not associated with more errors and poorer productivity regardless of whether the task is simple or\ncomplex."}, {"question": "An advantage of the \"paired comparison\" technique is that it:", "options": {"A": "helps reduce certain rater/observer biases", "B": "provides helpful information for employee feedback", "C": "is less time-consuming to complete than other techniques", "D": "is more acceptable to employees and supervisors than other methods"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The paired comparison technique is a relative method for assessing performance that entails comparing an employee to other employees on\nprede\u0000ned dimensions of job performance. One advantage of relative measures is that they help reduce rater biases.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"provides helpful information for employee feedback,\" is the opposite of what is true.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"is less time-consuming to complete than other techniques,\" is the opposite of what is true.\nAnswer D: Employees tend to dislike relative measures because they do not want to be compared to other employees."}, {"question": "Research has shown that membership in a cohesive (versus noncohesive) group is associated with:", "options": {"A": "greater productivity, greater satisfaction, and reduced turnover", "B": "greater satisfaction, reduced turnover and, in some situations, greater productivity", "C": "greater productivity but no signi\u0000cant impact on satisfaction or turnover", "D": "in the long run, reduced productivity, lower satisfaction, and increased turnover"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Group cohesiveness has been linked to a number of bene\u0000ts and liabilities. Group cohesiveness has been consistently linked to reduced\nabsenteeism and turnover and higher levels of satisfaction and commitment by the research, but its association with productivity is moderated\nby a number of factors (e.g., the group's norms regarding productivity).\nAnswer A: The answer, \"greater productivity, greater satisfaction, and reduced turnover,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"greater productivity but no signi\u0000cant impact on satisfaction or turnover,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"in the long run, reduced productivity, lower satisfaction, and increased turnover,\" is incorrect."}, {"question": "Bazerman, Tenbrunsel, and Wade-Benzoni's (1998) distinction between the \"should self\" and the \"want self\" is most useful for understanding which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Ethical decision-making", "B": "A\u0000liation preferences", "C": "Employee satisfaction", "D": "Adolescent egocentrism"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Bazerman et al. distinguish between two competing selves - the \"should self,\" which is rational and cool-headed, and the \"want self,\" which is\nemotional and hot-headed. [Bazerman, M. H., Tenbrunsel, A. E., & K. A. Wade-Benzoni, Negotiating with yourself and losing: Understanding and\nmanaging con\u0000icting internal preferences, Academy of Management Review, 23,225-241, 1998.]. The distinction between the \"should self\" and\n\"want self\" has been applied to ethical, business, and other types of decision-making. With regard to ethical decision-making, the should self\nrefers to our ethical intentions and the belief that we should act in ways that are consistent with our ethical principles. In contrast, the want self\napplies to our actual behavior and re\u0000ects self-interest and a relative disregard for ethical concerns.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"a\u0000liation preferences\" is not associated with this theory's distinction between the \"should self\" and the \"want self.\"\nAnswer C: The answer, \"employee satisfaction\" is not associated with this theory's distinction between the \"should self\" and the \"want self.\"\nAnswer D: The answer, \"adolescent egocentrism\" is not associated with this theory's distinction between the \"should self\" and the \"want self.\""}, {"question": "Tiedeman and O'Hara's (1963) theory of career development was most in\u0000uenced by the work of:", "options": {"A": "Erikson", "B": "Bandura", "C": "Baumrind", "D": "Maslow"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Knowing that Tiedeman and O'Hara emphasize the role of the ego in career development would have helped you identify the correct answer to\nthis question. Tiedeman and O'Hara's theory is classi\u0000ed as a cognitive-developmental theory that is consistent with the work of Erikson,\nBruner, Piaget, and Allport. It views career development as being an aspect of ego identity development and as a process that continues\nthroughout the lifespan.\nAnswer B: Bandura focused more on social learning theory.\nAnswer C: Baumrind focused more on research on parenting styles.\nAnswer D: Maslow focused more on motivational theory as a hierarchy of needs."}, {"question": "Chin and Benne (1976) propose that which of the following techniques would be part of a normative-reeducative strategy for overcoming resistance to change in an organization?", "options": {"A": "Providing information to employees about the need for change", "B": "Having groups of employees discuss ways to accomplish change", "C": "Having a strong leader describe ways to achieve change", "D": "Using rewards to encourage employees to comply with plans for change"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Chin and Benne (1976) identi\u0000ed three strategies for overcoming resistance to change in organizations: rational-empirical, normative-\nreeducative, and power-coercive. The use of the normative-reeducative strategy is based on the premise that peer pressure and sociocultural\nnorms are effective methods for promoting change. Therefore, having employees work together to accomplish change is likely to be part of\nthis strategy.\nAnswer A: Providing employees with information about the change when resistance to change is due to a lack of knowledge is characteristic of\nthe rational-empirical strategy.\nAnswer C: Relying on a strong leader to gain cooperation is characteristic of the power-coercive strategy.\nAnswer D: Using rewards is also characteristic of the power-coercive strategy."}, {"question": "Taylor's (1911) scienti\u0000c management is based on the premise that which of the following is most useful for maximizing employee motivation?", "options": {"A": "Opportunities for advancement", "B": "Opportunities to satisfy prepotent needs", "C": "Satisfying relationships with co-workers", "D": "Pay that is directly linked to performance"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "As its name implies, scienti\u0000c management involves applying the principles of science to the \u0000eld of management in order to determine the\nmost effective way for workers to perform their jobs. A premise of scienti\u0000c management is that workers are motivated primarily by economic\nself-interest, and Taylor proposed that productivity is maximized when pay is clearly linked to performance.\nAnswer A: Taylor's scienti\u0000c management did not \u0000nd that opportunities for advancement were directly linked to performance.\nAnswer B: This idea comes from the work done by Malow.\nAnswer C: This sounds more like McClelland's need for a\u0000liation."}, {"question": "Level of organizational commitment is most predictive of which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Turnover", "B": "Job satisfaction", "C": "Quantity and quality of work", "D": "Life satisfaction"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Organizational commitment refers to the strength of a person's identi\u0000cation with and involvement in a particular organization. Like job\nsatisfaction, commitment appears to have the highest correlation with turnover. It is also related to the individual's willingness to exert effort on\nbehalf of the company.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"job satisfaction,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"quantity and quality of work,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"life satisfaction,\" is incorrect."}, {"question": "In contrast to Maslow's need hierarchy theory, Alderfer's ERG theory:", "options": {"A": "places motivation within a satisfaction/dissatisfaction framework", "B": "proposes that needs emerge in a predictable order", "C": "assumes that needs may become more (rather than less) important as they are ful\u0000lled", "D": "has received little support as an explanation for motivation in the workplace"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "ERG theory is a modi\u0000cation of Maslow's need hierarchy theory. Among other things, it reduces Maslow's \u0000ve needs to three: existence,\nrelatedness, and growth. The fact that needs may become more (rather than less) important as they are ful\u0000lled is an assumption of ERG\ntheory and the opposite of what Maslow's theory predicts.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"places motivation within a satisfaction/dissatisfaction framework,\" sounds like Herzberg's two-factor theory.\nAnswer B: The answer, \"proposes that needs emerge in a predictable order,\" is a characteristic of Maslow's (not Alderfer's) theory.\nAnswer D: Alderfer's theory has actually received more support than Maslow's."}, {"question": "In organizations, __________ involves training workers to perform different tasks and activities.", "options": {"A": "vestibule training", "B": "cross-training", "C": "over-training", "D": "frame-of-reference training"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with the off-the-job and on-the-job training techniques that are described in the Industrial-Organizational\nPsychology chapter of the written study materials so that you can answer questions like this one. As its name suggests, cross-training involves\ntraining an employee to perform other tasks (usually tasks related to his/her current job). Cross-training bene\u0000ts the employer by increasing the\n\u0000exibility of employees and the employees by increasing their interest and motivation.\nAnswer A: Vestibule training is training done in a simulated environment when on-the-job training would be too costly or dangerous.\nAnswer C: Over-training is when an employee trains to such an extent that the employee becomes stale and less productive.\nAnswer D: Frame-of-reference training is training to improve the accuracy of evaluators' performance ratings of employees."}, {"question": "The purpose of a __________ is to guide the development of a program so that its \u0000nal version maximizes the likelihood that the program will achieve its goals.", "options": {"A": "summative evaluation", "B": "formative evaluation", "C": "needs assessment", "D": "functional assessment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "For the exam, you want to be familiar with the difference between formative and summative evaluations so that you can answer questions like\nthis one. Formative evaluations are conducted while a training program is being developed (\"formed\") to obtain the information needed to\ndetermine what modi\u0000cations are required.\nAnswer A: A summative evaluation is conducted after a training program has been developed and administered to trainees in order to assess\nthe program's outcomes.\nAnswer C: A needs assessment is conducted to determine if performance problems can be alleviated by providing training and if so, to identify\nthe content of a training program.\nAnswer D: A functional assessment (also known as a functional behavioral assessment) is conducted to identify the functions that behavior\nserves in order to identify ways of modifying that behavior."}, {"question": "Which of the following would be most effective for reducing a rater's leniency bias?", "options": {"A": "BARS", "B": "Peer ratings", "C": "Forced-choice technique", "D": "BIB"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "A leniency bias occurs when a rater rates all ratees very positively regardless of their actual level of performance. Likert-type scales are most\nsusceptible to leniency and other rater biases. A forced-choice scale presents items that have been identi\u0000ed as being of equal social\ndesirability in dyads or triads and requires the rater to pick the item that best describes the ratee. Because of its format, it eliminates the\nleniency bias.\nAnswer A: BARS is a type of rating scale. Although it was developed to help increase rater accuracy, the research has not been particularly\nfavorable.\nAnswer B: Peer ratings would not overcome the problem of leniency.\nAnswer D: BIB - biographical information blank - is not a rating scale."}, {"question": "Which of the following work shifts is likely to be associated with the least amount of job-related stress?", "options": {"A": "Permanent day shift", "B": "Permanent night shift", "C": "Rotating shifts", "D": "Compressed workweek with rotating shifts"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Unfortunately, the research on work schedules is very inconsistent with different studies comparing different types of schedules and producing\ndifferent results with regard to the outcomes associated with \u0000extime, compressed workweek, rotating shifts, etc. In one study, workers who\nwere switched from rotating shifts to permanent day shifts showed signi\u0000cant increases in several measures of well-being [T. Akerstedt and L.\nTorsvall, Experimental changes in shift schedules: The effect on well-being, Ergonomics, 21(10), 849-856, 1978]. In a more recent study,\nworkers on \u0000xed and rotating shifts were compared, and those on the \u0000xed shift reported less stress and better work attitudes [M. Jamal and V.\nBaba, Shiftwork and department-type related to job stress, work attitudes, and behavioral intentions: A study of nurses, Journal of\nOrganizational Behavior, 13(5), 449-464, 1992].\nAnswer B: Studies comparing day and night shifts have usually found the day shift to be associated with better outcomes. Apparently, this is\nbecause, for most people, the night shift is inconsistent with the normal sleep-wake cycle.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"rotating shifts,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"compressed work week with rotating shifts,\" is incorrect."}, {"question": "The belief that job-related behaviors are related to the similarity between basic personality and characteristics of the work environment underlies which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Roe's \u0000elds and levels theory", "B": "Holland's occupational themes", "C": "Alderfer's ERG theory", "D": "Super's stages of vocational development"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Holland's six occupational themes represent basic personality types. Holland proposed that job satisfaction, performance, and so on are\ndirectly related to the degree of similarity between the person's basic personality and the nature of the environment. A conventional person, for\ninstance, is happiest in a conventional environment.\nAnswer A: Roe's theory focuses on the relationship between childhood experience and occupational choice.\nAnswer C: ERG theory is a theory of motivation that posits three basic needs (existence, relatedness, and growth).\nAnswer D: Super's theory emphasizes the role of self-concept in vocational development and choice."}, {"question": "According to Herzberg's \"two-factor theory:\"", "options": {"A": "job motivation increases as job responsibility and autonomy increase", "B": "job performance is determined by the interaction of two factors -- job content and job context factors", "C": "job motivation is a direct result of job outcomes", "D": "the factors that increase an employee's motivation depend on the employee's level of growth need"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Herzberg's two-factor theory is a theory of both satisfaction and motivation. As its name implies, it proposes that satisfaction and motivation\nare affected by two different types of job-related factors. According to Herzberg, job-related factors can be divided into two types: hygiene (job\ncontext) factors and motivator (job content) factors. Only the second can affect satisfaction and motivation; i.e., providing workers with jobs\nthat are high in responsibility, challenge, and autonomy will result in satis\u0000ed and motivated workers. (The presence of hygiene factors only\nensures that workers are not dissatis\u0000ed).\nAnswer B: The answer, \"job performance is determined by the interaction of two factors -- job content and job context factors,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer C: Although the answer, \"job motivation is a direct result of job outcomes,\" is not wrong, it is too general and, therefore, not the best\nanswer of those given.\nAnswer D: Although the answer, \"the factors that increase an employee's motivation depend on the employee's level of growth need,\" is true, it\nis not part of Herzberg's theory."}, {"question": "When using the multiple hurdles approach in personnel selection, a critical decision is:", "options": {"A": "the order in which the predictors will be administered", "B": "how each predictor should be weighted", "C": "whether or not the person administering the criterion should also evaluate predictor performance", "D": "which statistical test to use to determine if results are statistically signi\u0000cant"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "As its name implies, the multiple hurdles technique involves administering the predictors one at a time. To maximize the usefulness of the\nmultiple hurdles technique, predictors must be administered in a logical order (e.g., from least to most di\u0000cult or least to most expensive).\nAnswer B: The answer, \"how each predictor should be weighted,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"whether or not the person administering the criterion should also evaluate predictor performance,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"which statistical test to use to determine if results are statistically signi\u0000cant,\" is incorrect."}, {"question": "In the 1950s, researchers at Ohio State University developed an in\u0000uential theory of leadership that focused on a leader's:", "options": {"A": "personality traits", "B": "behaviors", "C": "attitudes toward subordinates", "D": "power"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Results of the Ohio State leadership studies indicated that leaders can be described in terms of two behavioral dimensions - consideration and\ninitiating structure. The Ohio State group focused on the behaviors exhibited by supervisors and other organizational leaders.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"personality traits,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"attitudes toward subordinates,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"power,\" is incorrect."}, {"question": "When using a ___________________, the rater is provided with a list of behavioral examples and asked to indicate whether the ratee's performance is equal to, worse than, or better than the performance described in each example.", "options": {"A": "critical incident technique", "B": "behavioral observation scale", "C": "forced-choice method", "D": "mixed standard scale"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The mixed standard scale (Blanz and Ghiselli, 1971) was originally designed to reduce rater biases. Note that if you're unfamiliar with this\ntechnique, you may have been able to identify it as the correct answer to this question using the process of elimination. When using the mixed\nstandard scale, three critical incidents representing good, average, and poor performance for several dimensions of job performance are\nidenti\u0000ed. They are then listed in the rating scale in a random order, and the rater indicates, for each incident, whether the rater's performance is\nequal to, worse than, or better than the described behavior.\nAnswer A: Although the mixed standard scale uses critical incidents, it is not the same as the \"critical incident technique,\" which involves\nrecording speci\u0000c behaviors that are associated with outstanding or poor performance and then marking those behaviors that apply to the\nratee.\nAnswer B: In the \"behavioral observation scale,\" the rater uses one or more scales to measure how many times an employee performed the\nbehavior in question.\nAnswer C: In the \"forced-choice method,\" the rater selects the alternative that best or least describes the ratee."}, {"question": "Roe and Holland share in common an emphasis on the role of ___________ in career choice.", "options": {"A": "aptitude", "B": "personality", "C": "social pressure", "D": "vocational interests"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Familiarity with Roe and/or Holland would have helped you choose the correct response to this question. Their approaches to career\ndevelopment and choice are described in the Industrial-Organizational Psychology chapter of the written study materials. Holland proposes\nthat the best career choice is the career that provides a match between the individual's personality characteristics (e.g., conventional, artistic)\nand the characteristics of the work environment. Roe links career choice to personality and basic needs.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"aptitude,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"social pressure,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"vocational interests,\" is incorrect."}, {"question": "One prediction of goal-setting theory is that di\u0000cult goals result in greater productivity than easy or ambiguous goals. With regard to task di\u0000culty, the studies have shown that:", "options": {"A": "the more complex the task, the stronger the link between goal di\u0000culty and productivity", "B": "the less complex the task, the stronger the link between goal di\u0000culty and productivity", "C": "the relationship between task complexity and productivity is curvilinear, with the strongest link being between moderate goal complexity and productivity", "D": "the relationship between task complexity and productivity is curvilinear, with the strongest links being between low and high goal complexity and productivity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Even if you're not familiar with the research, it makes sense that goal-setting theory would apply better to simple tasks than di\u0000cult tasks since\nperformance on the latter would also depend on other factors in addition to the acceptance of di\u0000cult goals. This is the conclusion reached by\nthe developers of goal-setting theory. See R. F. Wood, E. A. Locke, and A. J. Mento, Task complexity as a moderator of goal effects: A meta-\nanalysis, Journal of Applied Psychology, 72, 416-425, 1987.\nAnswer A: The answer, \"the more complex the task, the stronger the link between goal di\u0000culty and productivity,\" is the opposite of what is true.\nAnswer C: The answer, \"the relationship between task complexity and productivity is curvilinear with the strongest link being between moderate\ngoal complexity and productivity,\" is incorrect.\nAnswer D: The answer, \"the relationship between task complexity and productivity is curvilinear with the strongest links being between low and\nhigh goal complexity and productivity,\" is incorrect."}, {"question": "As a researcher, you have conditioned a rat to respond to a whistle as a signal of feeding. You then add a \u0000ashing light to the whistle to signal food. After a training session, the light is only needed to signal feeding time. Which of the following did you demonstrate?", "options": {"A": "Preparatory-response theory", "B": "Stimulus-substitution theory", "C": "Compensatory-response theory", "D": "Rescola-Wagner theory"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Stimulus-substitution theory posits that after pairing a new stimulus (light) with an existing conditioned stimulus (whistle), it is possible to\nreplace the conditioned stimulus (whistle) with the new stimulus (light) and elicit the same response.\nAnswer A: Preparatory-response theory proposes that the purpose of the conditioned response is to prepare for the presentation of the\nunconditioned stimulus.\nAnswer C: Compensatory-response theory is a version of the preparatory-response theory and is based on the opponent-process theory of\nemotion and motivation.\nAnswer D: Rescola-Wagner's theory posits that conditioning proceeds from pairing to pairing as a \u0000xed proportion of the maximum amount of\nconditioning that can be achieved with the unconditioned stimulus."}, {"question": "Which of the following is a key process in development and learning according to Vygotsky's sociocultural theory?", "options": {"A": "Assimilation", "B": "Adaptation", "C": "Culture-speci\u0000c tools", "D": "Accommodation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Culture-speci\u0000c tools are items in the culture that teach children about the expectations of the group. These tools help the child form their view\nof the world. Examples include books, computers, and traditions.\nAnswer A: Assimilation was de\u0000ned by Piaget as the cognitive process of \u0000tting new information into existing cognitive schemas, perceptions,\nand understanding.\nAnswer B: Adaptation is a process that plays an important role in Piaget's theory of cognitive development. It refers to an ability to adjust to\nchanges and new experiences and to accept new information.\nAnswer D: Accommodation was de\u0000ned by Piaget as the cognitive process of revising existing cognitive schemas, perceptions, and\nunderstanding so that new information can be incorporated."}, {"question": "________ is a response bias arising from discrimination learning in which directional, but limited, preference for or avoidance of unusual stimuli is displayed.", "options": {"A": "generalization gradient", "B": "peak shift", "C": "excitatory gradient", "D": "inhibitory gradient"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Peak shift occurs after discrimination training involving two stimuli along a common dimension (e.g., brightness). The peak of the response\ngradient is shifted in a direction away from the less favorable stimulus to a point beyond the value of the stimulus associated with\nreinforcement.\nAnswer A: Generalization gradient graphs the similarity or difference between two stimuli versus the similarity or difference in their elicited\nresponses.\nAnswer C: Excitatory gradient graphs an increased tendency to respond to a stimulus and to other stimuli that resemble it.\nAnswer D: Inhibitory gradient graphs a decreased tendency."}, {"question": "A father puts his son in his room every time the boy hits his sister. To increase the probability that the boy will stop hitting his sister, the father should:", "options": {"A": "give the child 50 cents each time he is nice to his sister", "B": "initially send the boy to his room every time he hits his sister and then send him to his room every three times he hits his sister", "C": "gradually increase the length of time the boy spends in his room after each time he hits his sister", "D": "sometimes send the boy to his room for 10 minutes and other times send him to his room for 20 minutes or 30 minutes after he hits his sister"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The parent is using negative punishment in order to decrease the probability that the behavior will occur again. Negative punishment is the\nremoval of a stimulus following a behavior in order to decrease that behavior. In this example, the father is utilizing time-out, a type of negative\npunishment. Punishment, like all forms of behavior therapy used to reduce undesirable behaviors, is most effective when used in conjunction\nwith reinforcement for alternate desirable behaviors. In this case, the 50 cents would represent reinforcement (a stimulus that increases the\nprobability of a behavior) for the alternate desirable behavior of being nice.\nAnswer B: Punishment is more effective when it is applied consistently (after every performance of a behavior) than when it is applied\nintermittently.\nAnswer C: A gradual increase in the intensity of punishment can produce habituation. In other words, if the child has to spend more and more\ntime in his room, he may get used to it and won't mind it as much.\nAnswer D: This strategy will do nothing to decrease the occurrence of the behavior and may produce habituation."}, {"question": "A university freshman would be best advised to rely primarily on which of the following when studying for his Psychology 101 \u0000nal exam?", "options": {"A": "Maintenance rehearsal", "B": "Stimulus coding", "C": "Imagery", "D": "Elaborative rehearsal"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "In this situation, the student will have to memorize and understand fairly complex information. Elaborative rehearsal involves actively analyzing\ninformation and relating it to previously stored data. It is the best way to encode information into long-term memory.\nAnswer A: Maintenance rehearsal involves simply repeating information with little or no processing and is less effective than elaborative\nrehearsal.\nAnswer B: Stimulus coding refers to connecting an image with a part of a word (e.g., the \u0000rst syllable). It is especially good for paired-associate\ntasks such as foreign language learning (matching an English word to its foreign-language equivalent).\nAnswer C: Imagery techniques, such as the method of loci and the keyword method, may be useful as memory devices. For a large amount of\ncomplex material, elaboration is better."}, {"question": "Escape and avoidance conditioning involve which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Positive reinforcement", "B": "Negative reinforcement", "C": "Positive punishment", "D": "Negative punishment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Escape and avoidance conditioning both establish a response that allows the individual to elude an aversive stimulus utilizing negative\nreinforcement. In escape and avoidance conditioning, the individual's behavior increases because it enables him/her to escape or avoid a\nstimulus (i.e., a stimulus is taken away following the response). Escape conditioning is simply a straightforward application of negative\nreinforcement, while avoidance conditioning is more complex and involves a combination of negative reinforcement and classical conditioning.\nAnswer A: Positive reinforcement occurs when a behavior increases because of the stimulus that is applied (or added) following the behavior.\nEscape and avoidance conditioning both utilize negative, not positive, reinforcement.\nAnswer C: Positive punishment occurs when a behavior decreases because of the application of a stimulus following the behavior and is not a\ncomponent of escape or avoidance conditioning.\nAnswer D: Negative punishment occurs when a behavior decreases because of the removal or cessation of a stimulus following the behavior\nand is not a component of escape or avoidance conditioning."}, {"question": "If a bell begins to ring and continues to ring until food is presented it is an example of which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Positive transfer", "B": "Delayed conditioning", "C": "Narrowing", "D": "Negative contrast"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Delayed conditioning is a procedure in which the conditioned stimulus is presented and remains present for a \u0000xed period (delay) before the\nunconditioned stimulus is introduced.\nAnswer A: Positive transfer occurs in motor skills learning when the practice of one task improves learning or performance on a similar task.\nAnswer C: Narrowing is a technique of stimulus control that involves gradually reducing the range of situations in which an unwanted behavior\nis allowed to occur.\nAnswer D: Negative contrast is a type of behavioral contrast in which there is a decrease in responding in the presence of one stimulus due to\nan increase in the reinforcement conditions for another stimulus."}, {"question": "Which of the following proposes that an individual chooses where their attention is focused based on the physical qualities of the stimulus in addition to preventing overstimulation of the senses?", "options": {"A": "Trace decay theory", "B": "Multi-component model", "C": "Feature-integration theory", "D": "Filter theory of selective attention"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Filter theory of selective attention posits that two sensory stimuli presented at the same time are maintained for a brief period in the sensory\nregister where a \u0000lter selects one of the stimuli to pass through a limited sensory channel based on its physical characteristics while the other\nstimulus is held in a temporary buffer for later processing, and the stimulus that passes through the channel to short-term memory is\nprocessed for meaning and comes into conscious awareness.\nAnswer A: Trace decay theory explains that if memories are not reviewed or recalled consistently, they will begin to decay and will ultimately be\nforgotten.\nAnswer B: The multi-component model separates working memory functionally from long-term memory to account for functionality that goes\nbeyond the mere activation of long-term traces.\nAnswer C: Feature-integration theory is a two-stage theory of attention that suggests once an object has been correctly integrated, it ordinarily\ncontinues to be perceived and stored in memory as a singular object."}, {"question": "Elaborative rehearsal is most useful for:", "options": {"A": "encoding information in sensory memory", "B": "holding information in working memory for an extended period of time", "C": "transferring information from sensory memory to short-term memory", "D": "transferring information from short-term memory to long-term memory"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Elaborative rehearsal refers to making new information more meaningful by associating it with previously acquired information. The research\nhas con\u0000rmed that elaborative rehearsal is an effective way for ensuring that material is transferred from short-term memory to long-term\nmemory.\nAnswer A: Elaborative rehearsal is a method for improving long-term memory and not for encoding information in sensory memory, which is\nresponsible for the temporary storage of incoming sensory signals.\nAnswer B: Rote repetition (e.g., repeating a phone number) is useful for maintaining information in working memory.\nAnswer C: Sensory input is transferred from sensory memory to short-term memory by focusing attention on that input."}, {"question": "Various techniques help individuals overcome di\u0000culties in performance and increase memory strength. Mnemonic techniques and spaced repetition are two ways Ebbinghaus proposed to overcome which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Yerkes-Dodson Law", "B": "The Forgetting Curve", "C": "Positive contrast effect", "D": "Misinformation effect"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "According to Ebbinghaus, the Forgetting Curve describes that individuals start losing their memory of learned knowledge over time, in a matter\nof days or weeks, unless the learned knowledge is consciously reviewed repeatedly.\nAnswer A: The Yerkes-Dodson Law suggests that there is a relationship between performance and arousal. Moreover, performance increases\nwith physiological or mental arousal but only to a point. When the level of arousal or stress becomes too high, performance decreases.\nAnswer C: The positive contrast effect occurs when a shift from low to high reward magnitude produces a greater level of responding than if\nthe reward magnitude had always been high.\nAnswer D: The misinformation effect refers to information received from others about an event that can alter memory to conform to the\ninformation received from others."}, {"question": "Which one of the following components of the working memory model integrates auditory, visual, and spatial information and provides a means to allow multiple sources of information to be considered simultaneously?", "options": {"A": "Episodic buffer", "B": "Phonological loop", "C": "Visuospatial sketchpad", "D": "Central executive"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The episodic buffer is a temporary multimodal store that combines information from the phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad\nsubsystems of working memory with information about time to form and maintain an integrated, detailed representation of a given stimulus or\nevent that can then be deposited into long-term memory as necessary.\nAnswer B: Phonological loop is a component of the working memory model that deals with spoken and written material.\nAnswer C: Visuospatial sketchpad is a component of working memory responsible for handling visual and spatial information.\nAnswer D: The central executive is a component that manages the activities of the phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad. It is, however,\nof limited capacity and does not have any storage ability of its own."}, {"question": "The ________ is an indirect measure of sympathetic autonomic activity that is associated with both emotion and attention.", "options": {"A": "taste-aversion learning", "B": "conditioned suppression", "C": "eyeblink conditioning", "D": "skin-conductance response"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Skin-conductance response is an indirect measure of sympathetic autonomic activity that is associated with both emotion and attention. The\namplitude of skin-conductance responses is related to the level of arousal elicited by visual stimuli with either positive or negative emotional\nvalence.\nAnswer A: Taste-aversion learning refers to a learned pattern of aversion to a speci\u0000c food. Speci\u0000cally, for animals, learned food aversion has\nbeen favored by natural selection and helps them avoid poisonous foods.\nAnswer B: Conditioned suppression occurs when a conditioned response to a positive stimulus is reduced by another stimulus that is\nassociated with an aversive stimulus.\nAnswer C: Eyeblink conditioning is a paradigm commonly used to investigate the neural mechanisms underlying motor learning. It involves the\npaired presentation of a tone-conditioning stimulus that precedes and co-terminates with an air puff unconditioned stimulus."}, {"question": "Which of the following is associated with the sensation and feeling when someone smiles after seeing someone else smile?", "options": {"A": "Mirror neurons", "B": "Self-modeling", "C": "Imitation", "D": "Observational learning"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Mirror neurons are a type of brain cell that responds equally when an individual performs an action and when an individual witnesses someone\nelse perform the same action.\nAnswer B: Self-modeling refers to the behavioral change that results from the repeated observation of oneself on videotapes that show only\ndesired target behaviors.\nAnswer C: Imitation refers to the process of copying the behavior of another person, group, or object, intentionally or unintentionally.\nAnswer D: Observational learning describes the process of learning through watching others, retaining the information, and then later\nreplicating the behaviors that were observed."}, {"question": "Creating an acronym involves:", "options": {"A": "forming a word or pronounceable sequence of letters from the \u0000rst letter of the words included in a list of words", "B": "creating a sentence or rhyme using the \u0000rst letter of the words included in a list of words", "C": "creating an image that combines the images of two or more words", "D": "chunking items together to make a smaller number of items"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "This is the method used to form an acronym. For example, students sometimes use the acronym \"PEMDAS\" to remember the order of\noperations in mathematics - i.e., parentheses, exponents, multiplication, division, addition, and subtraction. For the exam, you want to be\nfamiliar with acronyms and other mnemonic devices that are described in the Learning Theory chapter of the written study materials.\nAnswer B: This is the procedure for creating an acrostic. \"Please excuse my dear Aunt Sally\" is an acrostic for the order of operations in\nmathematics.\nAnswer C: This describes the keyword method.\nAnswer D: Chunking is useful for maintaining information in short-term memory."}, {"question": "A potential problem with the Premack Principle is that:", "options": {"A": "it is often di\u0000cult to identify an alternative behavior that serves the same function as the target behavior", "B": "it may not be possible to apply the reinforcer immediately following the target behavior", "C": "for some people, secondary reinforcers have little value", "D": "it is di\u0000cult to extinguish a behavior without simultaneously reinforcing an alternative one"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The Premack Principle entails reinforcing a low-frequency behavior with a high-frequency behavior or activity. The high-frequency behavior or\nactivity may not be immediately available, which can reduce the effectiveness of the intervention since positive reinforcement is most effective\nwhen the reinforcer can be delivered immediately following the target behavior.\nAnswer A: The Premack Principle does not rely on the low-frequency and high-frequency behaviors having similar functions.\nAnswer C: Although this may be true, this is not a potential problem with the Premack Principle.\nAnswer D: The Premack Principle is an application of positive reinforcement, not extinction. Differential reinforcement combines positive\nreinforcement with extinction."}, {"question": "Behavioral therapy where individuals are reinforced or rewarded to assist in positive behavioral change is an integral part of which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Cognitive therapy", "B": "Implosive therapy", "C": "Contingency management", "D": "Negative practice"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Contingency management is a behavioral therapy that uses motivational incentives and tangible rewards to help an individual make positive\nbehavioral changes.\nAnswer A: Cognitive therapy focuses on present thinking, behavior, and communication rather than on past experiences and is oriented toward\nproblem-solving.\nAnswer B: Implosive therapy is used to extinguish a conditioned response without deliberately substituting an alternative response.\nAnswer D: Negative practice involves the repeated performance of a behavior to eliminate or reduce the occurrence of that behavior."}, {"question": "Bandura's social cognitive theory describes the in\u0000uence of individual experiences, the actions of others, and environmental factors on individual health behaviors. Which of the following is considered a key component of this theory related to behavior change?", "options": {"A": "Cognitive load", "B": "Equilibration", "C": "Scaffolding", "D": "Self-e\u0000cacy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Self-e\u0000cacy is a key component of social cognitive theory and refers to an individual's belief in his or her ability to execute behaviors necessary\nto produce speci\u0000c performance attainments.\nAnswer A: Cognitive load relates to the amount of information that working memory can hold at one time. Sweller (1988) proposed that since\nworking memory has a limited capacity, instructional methods should avoid overloading it with additional activities that do not directly\ncontribute to learning.\nAnswer B: Equilibration is a concept developed by Piaget that describes the cognitive balancing of new information with old knowledge.\nAnswer C: Vygotsky outlined scaffolding as a tool for growth. Speci\u0000cally, it is an instructional technique where individualized support is\nprovided by incrementally improving an individual's ability to build on prior knowledge."}, {"question": "Although new neurons develop throughout an individual's life, our brains reach maximum size during the early twenties and then begin to decline in volume and blood \u0000ow slowly. Noting that there are differences among individuals, some cognitive abilities continue to improve well into older age. Speci\u0000cally, regarding memory which of the following continues to improve for many older adults?", "options": {"A": "Procedural memory", "B": "Semantic memory", "C": "Episodic memory", "D": "Long-term memory"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Semantic memory is the ability to recall concepts and general facts that are not related to speci\u0000c experiences. This type of memory also\nincludes vocabulary and knowledge of language and tends to improve with age.\nAnswer A: Procedural memory is remembering how to do things, such as how to tell time by reading the numbers on a clock. This typically\nremains the same with age.\nAnswer C: Episodic memory captures the \"what,\" \"where,\" and \"when\" of an individual's daily life. This typically declines over time.\nAnswer D: Long-term memory refers to the storage of information over an extended period. This type of memory typically declines as\nindividuals age."}, {"question": "A rat is trained to distinguish between different color lights and learns to earn a reward for always pressing the lever under the red light. This is an example of which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Emulation learning", "B": "Stimulus generalization", "C": "Spontaneous recovery", "D": "Discrimination learning"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Discrimination learning refers to the formation of associations between different stimuli and corresponding outcomes or behaviors.\nAnswer A: Emulation learning occurs when individuals learn about parts of their environment and use this to achieve their own goals.\nAnswer B: Stimulus generalization is the tendency for stimuli similar to an original stimulus in a learning paradigm to produce a response\napproximating that which was learned under the original condition.\nAnswer C: Spontaneous recovery refers to the sudden reappearance of a previously extinct conditioned response after the unconditioned\nstimulus has been removed for some time."}, {"question": "If an individual swats at a bee and is stung as a result, thus decreasing future swatting behavior, they are exhibiting which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Positive punishment", "B": "Negative reinforcement", "C": "Negative punishment", "D": "Escape contingency"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Positive punishment involves the addition of an undesirable consequence (i.e., a bee sting) which decreases the likelihood of the behavior (i.e.,\nswatting) occurring again.\nAnswer B: Negative reinforcement is the encouragement of certain behaviors by removing or avoiding a negative outcome or stimuli.\nAnswer C: Negative punishment reduces a behavior or response by taking away a favorable stimulus following that action.\nAnswer D: An escape contingency is a situation in which an individual must respond in a particular way to escape from an aversive stimulus."}, {"question": "Ebbinghaus was one of the \u0000rst investigators to systematically study memory. In his studies, Ebbinghaus used himself as a subject and memorized lists of nonsense syllables. Results of his research indicated that:", "options": {"A": "syllables in the middle of a list are better remembered than those at the beginning and end of the list", "B": "when memorizing syllables, new learning tends to interfere with previous learning", "C": "overlearning improves memory for syllables up to a point but thereafter has no effect", "D": "rote learning of syllables tends to lead to rapid forgetting"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Ebbinghaus memorized syllables using rote learning (verbatim learning through repetition) and found that rote learning of nonsense syllables\nleads to rapid forgetting. Ebbinghaus was interested in assessing the effects of time on memory and, based on the results of his studies,\nconstructed a \"curve of forgetting,\" which shows that 50% of memorized syllables are forgotten within less than one hour.\nAnswer A: This is the opposite of what is true. In addition, this was not a phenomenon discussed by Ebbinghaus.\nAnswer B: This phenomenon is referred to as retroactive inhibition but was not discussed by Ebbinghaus.\nAnswer C: This is not true; the greater the overlearning, the greater the retention."}, {"question": "As de\u0000ned by Bandura, self-e\u0000cacy beliefs have four sources. These are:", "options": {"A": "prior accomplishments, observations of others, verbal persuasion, and emotional and physiological states", "B": "external reinforcement, internal (self) reinforcement, vicarious reinforcement, and logical veri\u0000cation", "C": "observation of others, imitation of others, reinforcement by others, and self-reinforcement", "D": "observation, cognitive mediation, rehearsal, and successful performance"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "These are the four sources of self-e\u0000cacy that Bandura has identi\u0000ed. Bandura's (1982, 1986) theory of self-e\u0000cacy proposes that people\nundertake activities they believe they are capable of performing.\nAnswers B, C, & D are incorrect. These answer choices include terms from various learning theories, including Bandura's theory of\nobservational learning, but they do not describe the four sources of self-e\u0000cacy beliefs."}, {"question": "Tova and Thom are in their early 30s, have been married for four years, and are expecting their \u0000rst child. The only vehicle they have had since their marriage is a two-seater sports car, which they both love, but they realize they will have to trade it for something more \"sensible\" when the baby is born. In terms of Piaget's theory of cognitive development, the couple's realization about their car can be best viewed as an example of:", "options": {"A": "assimilation", "B": "accommodation", "C": "centration", "D": "decalage"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "This is an example of a question that takes a concept that may be familiar but places it in an unexpected context. Accommodation involves\nmodifying existing cognitive structures or creating new ones to meet the demands of the environment. In this situation, the couple has realized\nthat they will have to alter their schema for \"car.\"\nAnswer A: Assimilation involves interpreting new information in terms of existing cognitive structures.\nAnswer C: Centration is the inability to focus on more than one aspect of an object or situation at a time and underlies the inability to conserve.\nAnswer D: A horizontal decalage is the name for the gradual mastery of logical concepts."}, {"question": "Congenital cytomegalovirus (CMV) is the second-most common cause of an intellectual disability after Down Syndrome. In addition to cognitive impairments, it is most likely to cause which of the following?", "options": {"A": "Facial deformities", "B": "Heart and lung abnormalities", "C": "Genital, urinary, and intestinal abnormalities", "D": "Hearing and visual impairments"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "CMV is a type of herpes virus. Although infection with CMV during adulthood usually produces no or mild symptoms, infection during prenatal\ndevelopment or birth can be life-threatening. Approximately 10% of infants infected with CMV show symptoms at birth, and about 10 to 15% of\ninfants who are asymptomatic at birth develop symptoms during the \u0000rst few years of life. Common symptoms include some degree of\nintellectual disability and hearing and visual impairments.\nAnswer A: Prenatal exposure to alcohol is likely to cause facial deformities.\nAnswer B: Rubella is likely to cause heart and lung abnormalities, or even worse, death.\nAnswer C: CMV is not directly linked to genital, urinary, or intestinal abnormalities."}, {"question": "According to Kohlberg, the moral judgments of preschool children are ordinarily based on:", "options": {"A": "the dictates of the superego", "B": "role conformity", "C": "an inner sense of right and wrong", "D": "the consequences of the act"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Kohlberg's theory of moral development distinguishes between three stages, with each stage including two substages. The three stages are\npre-conventional, conventional, and post-conventional. Preschool children are in the pre-conventional stage of moral development and base\ntheir moral judgments on the consequences of actions.\nAnswer A: Kohlberg did not refer to the superego.\nAnswer B: This is more characteristic of individuals in the conventional stage.\nAnswer C: Kohlberg did not describe moral development in terms of an \"inner sense.\""}, {"question": "Which of the following statements illustrates animistic thinking?", "options": {"A": "Step on a crack, break your mother's back.", "B": "My knees are shaking, so I must be scared.", "C": "At night the sun takes a nap.", "D": "The man on the TV is talking to me."}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Animistic thinking is the tendency to attribute life (and life-like qualities) to inanimate objects. This is a type of precausal reasoning, which is\ncharacteristic of the preoperational stage.\nAnswer A: This is superstitious thinking and does not re\u0000ect the life-like attribution central to animism.\nAnswer B: This illustrates the James-Lange theory of emotion.\nAnswer D: This is an example of a delusion of reference."}, {"question": "Although the nature-nurture controversy has not been resolved, many theorists now view development as involving both factors. More speci\u0000cally, theorists who take a middle ground with regard to this issue:", "options": {"A": "view some traits as being due primarily to nature and others as being due primarily to nurture", "B": "believe that genetic potentials (nature) cannot be realized unless the appropriate environmental experiences (nurture) occur", "C": "believe that most traits re\u0000ect about equal contributions of nature and nurture", "D": "view individual differences as due to genetic factors (nature) and group differences as due to environmental factors (nurture)"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Recent theories of development typically view nature and nature as contingent factors; i.e., development cannot occur without the ongoing\ncontributions of each factor. This is the only response that suggests a contingent relationship between the factors and, therefore, is the best\nanswer.\nAnswer A: Although this is true, this answer is not the best one since it does not address the contingent relationship between nature and\nnurture.\nAnswer C: This is not a good description of current thinking about the nature-nurture controversy.\nAnswer D: This is not an accurate description of the current status of the nature-nurture controversy."}, {"question": "____________ theory of socially mediated learning is based on the premise that learning is \u0000rst socially mediated, then self- mediated.", "options": {"A": "Vygotsky's", "B": "Piaget's", "C": "Dewey's", "D": "Gagne's"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The cognitive development approaches of Piaget and Vygotsky are likely to be featured on the exam. Vygotsky's sociocultural theory is also\nreferred to as the theory of socially mediated learning. Vygotsky described learning as \u0000rst social, then individual.\nAnswer B: Piaget's developmental theory (which is also known as the theory of genetic epistemology) proposes that individuals build cognitive\nstructures in active and adaptive ways (e.g., through assimilation and accommodation).\nAnswer C: Dewey's theory emphasizes the experiential aspects of learning, and it proposes that learning is the result of our experiences and\nattempts to make sense of those experiences.\nAnswer D: Gagne's approach to learning focuses on instruction and is sometimes categorized as an information processing approach. It\nfocuses on learning outcomes (cognitive effective or psychomotor) and the conditions that facilitate each type of outcome."}, {"question": "\"I have two feets\" and \"I runned fast\" are examples of the grammatical error known as:", "options": {"A": "telegraphic speech", "B": "holophrastic speech", "C": "overextension", "D": "overregularization"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Overregularization occurs when children overextend grammatical rules - e.g., add \"s\" to feet to form the plural or \"ed\" to run to form the past\ntense.\nAnswer A: Telegraphic speech consists of primarily content words (e.g., Ball go, Daddy jump, Want cup), which omits adjectives, articles, and\nother grammatical morphemes.\nAnswer B: Holophrastic speech is when children (typically between 10 and 18 months) use one word at a time when speaking. For example,\ndepending on the context and how the word is spoken, milk may mean That is milk, I want more milk, or I spilled the milk.\nAnswer C: Overextension is the tendency of very young children to extend the use of a word beyond the scope of its speci\u0000c meaning, such as\nby referring to all animals as \"doggie.\""}, {"question": "Bell and Ainsworth (1972) conducted one of the \u0000rst longitudinal studies on the impact of maternal responsiveness on infant crying. The results of their research indicated that a mother's prompt and consistent response to her infant's crying during the \u0000rst few months of the infant's life:", "options": {"A": "has no effect on the amount of infant crying in subsequent months", "B": "is associated with an increase in the amount of infant crying in subsequent months", "C": "is associated with a decrease in the amount of infant crying in subsequent months", "D": "is associated with a decrease in the amount of infant crying in subsequent months only for infants high in irritability"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Research on the impact of caregiver responsiveness and infant crying has, like research on a number of other issues, produced inconsistent\nresults. However, this question is asking about the results of a speci\u0000c study rather than the conclusion that can be drawn from the existing\nstudies. Bell and Ainsworth (1972) found that consistent and prompt maternal response to infant crying during the \u0000rst three months of the\ninfant's life was associated with a reduced frequency and shorter duration of crying and fussing in subsequent months. Note, however, that\ntheir study was criticized on methodological grounds and that subsequent studies have provided both con\u0000rming and discon\u0000rming results.\n[e.g. Regine, A.S. & Maarit, S. (2007). Natural parenting - back to basics in infant care. Evolutionary Psychology, 5(1), 102-18.\nAnswer A: Incorrect.\nAnswer B:Incorrect.\nAnswer D: Research by Bell and Ainsworth and other investigators has not found that the level of infant irritability mediates the relationship\nbetween caregiver responsiveness and infant crying."}, {"question": "Neo-Piagetian theories of cognitive development differ from Piaget's theory in terms of their:", "options": {"A": "emphasis on information processing", "B": "emphasis on the role of social in\u0000uences", "C": "rejection of a stage model of development", "D": "rejection of Piaget's notion of domain speci\u0000city"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The neo-Piagetian theories use information processing concepts to explain proposed shortcomings of Piagetian theory.\nAnswer B: An emphasis on social in\u0000uences is not a factor that distinguishes Piagetian and neo-Piagetian theories.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. The neo-Piagetians view cognitive development as involving stages.\nAnswer D: This is the opposite of what is true. Neo-Piagetians are interested in cognitive development within speci\u0000c domains."}, {"question": "In the brain, the __________ lobes are the last to fully develop and myelinate.", "options": {"A": "frontal", "B": "temporal", "C": "parietal", "D": "occipital"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Development of the cortex corresponds to the emergence of various capabilities. The frontal lobes mediate a number of functions including\nhigher-order cognitive skills, which do not fully develop until early adulthood.\nAnswers B, C, and D: The frontal lobes in the brain are the last to fully develop and myelinate."}, {"question": "PKU (phenylketonuria) is an inherited disorder that can cause Intellectual Disability:", "options": {"A": "only in individuals who are homozygous for the condition", "B": "only in individuals who are heterozygous for the condition", "C": "in individuals who are either homozygous or heterozygous for the condition", "D": "in an individual who has at least one parent who is homozygous for the condition"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "PKU is an autosomal recessive condition, which means that an individual must have two recessive genes (i.e., be homozygous) to have the\ndisorder.\nAnswers B, C, and D: These responses provide incorrect information - PKU can cause Intellectual Disability only in individuals who are\nhomozygous for the condition."}, {"question": "Research conducted by Mamie and Kenneth Clark in the 1940s led to their conclusion that:", "options": {"A": "for young Black children, recognition of their race is linked to low self-esteem", "B": "distrust of Whites by adolescent and adult Blacks is a healthy, adaptive response to racism", "C": "young Black children do not begin to form a racial identity until they enter school", "D": "when compared to White children, Black children exhibit accelerated motor development during the \u0000rst three years of life"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Sociologists Kenneth and Mamie Clark studied the identity and self-esteem of African American children in the 1930s and 1940s. The results\nhelped the NAACP win the \"Brown v. Board of Education\" case, which led to the banning of school segregation.\nAnswers B, C, and D: The work of Kenneth and Mamie Clark concluded that for young Black children, recognition of their race is linked to low\nself-esteem."}, {"question": "Which of the following is true about gender differences in newborns?", "options": {"A": "Newborn girls have higher morbidity and mortality rates than do newborn boys.", "B": "Newborn boys have higher morbidity and mortality rates than do newborn girls.", "C": "Newborn girls and boys have similar morbidity and mortality rates.", "D": "Newborn girls have higher mortality rates, but newborn boys have higher morbidity rates."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Male newborns are not only less mature than female newborns but are also have higher mortality rates and are more vulnerable to a variety of\nillnesses. [e.g., Wells, J.C.K. (2000). Natural selection and sex differences in morbidity and mortality in early life. Journal of Theoretical Biology,\n202,(1), 65-76]\nAnswers A, C, and D: Research has indicated newborn boys have higher morbidity and mortality rates than do newborn girls."}, {"question": "Gender Role Con\u0000ict research is important primarily:", "options": {"A": "for determining sex-role identi\u0000cation in boys and adolescents", "B": "in explaining pre-feminism concepts of the roles of men and women", "C": "because men low in Gender Role Con\u0000ict experience less life satisfaction, self-esteem, and psychological well-being", "D": "because restrictive de\u0000nitions of masculinity affect men, women and society at large"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "There is considerable evidence that men that are high in Gender Role Con\u0000ict have many behavioral and mental health problems that affect\nfamily and work relationships as well as their own well-being and ability to participate positively in society. [e.g., O'Neil, J. M. (2015). Men's\ngender role con\u0000ict: Psychological costs, consequences, and an agenda for change. American Psychological Association].\nAnswer A: Gender Role Con\u0000ict has not been associated with sex-role identi\u0000cation.\nAnswer B: Gender Role Con\u0000ict continues to have an in\u0000uence on the behavior of men and is not primarily relevant to past concepts.\nAnswer C: This is opposite to the \u0000ndings of research on Gender Role Con\u0000ict. Men who have more Gender Role Con\u0000ict have more\npsychological adjustment problems. [e.g., Wong, J.Y. et al. (2016). Meta-Analyses of the Relationship Between Conformity to Masculine Norms\nand Mental Health-Related Outcomes. Journal of Counseling Psychology, 64(1), 80 93.]"}, {"question": "Kochanska's (1997) research \u0000ndings suggest that, during the toddler years, the optimal parental behavior for ensuring the development of conscience in a child depends on the child's:", "options": {"A": "activity level", "B": "level of fearfulness", "C": "intelligence", "D": "attachment"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "A number of researchers have found that fearful children score higher on measures of conscience, and Kochanska extended this \u0000nding by\nshowing that the relationship between fearfulness and conscience development is affected by caregivers' socialization practices. Speci\u0000cally,\nshe found that, among fearful toddlers, conscience development is fostered when the mother uses \"gentle discipline.\" In contrast, among\nfearless toddlers, conscience development depends more on a secure mother-child attachment and maternal responsiveness. [Kochanska, G.\n(1997). Multiple pathways to conscience for children with different temperaments: From toddlerhood to age 5, Developmental Psychology,\n33(2), 228-240.]\nAnswers A, C, and D: Kochanska's research focused on the relationship between fearfulness and conscience development."}, {"question": "Which of the following is not true about the impact of childhood sexual abuse on adolescent adjustment?", "options": {"A": "Boys are more likely to use substances.", "B": "Girls are more likely to show symptoms of depression.", "C": "Both boys and girls show maladaptive coping.", "D": "Signi\u0000cant racial and ethnic differences are shown in coping response."}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Gray and Rarick (2018) examined a national sample of more than 13,000 adolescents and showed that boys were more likely to use\nsubstances, but girls were more likely to show symptoms of depression in the context of childhood sexual abuse history. No racial or ethnic\ndifferences in coping were observed. [Gray, S., & Rarick, S. (2018). Exploring Gender and Racial/Ethnic Differences in the Effects of Child Sexual\nAbuse. Journal of child sexual abuse, 27(5), 570587.]\nAnswer A: This answer is true.\nAnswer B: This answer is true.\nAnswer C: This answer is true."}, {"question": "For most children, expressions of pride, shame, and guilt are \u0000rst evident by ________ months of age.", "options": {"A": "12", "B": "20", "C": "30", "D": "42"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Self-conscious emotions become apparent during the second and third years of life. There is evidence that empathy, jealousy, and\nembarrassment emerge at about 18 to 24 months of age and are followed by pride, shame, and guilt at about 30 months. [e.g., Lewis, M.\n(2002). Early emotional development in A. Slatter and M. Lewis (Eds.). Introduction to infant development. New York: Oxford University Press.]\nAnswers A, B, and D: Self-conscious emotions become apparent during the second and third years of life - speci\u0000cally pride, shame, and guilt at\nabout 30 months."}, {"question": "The state of the evidence on sensory compensation in deaf children is that:", "options": {"A": "children who are deaf outperform peers without hearing de\u0000cits on most visual tasks, and this difference is apparent by four to six years of age", "B": "children who are deaf outperform peers without hearing de\u0000cits on most visual tasks, but this difference is not apparent until preadolescence", "C": "children who are deaf perform similarly to peers without hearing de\u0000cits on most visual tasks", "D": "children who are deaf do more poorly than peers without hearing de\u0000cits on most visual tasks"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Contrary to the predictions of the sensory compensation hypothesis, deafness is not generally associated with superior visual abilities. One\nexception is that some deaf children and adults who use sign language exhibit superior visual processing skills on mental rotation, face\nrecognition, and visual attention tasks, but this advantage appears to be due to the use of sign language rather than to deafness itself.\n[Albertine, J.A. (2010). Deafness and hearing loss. In I. B. Weiner and W. E. Craighead (Eds.) The Corsini encyclopedia of psychology, (pp 461-\n462). Hoboken, NJ: John Wiley & Sons, 2010.]\nAnswer A: Incorrect.\nAnswer B: Incorrect.\nAnswer D: Incorrect."}, {"question": "Malnutrition during fetal development is likely to have which effect on the brain?", "options": {"A": "Scarring due to the death of brain cells", "B": "Synaptic enlargement", "C": "Malformation of the brain cells", "D": "A reduced number of brain cells"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The most consistent \u0000nding of the research is that malnutrition during fetal development results in a reduced number of brain cells. There is\nalso some evidence that there are problems in myelination.\nAnswer A: Incorrect.\nAnswer B: Incorrect.\nAnswer C: Incorrect."}, {"question": "Studies on information processing during the \u0000rst two years of life indicate that infants \u0000rst exhibit recognition memory for up to 24 hours following presentation of a stimulus at about ____ months of age.", "options": {"A": "3", "B": "5", "C": "7", "D": "9"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "By three months of age, infants habituate to visual stimuli - i.e., show less response to the second presentation of a stimulus for periods up to\n24 hours. This is interpreted as indicating that the infant recognizes the stimulus.\nAnswer B: Incorrect.\nAnswer C: By 7 months, infants begin to show signs of recall memory.\nAnswer D: Incorrect."}, {"question": "A child living in the United States is exposed to both English and a second language between the ages of six months and three years and, as a result, becomes \u0000uent in both languages. If the child had not been exposed to the second language until after the age of 4 or 5, she would have had more trouble acquiring the second language. This provides evidence for:", "options": {"A": "the concept of critical periods", "B": "the concept of sensitive periods", "C": "the Whor\u0000an hypothesis", "D": "the notion of imprinting"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Although some authors use the terms \"critical period\" and \"sensitive period\" interchangeably, they actually mean two different things. A\nsensitive period refers to an optimal period rather than a necessary period. The situation described in this question suggests there is a\nsensitive period for dual language learning.\nAnswer A: A critical period refers to a time when certain developmental events MUST happen, or later aspects of development will not occur.\nAnswer C: The Whor\u0000an hypothesis says that the structure of a language determines or greatly in\u0000uences the modes of thought and behavior\ncharacteristic of the culture in which it is spoken. This does not apply to this question.\nAnswer D: Although imprinting is also a type of phase-sensitive learning, it speci\u0000cally refers to the example of certain species of animals that\nform an attachment to the \u0000rst large moving object that they meet."}, {"question": "For most children, holophrastic speech begins between the ages of:", "options": {"A": "8 to 12 months", "B": "12 to 18 months", "C": "18 to 24 months", "D": "24 to 36 months"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "A child is exhibiting holophrastic speech when they use a single word to convey an entire idea - e.g., uses the word \"juice\" to mean \"give me\nsome juice.\" Most children exhibit holophrastic speech by 12 to 18 months.\nAnswer A: Incorrect.\nAnswer C: Children begin exhibiting telegraphic speech (the use of two words to convey an entire sentence) between the ages of 18 and 24\nmonths.\nAnswer D: By about 24 to 36 months, most children use sentences of three or more words."}, {"question": "A drive for order that involves the testing of patterns and structures against the real world is referred to as:", "options": {"A": "elaboration", "B": "deduction", "C": "equilibration", "D": "accommodation"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "As de\u0000ned by Piaget equilibration involves a combination of assimilation and accommodation and is motivated by a drive for balance or order.\nThis is a di\u0000cult question because it asks about a familiar concept using unfamiliar language.\nAnswer A: Elaboration refers to the process of increasing the number of associations between items of information which enhances the\nmeaningfulness of the information and is associated with improvements in retention and retrieval.\nAnswer B: Deduction refers to reasoning from the general to the speci\u0000c.\nAnswer D: Accommodation is only one aspect of equilibration."}, {"question": "According to Gyorgy Gergely (1994), visual feature representation is a necessary precondition for:", "options": {"A": "gender identity development", "B": "mirror self-recognition", "C": "attachment", "D": "social referencing"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "According to Gergely, the ability to recognize oneself in the mirror (which occurs between 1-1/2 and 2 years in humans) requires a certain level\nof cognitive development. One cognitive prerequisite is the construction of \"a visual feature representation of the typical physical appearance\nof the not-directly-visible parts of ... [the] body\" [(Gergely, G. (1994).From self-recognition to theory of mind. in S. T. Parker et al., (Eds.). Self-\nawareness in animals and humans. Boston: Cambridge University Press, p. 55.]\nAnswer A: Incorrect. Gender identity is not related to visual feature representation.\nAnswer C: Incorrect. Attachment is not related to visual feature representation.\nAnswer D: Incorrect. Social referencing is not related to visual feature representation."}, {"question": "During Piaget's heteronomous stage, children's moral judgments are based on:", "options": {"A": "the intentions behind the act", "B": "the seriousness of the consequences of the act", "C": "the status or authority of the actor", "D": "the events leading up to the act"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Piaget proposed a sequence of moral development involving three stages: premoral, moral realism, and moral relativism. Rules and the\nconsequences of violating a rule are the basis of moral judgment during the stage of moral realism (which is also called the heteronomous\nstage).\nAnswer A: This is more characteristic of moral relativism (which is also known as the autonomous stage).\nAnswer C: Incorrect.\nAnswer C: Incorrect."}, {"question": "According to Gerald Patterson and his colleagues, which of the following is a key contributor to Conduct Disorder?", "options": {"A": "The child's temperament", "B": "The child's cognitive skills", "C": "The strength of the parent-child attachment", "D": "The parent's child-rearing skills"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Gerald Patterson and his colleagues take a social learning approach to explain aggressive behavior in children and adolescents. His \"coercion\nhypothesis\" focuses on interactions between parents and their children and identi\u0000es the parents' child-rearing skills as a key determinant of\nthe nature of these interactions. [e.g., Smith, J. D., Dishion, T. J., Shaw, D. S., Wilson, M. N., Winter, C. C., & Patterson, G. R. (2014). Coercive\nfamily process and early-onset conduct problems from age 2 to school entry. Development and Psychopathology, 26(4 Pt 1), 917932.]\nAnswers A, B, and C: Patterson and his colleagues believe parent's childrearing skills are a key contributor to Conduct Disorder. Their research\ndid not investigate child temperament, the child's cognitive skills, or parent-child attachment."}, {"question": "A 22-month old refers to all vehicles - e.g., cars, buses, and trucks - as \"cars.\" However, when asked to point to a picture of a bus or truck, she is able to do so. This represents:", "options": {"A": "represents normal language development", "B": "giftedness in the area of language development", "C": "an early sign of autism", "D": "an early sign of a language production disorder"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Children's production and comprehension of language develop at different rates. Overextension (applying a word to a wider collection of\nobjects or events than is appropriate) is a normal part of language acquisition. In addition, young children exhibit greater understanding\n(comprehension) of language than is suggested by their production of language.\nAnswer B: The child is displaying a normal level of language development and thus is not gifted.\nAnswer C: This pattern does not suggest autism.\nAnswer D: This pattern does not suggest any language disorder."}, {"question": "Studies investigating stepparent-stepchild relationships indicate that:", "options": {"A": "stepparent-stepchild relationships are less likely to be con\u0000ictual when the child is in early adolescence when the remarriage occurs", "B": "stepfathers tend to be distant and disengaged from their stepchildren", "C": "stepmothers tend to be more authoritative in parenting style than stepfathers", "D": "stepfather-stepchild relationships tend to be more di\u0000cult for boys than for girls"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "This \u0000nding is often cited in the literature and is particularly true for stepfathers who do not have biological children. [Santrock, J.W., Sitterle,\nK.A. and Warshak, R.A. (1988). Parent-child relationships in stepfather families in P. Bronstein and C. P. Cowan (Eds.) Fatherhood today. New\nYork,: Wiley.]\nAnswer A: In most cases, this is the opposite of what is true: relationships with stepparents are most di\u0000cult when remarriage occurs when\nthe child is in early adolescence\nAnswer C: There is some evidence that stepparents tend to be more authoritarian (not authoritative) than biological parents are.\nAnswer D: This is the opposite of what has been found to be true."}, {"question": "Which of the following best describes the relationship between REM sleep and dreaming?", "options": {"A": "All dreaming occurs during REM sleep.", "B": "Most dreaming occurs during REM sleep and dreams during non-REM sleep are less vivid and elaborate and more realistic.", "C": "Most dreaming occurs during REM sleep but dreams during non-REM sleep are more vivid, elaborate, and bizarre in nature.", "D": "Most dreaming occurs during REM sleep and dreams during non-REM sleep occur only when the individual has been sleep-deprived."}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Studies on sleep and dreaming have consistently con\u0000rmed that most dreams occur during REM (rapid eye movement) sleep. Although some\ndreaming occurs during non-REM (Stages 1 through 4) sleep, these dreams consist of \u0000eeting images and are less vivid, detailed, and story-like\nthan the dreams that occur during REM sleep.\nAnswer A: While most dreams occur during REM sleep, some dreaming occurs during non-REM sleep. Dreams during non-REM sleep are\ntypically logical and more realistic.\nAnswer C: This is partially true in that most dreams occur during REM sleep; however, those that occur during non-REM sleep are actually more\nlogical and realistic, whereas those that occur during REM sleep are more vivid and bizarre.\nAnswer D: Most dreams do occur during REM sleep, but sleep deprivation does not impact whether or not someone experiences dreams during\nnon-REM sleep. Dreaming during non-REM sleep is typical and the dreams are usually more realistic and logical."}, {"question": "Patients who have recently started taking naltrexone (ReVia) as a treatment for alcohol dependence are most likely to experience which of the following drug side effects?", "options": {"A": "dizziness, ataxia, visual disturbances, nausea, and rash", "B": "blurred vision, sexual dysfunction, weight gain, edema, and tremor", "C": "shortness of breath, increased dreaming, nausea, diarrhea, and bradycardia", "D": "abdominal cramping, nausea, insomnia, nervousness, and headache"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "As a treatment for alcohol dependence, naltrexone is an opioid antagonist and blocks the part of the brain that feels pleasure, therefore,\nblocking the craving for and reinforcing effects of alcohol. While these are potential side effects of naltrexone, most people experience few\nside effects when taking naltrexone as a treatment for alcohol dependence.\nAnswer A: These side effects are characteristic of carbamazepine, an anti-seizure drug that is also used to treat bipolar disorder.\nAnswer B: These are possible side effects of the MAO inhibitors.\nAnswer C: These are possible side effects of the beta blocker propranolol."}, {"question": "The _________ contains neural circuits that control sequences of movements that constitute species-typical behaviors, such as \u0000ghting and mating.", "options": {"A": "substantia nigra", "B": "cerebellar peduncles", "C": "hypothalamus", "D": "medulla oblongata"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The hypothalamus contains neural circuits that control sequences of movements that constitute species-typical behaviors, such as \u0000ghting\nand mating.\nAnswer A: The substantia nigra is a midbrain dopaminergic nucleus which has a critical role in modulating motor movement and reward\nfunction.\nAnswer B: A cerebellar peduncle is a nerve tract that permits communication between the cerebellum and other parts of the central nervous\nsystem. As a whole, the peduncles assist in re\u0000ning motor movements, learning new motor skills, and converting proprioceptive information\ninto balance and posture maintenance.\nAnswer D: The medulla oblongata is responsible for regulating several basic functions of the autonomic nervous system, including respiration,\ncardiac function, vasodilatation, and re\u0000exes like vomiting, coughing, and sneezing."}, {"question": "Research on the role of hormones on sexual arousal suggests that _______.", "options": {"A": "androgen is responsible for arousal in males, while estrogen is responsible for arousal in females", "B": "estrogen is responsible for arousal in females experiencing menopause and receiving hormone replacement therapy", "C": "estrogen is responsible for arousal in both males and females", "D": "androgen is responsible for arousal in both females and males"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Although research on the role of hormones in sexual behavior remains somewhat inconclusive, there is su\u0000cient evidence to conclude that\ncertain hormones do play a role in human sexual arousal. The research suggests that estrogen plays an insigni\u0000cant role in female sexual\nmotivation and arousal and that androgen, which is produced by the adrenal cortex, plays an important role in the sexual functioning of both\nmales and females.\nAnswer A: Androgen plays an important role in the sexual functioning of both males and females and research suggests that estrogen plays an\ninsigni\u0000cant role in sexual arousal.\nAnswer B: When women experience a loss of sexual interest following menopause, androgen was found to be more effective for restoring\nsexual arousal. There is no clear evidence that the increase in estrogen from hormone replacement therapy improves sex drive.\nAnswer C: Research suggests that estrogen plays an insigni\u0000cant role in sexual arousal."}, {"question": "Morphine, an opioid agonist, produces euphoria and other pleasurable feelings primarily by increasing __________ activity in the \"reward center\" of the brain.", "options": {"A": "dopamine", "B": "serotonin", "C": "glutamate", "D": "norepinephrine"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Agonists are drugs that mimic the activity of endogenous neurotransmitters. Opioid agonists are drugs that stimulate activity at receptors\nstimulated by endorphins and other naturally occurring opioids. Naturally occurring opioids, such as morphine, exert their effects by increasing\ndopamine activity at opioid receptors in the ventral tegmental area and nucleus accumbens.\nAnswer B: Serotonin regulates anxiety, happiness, and mood. Low levels of serotonin have been associated with depression. Increased levels\nof serotonin as a result of medication decrease arousal.\nAnswer C: At normal concentrations, glutamate is crucial for brain functions such as learning and memory. However, at high concentrations,\nincreased activity caused by glutamate results in over-excitation of nerve cells, which eventually leads to cell death.\nAnswer D: Together with adrenaline, norepinephrine increases heart rate and blood pumping from the heart. Norepinephrine also increases\nblood pressure and helps break down fat, and increases blood sugar levels to provide more energy to the body."}, {"question": "Long-term stress can result in excessive production of ___________ which, in turn, can suppress the body's immune system.", "options": {"A": "antigens", "B": "interferons", "C": "corticosteroids", "D": "anabolic steroids"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Chronic stress leads to the release of corticosteroids (cortisol and corticosterone) by the adrenal cortex. Continually elevated levels of\ncorticosteroids cause suppression of the immune system by interfering with the production of antibodies and T-cells, ultimately lowering the\nbody's resistance to disease.\nAnswer A: Antigens are substances (e.g., bacteria, viruses) that stimulate the body's production of antibodies.\nAnswer B: Interferons are antiviral proteins that are produced by certain cells in the body in response to exposure to a virus.\nAnswer D: Anabolic steroids are manufactured substances that mimic male sex hormones."}, {"question": "A reading disorder where a person can read words phonetically but has di\u0000culty reading irregularly spelled words by the whole-word method is called _______.", "options": {"A": "direct dyslexia", "B": "spelling dyslexia", "C": "phonological dyslexia", "D": "surface dyslexia"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Surface dyslexia is a de\u0000cit in whole-word reading, usually caused by a lesion of the left lateral temporal lobe. Individuals with surface dyslexia\nhave di\u0000culty recognizing words as a whole and, therefore, must listen to their pronunciation to understand what they are reading.\nAnswer A: Direct dyslexia is a language disorder caused by brain damage that results in di\u0000culty understanding words even when they are read\naloud.\nAnswer B: Spelling dyslexia is an alternative name for word-form dyslexia. Word-form dyslexia is a disorder in which a person can read a word\nonly after spelling out the individual letters.\nAnswer C: Phonological dyslexia is a reading disorder in which a person can read familiar words but has di\u0000culty reading unfamiliar words or\npronounceable nonwords."}, {"question": "Accidental changes in the chromosomes of sperm or eggs that join together and develop new organisms is(are) called ________.", "options": {"A": "selective advantage", "B": "evolution", "C": "mutations", "D": "natural selection"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "A mutation is a change in a DNA sequence. Mutations can result from DNA copying mistakes made during cell division, exposure to ionizing\nradiation, exposure to chemicals called mutagens, or infection by viruses.\nAnswer A: Selective advantage is the characteristic of an organism that enables and reproduce better than other organisms in a population in a\ngiven environment.\nAnswer B: Evolution is the change in the heritable characteristics of a species over several generations and relies on the process of natural\nselection.\nAnswer D: Natural selection is the process by which an organism adapts to its environment through selectively reproducing change in its\ngenotype."}, {"question": "Any difference in charge (positive or negative) across the membrane is called the _________.", "options": {"A": "membrane potential", "B": "resting potential", "C": "action potential", "D": "threshold of excitation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The membrane potential is a potential gradient that forces ions to passively move in one direction. Positive ions are attracted by the negative\nside of the membrane and negative ions by the positive side.\nAnswer B: The resting potential is the imbalance of electrical charge that exists between the interior of electrically excitable neurons and their\nsurroundings.\nAnswer C: An action potential is a sudden, fast, transitory, and propagating change of the resting membrane potential. Only neurons and\nmuscle cells are capable of generating an action potential.\nAnswer D: The excitation threshold refers to the level of neural depolarization that is necessary to generate an actional potential. More simply,\nthis means the level of excitation (through neurochemical stimulation) that is needed for a muscle to react appropriately to a stimulus."}, {"question": "The three main types of genetic testing (cytogenetic, biochemical, molecular) examine what speci\u0000c areas for any abnormalities?", "options": {"A": "chromosome structure, protein function, and mitochondrial nerves", "B": "DNA sequence, protein structure, and cortex neurons", "C": "chromosome structure, protein function, and DNA sequence", "D": "binomial function, DNA sequence, and protein structure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Cytogenetics involves the examination of chromosomes and their abnormalities, such as an extra copy of a chromosome. Biochemical testing\nutilizes techniques that examine the amount or activity level of proteins or enzymes instead of the gene. Molecular or DNA testing examines\nDNA sequences and tends to be the most e\u0000cacious methodology, particularly if the function of the protein is not known and a biochemical\ntest cannot be developed.\nAnswer A: Mitochondrial nerves are not part of the genetic material tested.\nAnswer B: Cortex neurons are not tested for abnormalities.\nAnswer D: Binomial function is not part of the genetic material that is tested."}, {"question": "Which of the following techniques is used to diagnose epilepsy or study the stages of sleep and wakefulness?", "options": {"A": "magnetoencephalography (MEG)", "B": "electroencephalogram (EEG)", "C": "autoradiography", "D": "positron emission tomography (PET)"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "EEGs, or \"writings of electricity from the head\" help detect potential problems associated with electrical impulses. They are used to diagnose\nepilepsy or study the stages of sleep and wakefulness, which are associated with characteristic patterns of electrical activity.\nAnswer A: MEG is a test that uses sensors to form an image of magnetic \u0000elds produced within the brain. These devices are used to \u0000nd the\nsources of seizures so that they can be removed surgically.\nAnswer C: Autoradiography can be translated as \"writing with one's radiation.\" This technique is used to study the distribution of radioactive\nisotopes in different organs.\nAnswer D: A PET scan is an imaging technique that reveals the functioning of the tissues and organs. A PET scan uses a radioactive tracer that\nenables visualization of metabolic processes."}, {"question": "Prosopagnosia is believed to be due to lesions in which of the following brain structures?", "options": {"A": "the junction of the occipital and temporal lobes", "B": "the parietal or frontal lobes", "C": "the precentral gyrus", "D": "the central sulcus"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Knowing that prosopagnosia is a type of visual agnosia and that the occipital lobe mediates visual processing may help with identifying the\ncorrect answer for this question. Prosopagnosia results from damage to the right fusiform gyrus located in the occipital and temporal lobe\nregions. The occipital lobe is involved in visual processing, while the temporal lobe mediates long-term memory. Damage to these areas may\nproduce an inability to recognize familiar faces.\nAnswer B: Although various forms of agnosia can be related to the parietal and frontal lobes, the occipitotemporal region is implicated in\nprosopagnosia. Anosognosia typically involves damage to the parietal or frontal lobes.\nAnswer C: The precentral gyrus contains the primary motor cortex and is not involved in visual processing.\nAnswer D: The central sulcus is the \u0000ssure separates the frontal and parietal lobes."}, {"question": "Biofeedback training and interventions focus on helping the individual _______.", "options": {"A": "decrease sympathetic nervous system activation and increase activation of the parasympathetic nervous system", "B": "decrease somatic nervous system activation and increase activation of the autonomic nervous system", "C": "increase sympathetic nervous system activation and decrease activation of the parasympathetic nervous system", "D": "increase somatic nervous systems activation and decrease activation of the autonomic nervous system"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Biofeedback is used to monitor and regulate autonomic nervous system functions (e.g., heart rate, blood pressure, galvanic skin response)\nthrough decreasing the sympathetic nervous system response and increasing the parasympathetic response.\nAnswer B: Although the somatic nervous system is a branch of the peripheral nervous system, it is responsible for sending and receiving\nsensory messages that control voluntary motor movement and skeletal muscles.\nAnswer C: Biofeedback training aims to decrease sympathetic nervous system arousal, which is activated during times of stress, and increase\nactivation of the parasympathetic nervous system to restore balance and homeostasis in the nervous system.\nAnswer D: Although the somatic nervous system is a branch of the peripheral nervous system, it is responsible for sending and receiving\nsensory messages that control voluntary motor movement and skeletal muscles."}, {"question": "Which type of seizure remains localized and produces a loss of consciousness?", "options": {"A": "grand mal seizure", "B": "complex partial seizure", "C": "simple partial seizure", "D": "generalized seizure"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Complex partial seizures lead to loss of consciousness. This type of seizure arises in one lobe of the brain and affects an individual's\nawareness.\nAnswer A: A grand mal seizure is a generalized, tonic-clonic seizure, which results in a convulsion. This type of seizure results in violent muscle\ncontractions and is caused by abnormal electrical activity throughout the brain.\nAnswer C: A simple partial seizure is one that remains localized but does not result in a loss of consciousness.\nAnswer D: A generalized seizure is one that involves most of the brain. These include absence, atonic, tonic, clonic, tonic-clonic, myoclonic, and\nfebrile seizures."}, {"question": "The ________, which consists of the caudate nucleus and the putamen, receives information regarding movements that are planned or that are in progress.", "options": {"A": "basal ganglia", "B": "globus pallidus", "C": "neostriatum", "D": "primary motor cortex"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The caudate nucleus and the putamen make up the neostriatum (or the striatum). It contains neuronal activity related to movements and\nfunctions as a relay-station for motivation.\nAnswer A: The basal ganglia refers to a group of subcortical nuclei responsible primarily for motor control, as well as other roles such as motor\nlearning, executive functions and behaviors, and emotions.\nAnswer B: The globus pallidus is considered part of the basal ganglia and is involved in the regulation of voluntary movement.\nAnswer D: The primary motor cortex is the area that provides the most important signal for the production of skilled movements."}, {"question": "MDMA causes cognitive de\u0000cits and selectively damages which type of neurons?", "options": {"A": "serotonergic", "B": "dopaminergic", "C": "histaminergic", "D": "norepinergic"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "MDMA causes the release of large amounts of serotonin in the brain. As a result, MDMA is toxic to central serotonergic nerve terminals and\naffects brain function negatively overall.\nAnswer B: The dopaminergic system is known for its stimulant and pleasurable effects in addition to its importance in learning. Elevated levels\nof dopamine are associated with mental illnesses such as schizophrenia, whereas low dopamine levels are associated with symptoms of\nParkinson's disease. MDMA releases a moderate amount of dopamine in the brain.\nAnswer C: Histaminergic pathways promote wakefulness and orchestrate disparate behaviors and homeostatic functions. Recent research\nsuggests that aberrant histamine signaling in the brain may also be a key factor in addictive behaviors and degenerative disease as Parkinson's\ndiseases and multiple sclerosis.\nAnswer D: Norepinergic pathways produce effects similar to dopamine such as alertness, focus, and motivation. MDMA releases large\namounts of norepinephrine."}, {"question": "Which brain mechanism causes cessation of hunger or thirst, produced by adequate and available supplies of nutrients or water?", "options": {"A": "negative feedback", "B": "satiety mechanism", "C": "detector", "D": "correctional mechanism"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Because of the delay between ingestion and replenishment of the depleted stores, ingestive behaviors are controlled by satiety mechanisms.\nAnswer A: Negative feedback is an essential characteristic of all regulatory systems. This type of feedback occurs when some function of the\noutput system, or mechanism, is fed back in a manner that tends to reduce the \u0000uctuations in the output.\nAnswer C: A detector, in a regulatory process, is a mechanism that signals when the system variable deviates from its set point.\nAnswer D: A correctional mechanism is one that is capable of changing the value of the system variable."}, {"question": "An individual experiencing tactile agnosia is likely experiencing damage to which areas of the brain?", "options": {"A": "premotor cortex and cerebellum", "B": "parietal lobe and somatosensory cortex", "C": "left temporal lobe, motor cortex, and somatosensory cortex", "D": "occipital lobe and somatosensory cortex"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The parietal lobe is mainly responsible for processing and integrating somatosensory information, including touch, texture, weight, shape, and\nsize, as well as heat, pain, and proprioception. With tactile agnosia, the patient is unable to recognize objects by touch without using the sense\nof sight.\nAnswer A: The premotor cortex, located in the frontal lobe, is involved in planning movement. The cerebellum, located at the base of the brain is\nresponsible for coordinating motor activity and maintaining smooth movements.\nAnswer C: While the somatosensory cortex is involved with processing sensory input, including touch, the left temporal lobe is involved with\nverbal memory and language comprehension. The motor cortex, located in the frontal lobe, next to the somatosensory cortex is involved with\nplanning and executing voluntary muscle movements.\nAnswer D: This answer is only partially correct. The somatosensory cortex is involved with processing sensory input through touch, but the\noccipital lobe is the primary visual cortex. When someone experiences tactile agnosia, they are unable to identify objects without the use of\nsight."}, {"question": "Which of the following method measures neurochemicals in the brain?", "options": {"A": "transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS)", "B": "electrical stimulation", "C": "positron emission tomography (PET)", "D": "magnetoencephalography (MEG)"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "A PET scan measures neurochemicals in the brain and localizes any radioactive substances that are absorbed.\nAnswer A: TMS is a noninvasive procedure that uses magnetic \u0000elds to stimulate nerve cells in the cerebral cortex to improve symptoms of\ndepression. TMS is typically used when other depression treatments have not been effective.\nAnswer B: Electrical stimulation is the use of a device to send gentle electrical pulses through the skin. Electrical stimulation stimulates\nneurons near the tip of the cannula.\nAnswer D: MEG records magnetic \u0000elds induced by neural activity. This technique can help determine the location of a group of neurons \u0000ring\nsynchronously."}, {"question": "Which of the following individuals is most likely to have hypertension?", "options": {"A": "a 52-year-old White man", "B": "a 65-year-old African-American man", "C": "a 43-year-old Hispanic woman", "D": "a 35-year-old Asian woman"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Research has con\u0000rmed that prevalence rates for hypertension are related to age, gender, and race/ethnicity. Information on the prevalence\nrates of hypertension in the United States are provided by the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (2020), which found the rates\nto be highest for African-Americans (non-Hispanic Black men and women), older individuals, and men. Hypertension prevalence was highest\namong non-Hispanic Black men and women.\nAnswer A: Overall, hypertension prevalence rates were lower for non-Hispanic White males compared to other answer options listed, and\ncontrolled hypertension was generally higher among non-Hispanic white adults than among non-Hispanic Black and non-Hispanic Asian adults.\nAnswer C: Hispanic women do experience higher prevalence rates of hypertension, particularly compared to Asian women; however, non-\nHispanic Black men and women experienced the highest rates of hypertension.\nAnswer D: This is not consistent with results of the National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (2020), which concluded that prevalence\nrates were highest for African-Americans (non-Hispanic Black men and women), older individuals, and men."}, {"question": "The _______ is located in the parietal lobe and receives information from the body senses.", "options": {"A": "central sulcus", "B": "calcarine \u0000ssure", "C": "striate cortex", "D": "insular cortex"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The central sulcus precisely divides the somatic sensory cortex of the parietal lobe from the motor cortex of the frontal lobe.\nAnswer B: The calcarine \u0000ssure is located on the medial surface of the occipital lobe and divides the visual cortex. It functions primarily in\ndiscerning the intensity, shape, size, and location of objects in the visual \u0000eld.\nAnswer C: The striate cortex is part of the visual cortex and is involved in processing visual information. It is the \u0000rst cortical visual area that\nreceives input from the lateral geniculate nucleus in the thalamus.\nAnswer D: The insular cortex links sensory experience and emotional valence. In addition to sensory signals from within the body, the insula\nreceives sensory information from the environment."}, {"question": "_______ is a process of removal of neurotransmitter from the synaptic cleft by the terminal button.", "options": {"A": "Enzymatic deactivation", "B": "Reuptake", "C": "Endocytosis", "D": "Excitation"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Reuptake is the process by which neurotransmitter molecules that have been released at a synapse are reabsorbed by the presynaptic neuron\nthat released them.\nAnswer A: Enzymatic deactivation is accomplished by an enzyme that destroys molecules of the neurotransmitter.\nAnswer C: Endocytosis means the process of entering a cell. It is a type of active transport that moves particles, such as large molecules, into\na cell.\nAnswer D: Excitation is the process by which nerve cells use their presynaptic terminals to stimulate the next receiving nerve cell in line to\ntransmit information onward."}, {"question": "The process whereby molecules distribute themselves evenly throughout the medium in which they are dissolved is called ________.", "options": {"A": "hyperpolarization", "B": "depolarization", "C": "electrostatic pressure", "D": "diffusion"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Diffusion is the movement of molecules from an area of high concentration of the molecules to an area with a lower concentration. The\ndifferences in the concentrations of the molecules in the two areas are called the concentration gradient.\nAnswer A: Hyperpolarization is when the membrane potential becomes more negative at a particular spot on the neuron's membrane.\nAnswer B: Depolarization is a process that causes a rapid change in membrane potential from a negative to a positive state.\nAnswer C: Electrostatic pressure is the force on two ions with a similar charge to repel each other and the force of two ions with the opposite\ncharge to attract to one another."}, {"question": "________ is the conscious experience and interpretation of information from the senses that involves neurons in the central nervous system.", "options": {"A": "Perception", "B": "Sensation", "C": "Saturation", "D": "Brightness"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Perception refers to the way sensory information is organized, interpreted, and consciously experienced.\nAnswer B: Sensation involves the cells of the nervous system that are specialized to detect stimuli from the environment.\nAnswer C: Saturation refers to the relative purity of the light that is perceived.\nAnswer D: Brightness is the second perceptual dimension of light and is a term that describes the intensity of light."}, {"question": "A male with a bruised spinal cord at C6 and C7 is likely to experience all of the following, except _______.", "options": {"A": "paralysis of all four limbs", "B": "the ability to achieve an erection", "C": "paresis", "D": "muscle weakness in the arms"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Individuals with complete severing of the spinal cord between C1 and C5 will experience quadriplegia. In this case, the individual experienced\nbruising at C6 and C7, so it is unlikely he has paralysis in all four limbs.\nAnswer B: Even with spinal cord damage, most re\u0000exes remain intact.\nAnswer C: Paresis, also known as muscle weakness, can occur when there is an incomplete severing of the spinal cord. The individual in the\nscenario has a bruised spinal cord, so muscle weakness is highly likely.\nAnswer D: An individual experiencing spinal cord injury at C6 or C7 may experience partial paralysis or weakness in the arms, depending on the\nextent of the injury. If an individuals spinal cord is severed, they are likely to experience paralysis in the legs and partial paralysis in the arms. In\nthis case, the individual bruised his spinal cord and weakness in the arms is likely."}, {"question": "An individual at Level ______ of the Rancho Scale of Cognitive Functioning Revised is unresponsive to sounds or light and appears to be in a state of deep sleep.", "options": {"A": "IV", "B": "XII", "C": "I", "D": "VIII"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The Rancho Scale of Cognitive Functioning Revised is used to monitor recovery from head trauma and involves rating the individual in terms of\nten levels of functioning. This answer accurately describes Level I. An individual who receives a Level I rating is unresponsive to sounds or light\nand appears to be in a state of deep sleep.\nAnswer A: An individual who receives a Level IV rating is confused and incoherent, may exhibit bizarre behavior, and is unable to care for\nthemselves.\nAnswer B: The Rancho Scale of Cognitive Functioning Revised only has ten levels of functioning.\nAnswer D: An individual who receives a Level VIII rating is oriented to person and place in familiar environments, needs minimal supervision for\nperforming routine activities, exhibits some de\u0000cits in judgment, abstract reasoning, and other aspects of intellectual functioning, and may be\noppositional and uncooperative."}, {"question": "Which piece of U.S. legislation requires that all people with disabilities undergo an evaluation to determine their speci\u0000c needs, the development of an education plan for each child with a disability attending public school, and the restriction of special education placement based on IQ tests alone?", "options": {"A": "AAIDD", "B": "ADA", "C": "IDEA", "D": "IEP"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The development of measures to assist in the diagnosis of Intellectual Disability is attributable to several factors. The Individuals with\nDisabilities Education Act (IDEA) requires the following: all disabled people from infancy to 21 years of age must be evaluated by a team of\nspecialists to determine their speci\u0000c needs; an Individualized Educational Program (IEP) must be developed for each disabled child enrolled in\nthe public education system that provides education for the student in the \"least restrictive environment,\" and that has been approved by the\nchild's parents; and while reliable, valid, and nondiscriminatory psychological and educational tests may be used, assignment to special\neducation classes cannot be made on the basis of IQ tests only.\nAnswer A: The American Association on Intellectual and Developmental Disabilities (AAIDD) is an American non-pro\u0000t professional\norganization concerned with intellectual disability and related developmental disabilities.\nAnswer B: The Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA) is a piece of legislation that prohibits discrimination against individuals with disabilities in\nemployment, transportation, communication, public accommodations, and governmental activities. With regard to assessment, it requires that\nany test administered to a job applicant or employee with a disability must accurately measure the skills and abilities the test was designed to\nmeasure rather than re\u0000ect the examinee's disability. ADA also mandates that employers make reasonable accommodations when testing\ndisabled examinees (e.g., by administering the test in accessible locations and modifying the testing materials).\nAnswer D: The IEP, Individualized Education Program, is a written document that's developed for each public school child who is eligible for\nspecial education. The IEP is designed to meet a child's unique educational needs and guarantees the necessary supports and services that\nare agreed upon and written for the child."}, {"question": "Someone who has achieved a high score on the Social theme of the RIASEC may be interested in a profession as a:", "options": {"A": "interior designer and musician", "B": "teacher or public speaker", "C": "psychologist or personnel manager", "D": "administrative assistant or sales manager"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Holland classi\u0000ed occupations and occupational interests into six themes, which he believed re\u0000ect basic personality characteristics. The\nrelationship between these themes is conceptualized in terms of a hexagon, with themes located closer to one another being more similar.\nStarting in the upper left of the hexagon, the themes are Realistic, Investigative, Artistic, Social, Enterprising, and Conventional (RIASEC).\nPsychologists and personnel managers are occupations that fall under the social theme.\nAnswer A: Both interior designers and musicians are included in the Artistic theme.\nAnswer B: While teachers fall under the Social theme, public speaking is included in the Enterprising theme.\nAnswer D: Administrative assistant falls under the Conventional theme. A sales manager is included in the Enterprising theme."}, {"question": "The Tower of London is a measure of:", "options": {"A": "attention, memory, and executive functioning", "B": "attention, visual memory, and hand-eye coordination", "C": "attention, visual memory, and executive functioning", "D": "visual-motor integration"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The Tower of London is a variation of the Tower of Hanoi puzzle and measures attention, memory, and executive functioning. It requires the\nexaminee to move disks, one at a time, so that they end up in a particular goal con\u0000guration. Poor performance has been linked to frontal lobe\ndamage, ADHD, autism, and depression.\nAnswers B, C, and D: The Tower of London speci\u0000cally measures attention, memory (working memory), and executive functioning."}, {"question": "Which assessment is a brief test of cognitive ability and is primarily used by employers to assist with hiring decisions?", "options": {"A": "The Wonderlic Basic Skills Test", "B": "The Strong Interest Inventory", "C": "The Wonderlic Personnel Test-Revised", "D": "The Kuder Occupational Interest Survey"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The Wonderlic Personnel Test-Revised (WPT-R) is a 12-minute test of cognitive ability for adults. It is used primarily by employers to assist with\nhiring decisions.\nAnswer A: The Wonderlic Basic Skills Test (WBST) is a 40-minute test that assesses job-related verbal and math skills and is used by\neducational institutions and employers to evaluate an individual's employability for an entry-level career position.\nAnswer B: The Strong Interest Inventory is an assessment that helps individuals identify their work personalities by exploring their interests in\nsix broad areas.\nAnswer D: The Kuder Occupational Interest Survey provides information on an examinee's interests in vocational interest areas."}, {"question": "Which of the following assessment instruments is NOT based on the Cattell-Horn-Carroll theory of cognitive abilities?", "options": {"A": "The Woodcock-Johnson IV", "B": "The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, Fifth Edition", "C": "The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2", "D": "The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2 (MMPI-2) is not based on the Horn-Cattell-Carroll (CHC) theory of cognitive abilities. The\nCHC uses overlapping cognitive theories to create a multidimensional classi\u0000cation system according to speci\u0000c, narrow, and broad ability\nlevels. The MMPI-2 measures a wide range of mental health symptoms and personality traits.\nAnswer A: The Woodcock-Johnson IV uses the CHC to measure the general intellectual ability and lower-order broad ability, including factors of\nComprehension-Knowledge (Gc), Fluid Reasoning (Gf), Short-Term Working Memory (Gwm), Cognitive Processing Speed (Gs), Auditory\nProcessing (Ga), Long Term Retrieval (Glr), and Visual Processing (Gv).\nAnswer B: The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, Fifth Edition uses the CHC to measure \u0000uid reasoning, quantitative reasoning, crystallized\nknowledge, short-term memory, and visual processing.\nAnswer D: The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV) uses the CHC to measure crystallized ability (Gc), visual\nprocessing (Gv), \u0000uid reasoning (Gf), short-term and working memory (Gsm), and processing speed (Gs)."}, {"question": "Which test is not a Wechsler assessment battery?", "options": {"A": "WISC", "B": "WIAT", "C": "WPPSI", "D": "WJ"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "WJ stands for Woodcock-Johnson. The Woodcock-Johnson IV (WJ IV) is used to assess intelligence, diagnose learning problems, and identify\nappropriate interventions. It consists of three co-normed batteries, including the WJ IV Tests of Cognitive Abilities, the WJ IV Tests of Oral\nLanguage, and the WJ IV Tests of Achievement. The WJ is not a Wechsler assessment battery.\nAnswer A: WISC stands for Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children.\nAnswer B: WIAT stands for Wechsler Individual Achievement Test.\nAnswer C: WPPSI stands for Wechsler Primary and Preschool Scale of Intelligence."}, {"question": "Which assessment would be least appropriate for an examinee with expressive language di\u0000culty?", "options": {"A": "Hiskey-Nebraska Test", "B": "Raven's Progressive Matrices", "C": "Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test", "D": "Kuhlmann-Anderson Test"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The Kuhlmann-Anderson Test-Eighth Edition is a multilevel battery for children in grades K through 12 and evaluates school learning ability. An\nexaminee's performance is reported in terms of Verbal, Quantitative, and Total Scores. Although the test assesses both verbal and nonverbal\nabilities, its content is less dependent on language than other individual and group intelligence tests. As performance on this assessment\nrequires some reliance on verbal expression, in contrast to the other options provided, this is the best response.\nAnswer A: The Hiskey-Nebraska Test is a measure of learning ability for children who have hearing or language impairments. It can be\nadministered verbally or in pantomime and consists of 12 nonverbal subtests that assess a broad range of intellectual abilities.\nAnswer B: Raven's Progressive Matrices are nonverbal measures of general intelligence (g) and are considered useful as multicultural tests\nbecause they are relatively independent of the effects of speci\u0000c educational and cultural learning. Because instructions for these tests are\nsimple and can be pantomimed if necessary, they can also be used with examinees who are hearing-impaired or non-English speaking or who\nhave aphasia or a physical disability.\nAnswer C: The Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test-Fourth Edition (PPVT-4) measures receptive vocabulary. It consists of cards that contain four\npictures. As each card is presented, the examiner verbally provides a stimulus word, and the examinee responds by indicating the picture that\nbest illustrates the meaning of the word. It is useful for people with a motor or speech impairment and can be administered to any examinee\nwho is able to hear the stimulus word, see the drawings, and in some way communicate a response."}, {"question": "Which type of assessment is also known as authentic assessment and involves \"observing and judging a pupil's skill in actually carrying out a physical activity or producing a product?\"", "options": {"A": "Dynamic Assessment", "B": "Performance-Based Assessment", "C": "Instructional Assessments", "D": "Curriculum-Based Measurement"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Performance-based assessment (PBA) is considered the \"centerpiece\" of Goals 2000, which was proposed by the Clinton administration. It is\nalso known as authentic assessment and involves \"observing and judging a pupil's skill in actually carrying out a physical activity (e.g., giving a\nspeech) or producing a product (e.g., building a birdhouse).\" Proponents of PBA argue that it provides an egalitarian method of evaluation and,\nconsequently, is useful for assessing students from culturally and linguistically diverse groups. A limitation of PBA is that it might re\u0000ect, to\nsome degree, prior knowledge and experience rather than what was learned in the current class.\nAnswer A: Dynamic assessment involves deliberate deviation from standardized testing procedures to obtain additional information about an\nexaminee and/or determine if he/she would bene\u0000t from assistance or instruction.\nAnswer C: Instructional assessments are directly linked to what examinees have learned in the classroom or other controlled setting and\ninclude curriculum-based measurement and performance-based assessment. As this response is not as speci\u0000c as performance-based\nassessment, this is not the best answer.\nAnswer D: Curriculum-based measurement (CBM) involves periodic assessment of school-aged children with brief standardized and validated\nmeasures of basic academic skills that re\u0000ect the current school curriculum for the purposes of evaluating instructional effectiveness and\nmaking instructional decisions."}, {"question": "Which of the following is most useful for explaining why so many people believe that their horoscopes accurately describe them?", "options": {"A": "Barnum effect", "B": "False consensus bias", "C": "Zeigarnik effect", "D": "Illusory correlation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Researchers have found that people are often willing to agree that vague descriptions of them, such as those presented in horoscopes,\naccurately describe their personal characteristics. The \"Barnum effect\" (also known as the \"Forer effect\") predicts that people tend to accept\nvague or general descriptions of themselves as accurate. The Barnum effect has been attributed to a number of factors, including gullibility,\nwishful thinking, and a con\u0000rmation bias.\nAnswer B: The \"false consensus bias\" is the tendency to overestimate the degree to which others agree with us.\nAnswer C: \"The Zeigarnik effect\" is the tendency to remember interrupted or uncompleted tasks better than completed ones.\nAnswer D: The \"Illusory correlation\" is the belief that there is a relationship between events when there actually isn't one."}, {"question": "The Barnum effect has been attributed to which of the following:", "options": {"A": "self-veri\u0000cation", "B": "overjusti\u0000cation", "C": "selective memory", "D": "self-monitoring"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The multifaceted nature of the self-concept has been offered as one explanation for the Barnum effect, which is the tendency for people to\naccept vague or general descriptions as accurate descriptions of themselves. The Barnum effect has also been attributed to other\nfactors including gullibility, selective memory, and the self-serving and con\u0000rmation biases.\nAnswers A, B, and D: None of these responses are associated with the Barnum effect."}, {"question": "Jenny and Craig are engaging in intercontinental dating via video chatting and other electronic means since they initially met through a dating website. She lives in Hong Kong, and he lives in Belfast. What does research indicate could be a potential problem as Jenny and Craig try to grow their relationship?", "options": {"A": "The long distance", "B": "Lack of quality and depth in their relationship", "C": "Their cultural differences", "D": "Lack of socialization outside of their relationship"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The use of the internet for interpersonal communication is not the same as traditional face-to-face communication in enriching the quality of\nlife. An absence of nonverbal cues, lack of warmth, and less demand for engagement in internet communication results in impersonality,\nshallow interactions, and di\u0000culty in building social support.\nAnswer A, C, and D: While these might be a part of ongoing long-distance relationship problems, they are not necessarily tied directly to the use\nof technology to foster the growth of a relationship."}, {"question": "The ___________ refers to the tendency to rely on case-speci\u0000c information and ignore or underuse certain data when estimating the likelihood of an event or characteristic.", "options": {"A": "Von Restorff effect", "B": "Forer effect", "C": "availability heuristic", "D": "base rate fallacy"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Researchers have identi\u0000ed a number of cognitive biases that distort our ability to accurately perceive or interpret situations. The phenomenon\ndescribed in the question is known as the \"base rate fallacy.\"\nAnswer A: The \"Von Restorff effect\" is the tendency to remember very unusual or unique objects or events (i.e., objects or events that \"stick out\nlike a sore thumb\").\nAnswer B: The \"Forer effect\" is another name for the \"Barnum effect\" and refers to the tendency to accept a vague or very general description of\noneself (e.g., a horoscope) as an accurate description.\nAnswer C: The\" availability heuristic\" is the tendency to focus on the most salient or affectively charged aspects of a stimulus or situation."}, {"question": "__________ are mental \"rules-of-thumb\" that help us make decisions or solve problems more quickly but that do not necessarily lead to the optimal decision or solution.", "options": {"A": "Prototypes", "B": "Mnemonics", "C": "Algorithms", "D": "Heuristics"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "Heuristics are mental shortcuts (rules-of-thumb) that simplify decision-making or problem-solving. For example, you would be relying on a\nheuristic if you assume that a higher price is always associated with a better quality product and then use price to make decisions about what\nproducts to buy.\nAnswer A: Prototypes are representative examples of a concept or category.\nAnswer B: Mnemonics are strategies used to improve memory.\nAnswer C: Algorithms are step-by-step problem-solving procedures or strategies that allow individuals to solve problems even if they do not\ncompletely understand the problem. (For the exam, you want to know the de\u0000nition of each of the terms listed in the answers to this question.)"}, {"question": "Evolutionary psychologists have proposed that a woman's waist-to-hip ratio provides potential mates information about the woman's:", "options": {"A": "reproductive potential", "B": "ability to take on more traditionally male-dominated roles", "C": "earning potential", "D": "intelligence"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Evolutionary psychologists propose that a woman's waist-to-hip ratio provides potential mates with information about her reproductive\npotential. According to Singh (1993), a waist-hip ratio of .7 is associated with greater fertility and health. (Adaptive signi\u0000cance of waist-to-hip\nratio and female physical attractiveness, Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 65, 293-307, 1993).\nAnswers B, C, and D: Evolutionary psychologists suggest that a woman's waist-to-hip ratio provides information about her reproductive\ncapabilities to potential mates."}, {"question": "As described by Kobasa (1979), the three components of \"hardiness\" are:", "options": {"A": "attachment, caring, and intimacy", "B": "commitment, control, and challenge", "C": "intellect, emotion, and will", "D": "what we do, what we think, and what we feel"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "\"Hardiness\" is a psychological style that has been linked to the ability to maintain good physical health under conditions of stress. According to\nKobasa (1979), hardy people (1) have a strong sense of commitment to their lives and work, (2) believe they can control or in\u0000uence the events\nthey encounter, and (3) view change as a positive challenge (Stressful life events, personality, and health: An inquiry into hardiness. Journal of\nPersonality and Social Psychology, 37, 1-11, 1979).\nAnswers A, C, D: Incorrect. Commitment, control, and challenge are the three components of \"hardiness,\" as described by Kobasa."}, {"question": "If you believe that during a full moon people tend to act \"crazier,\" you will be likely to notice incidents of bizarre behavior during a full moon and ignore similar incidents when the moon is not full. This tendency is referred to as the:", "options": {"A": "con\u0000rmation bias", "B": "saliency bias", "C": "self-ful\u0000lling prophecy bias", "D": "correspondence bias"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "In this situation, you are noticing events that con\u0000rm your beliefs. This tendency is referred to as the \"con\u0000rmation bias.\"\nAnswer B: The \"saliency bias\" occurs when a vivid (salient) stimulus in a situation is viewed as the cause of behavior in that situation.\nAnswer C: The \"self-ful\u0000lling prophecy\" effect occurs when one's expectations alter the behaviors of the targets of those expectations.\nAnswer D: The \"correspondence bias\" is another name for the \"fundamental attribution bias,\" which is the tendency to attribute the behaviors of\nothers to dispositional factors. (For the exam, be familiar with each of these terms.)"}, {"question": "The desire to bolster another person's welfare without direct regard for one's own interests is known as:", "options": {"A": "altruism", "B": "sympathy", "C": "empathy", "D": "identi\u0000cation"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Altruism is regarded as the desire to enhance another person's welfare without direct regard to one's own interests.\nAnswer B: Sympathy is the ability to perceive or understand another's misfortune.\nAnswer C: Empathy is the ability to recognize and respond sympathetically to the feelings of others.\nAnswer D: Identi\u0000cation occurs when the person changes his or her behavior because he or she wants to be liked by or identi\u0000ed with another\nperson."}, {"question": "Berscheid's (1989) \"emotion-in-relationships\" model predicts that strong emotions in close relationships are:", "options": {"A": "related to the degree to which prepotent needs are mutually satis\u0000ed", "B": "a function of each partners level of differentiation", "C": "moderated by personal beliefs about the acceptability of emotional expression", "D": "elicited by unexpected interruptions in routine interactions"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "According to E. Berscheid (1989), a partner is most likely to experience a high degree of emotion in an intimate relationship when his/her\npartner's behavior disrupts an organized sequence of behavior, with the nature of the emotion depending on the consequence of the disruption.\nAnswers A, B, and C: The model predicts that strong emotions in relationships are brought on by unexpected disruptions to routine interactions."}, {"question": "To determine the degree of association between number of hours studied and score on the EPPP for a sample of 50 licensure candidates, you would use which of the following bivariate correlation coe\u0000cients?", "options": {"A": "Spearman rho", "B": "Pearson r", "C": "Phi coe\u0000cient", "D": "Point biserial coe\u0000cient"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The Pearson product moment correlation coe\u0000cient (Pearson r) is the appropriate correlation coe\u0000cient when both variables are measured on\na continuous scale. Both variables in this example are on a continuous scale.\nAnswer A: Spearman rho is used to correlate two variables that are measured in terms of ranks.\nAnswer C: The phi coe\u0000cient is used to determine the correlation between two dichotomous variables.\nAnswer D: The point biserial correlation coe\u0000cient is appropriate when one variable is continuous and the other is a true dichotomy."}, {"question": "To calculate an effect size (Cohen's d), you need_____", "options": {"A": "the standard error of estimate of the criterion", "B": "the median scores for the pre- and post-tests", "C": "the means of the experimental and control groups", "D": "the actual and predicted scores for the outcome measure"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "Cohen's d is one of several methods researchers use to calculate an effect size and indicates the magnitude of the effect of a treatment (i.e.,\nthe difference between the means of the experimental [treatment] and control [no treatment] groups). You calculate Cohen's d by subtracting\nthe mean of the control group from the mean of the experimental group and dividing the result by a pooled standard deviation or by the control\ngroup standard deviation.\nAnswers A, B, and D: You calculate Cohen's d by subtracting the mean of the control group from the mean of the experimental group and\ndividing the result by a pooled standard deviation or by the control group standard deviation."}, {"question": "To compare the effectiveness of three different doses of a new drug for Attention De\u0000cit Hyperactivity Disorder (ADHD), an investigator obtains a sample of 16 adults recently diagnosed with ADHD. All participants in the study will receive all three doses of the drug but in different order: Four participants will \u0000rst receive the low dose for two weeks, then the moderate dose for two weeks, and then the high dose for two weeks; four other participants will \u0000rst receive the moderate dose for two weeks, then the low dose for two weeks, then the high dose for two weeks; and so on. Which of the following research designs is the investigator using?", "options": {"A": "Analogue", "B": "Dismantling", "C": "Counterbalanced", "D": "Solomon four-group"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The investigator is using a within-subjects design that involves having each participant receive all levels of the independent variable.\nCounterbalanced designs are within-subjects designs that help control order or practice effects by administering the levels of the independent\nvariable to different subjects (or groups of subjects) in a different order.\nAnswer A: The term \"analogue\" is used in the context of research design to refer to the investigation of a well-de\u0000ned research question in well-\ncontrolled conditions.\nAnswer B: The dismantling strategy is used to determine the effects of each component of a multi-component treatment and involves\nadministering different components to different groups of participants.\nAnswer D: The Solomon four-group design is used to evaluate the impact of pretesting on a study's internal and external validity."}, {"question": "In a positively skewed distribution of scores_____", "options": {"A": "the mean is larger than the median which is larger than the mode", "B": "the median is larger than the mode which is larger than the mean", "C": "the mode is larger than the median which is larger than the mean", "D": "the mode is larger than the mean which is larger than the median"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "In a positively skewed distribution, the distribution of scores is extended by a few outliers on the positive end of the distribution. Because the\nmean is affected more than the other measures of central tendency by outliers, it will be the largest number in a positively skewed distribution.\nThe median is always the middle score, so it will be second in magnitude, and the mode will be the smallest in magnitude. A \u0000gure that\nillustrates the relationships between the mean, median, and mode is included in the Statistics and Research Design chapter of the written study\nmaterials.\nAnswer B: By de\u0000nition, the median is always the middle score regardless of skewness.\nAnswer C: This describes a negatively skewed distribution.\nAnswer D: By de\u0000nition, the median is always the middle score regardless of skewness. The mode in a positively skewed distribution would be\nthe smallest in magnitude."}, {"question": "When calculating the F-ratio for an analysis of variance, which of the following provides an estimate of variability due to the effects of error only?", "options": {"A": "MST", "B": "MSW", "C": "MSB", "D": "MSA"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "You calculate the F-ratio by dividing a measure of treatment effects plus error (MSB) by a measure of error only (MSW). MSW stands for \"mean\nsquare within\" and is a pooled measure of variability within each of the treatment groups. It is an estimate of variability that is due to error only.\nAnswer A: MST is \"mean square total\" and is a measure of both treatment effects and error (MSB plus MSW).\nAnswer C: MSB stands for \"mean square between\" and is a measure of variability between treatment groups. It is an estimate of variability due\nto treatment effects plus error.\nAnswer D: MSA is not one of the terms used to calculate the F-ratio."}, {"question": "A researcher conducts two studies to test the effects of a cognitive-behavioral technique on attention span in children with ADHD. The studies are identical in procedures, number of participants, and so on, but in Study #1, p<.05, while in Study #2, p>.10. Based on this information, you can conclude that_____", "options": {"A": "the results of Study #1 indicate an effect opposite of the effect produced in Study #2", "B": "the effect size in Study #1 was larger than the effect size in Study #2", "C": "the researcher has made a Type II error in Study #2", "D": "a one-tailed test was used in Study #1; a two-tailed test in Study #2"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The difference between the two studies is that one achieved statistical signi\u0000cance (p<.05), while the other did not (p>.10). Signi\u0000cance is\nachieved when the effect size (effect of the IV) is large enough for a researcher to detect when using a statistical test. There is enough\ninformation given in this question to draw this conclusion.\nAnswer A: The results may or may not be in the same direction. There is insu\u0000cient information in the question to determine which is the case.\nAnswer C: Study #2 may involve a Type II error, or, alternatively, Study #1 may involve a Type I error. Whether either of these is true cannot be\ndetermined from the information provided in the question.\nAnswer D: There is nothing in the question that implies that this is true."}, {"question": "Statistical regression threatens a study's internal validity when_____", "options": {"A": "individuals are included in a study because of their extremely high (or low) scores on a pretest", "B": "individuals are included in a study because of their moderate (mid-range) scores on a pretest", "C": "the measure of the dependent variable is modi\u0000ed during the course of a study", "D": "ratings on the measure of the dependent variable are affected by \"criterion contamination\""}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "Statistical regression is the tendency of extreme scores to \"regress\" or move toward the mean on retesting and is a threat to a study's internal\nvalidity whenever researchers select participants on the basis of their extreme pre-test scores. In this situation, it may not be possible to\ndetermine if a change in post-test scores is due to the effects of the independent variable or to statistical regression.\nAnswer B: Mid-range scores are not likely to result in a large enough difference in scores to be considered a threat to internal validity. Statistical\nregression is a threat to a study's internal validity whenever a researcher selects participants based on their extreme pre-test scores.\nAnswer C: This is a typical practice during research. Statistical regression is a threat to a study's internal validity whenever a researcher selects\nparticipants based on their extreme pre-test scores.\nAnswer D: Criterion contamination occurs when the dependent variables (criterion) are in\u0000uenced by factors NOT related to the study, whereas\nstatistical regression is a threat to a study's internal validity whenever participants are selected based on their extreme pre-test scores."}, {"question": "The split-plot ANOVA is used when_____", "options": {"A": "one of the independent variables is an extraneous variable", "B": "all independent variables are quantitative", "C": "the researcher has used a \"matched-pair\" design", "D": "the researcher has used a \"mixed\" design"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "The split-plot ANOVA is one of several forms of the ANOVA that you want to be familiar with for the exam. The split-plot ANOVA is also referred\nto as the mixed ANOVA, where one of the independent variables is \u0000xed, and the other independent variable is a repeated measures variable.\nFor additional information on the split-plot ANOVA, see the Statistics and Research Design chapter of the written study materials.\nAnswer A: You would not include an extraneous variable as an independent variable in this model. The split-plot ANOVA is also referred to as\nthe mixed ANOVA, where one of the independent variables is \u0000xed, and the other independent variable is a repeated measures variable.\nAnswer B: The split-plot ANOVA is also referred to as the mixed ANOVA, where one of the independent variables is \u0000xed, and the other\nindependent variable is a repeated measure variable.\nAnswer C: Matched pairs are a type of repeated measures design where the repeated measurement is on similar subjects that are matched on\nkey attributes rather than on the same subjects."}, {"question": "To determine if alcohol use is better controlled through aversion therapy, self-hypnosis, or cognitive self-control, a researcher randomly assigns participants who drink at least 6 alcoholic beverages per day to one of the treatment groups and determines the average number of alcoholic beverages each participant consumes each day during the 30 days following administration of the appropriate intervention. Which of the following scales of measurement is the researcher using for the dependent variable in this study?", "options": {"A": "Ordinal", "B": "Ratio", "C": "Interval", "D": "Nominal"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The dependent variable in this study is the number of alcoholic beverages consumed. A ratio scale has the properties of order, equal intervals,\nand the only one with an absolute zero point -- the number of alcoholic beverages has all three properties.\nAnswer A: Ordinal scales divide observations into categories and provides information on the order of those categories.\nAnswer C: An interval scale has the property of the order and equal intervals, and sometimes an arbitrary zero point.\nAnswer D: Nominal scales are used to sort variables into unordered categories."}, {"question": "A researcher compares the effectiveness of two different relaxation techniques for relieving test anxiety by randomly assigning 30 students who have high levels of anxiety to one of the treatments so that each group includes 15 students. If the researcher uses the t-test for independent (unrelated) samples to analyze the data obtained, the degrees of freedom will be_____.", "options": {"A": "13", "B": "14", "C": "28", "D": "29"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The formula for the degrees of freedom for the t-test for independent samples is N-2, where N is the total number of participants. The total\nnumber of participants is 30, so 30 - 2 = 28.\nAnswers A, B, and D: The formula for the degrees of freedom for the t-test for independent samples is N-2, where N is the total number of\nparticipants."}, {"question": "The standard error of the mean is used to_____.", "options": {"A": "construct a con\u0000dence interval around an obtained test score", "B": "construct a con\u0000dence interval around a predicted criterion score", "C": "calculate the standard deviation of the sampling distribution", "D": "calculate an estimate of the standard deviation of test scores in a population"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "There are three standard errors that you should be familiar with for the exam - the standard error of the mean, the standard error of\nmeasurement, and the standard error of the estimate. The standard error of the mean is the standard deviation of the sampling distribution of\nthe mean. You calculate this by dividing the population standard deviation by the square root of the sample size.\nAnswer A: The standard error of measurement is used to construct a con\u0000dence interval around an obtained (measured) test score.\nAnswer B: The standard error of the estimate is used to construct a con\u0000dence interval around a predicted (estimated) criterion score.\nAnswer D: The standard deviation of test scores for a sample can be used to estimate the standard deviation of test scores in the population\nby using (N-1) instead of N in the denominator of the formula for the standard deviation."}, {"question": "You design a study to compare the effectiveness of three different treatments for stress. You divide 30 EPPP candidates equally among three treatments and measure their stress level before the treatment begins and one week and six weeks after applying the treatment. The design of your study is best described as_____.", "options": {"A": "counterbalanced", "B": "mixed", "C": "natural groups", "D": "between-groups"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "This study has two independent variables - treatment and time. This is a mixed design because one of the variables is a between-groups\nvariable (treatment), while the other is a within-subjects variable (time).\nAnswer A: Counterbalancing involves presenting the different levels of an IV to different subjects or groups of subjects in different orders.\nAnswer C: The term \"natural groups\" is used to describe a study in which the IV is an organismic variable (e.g., intelligence, self-esteem).\nAnswer D: In a between-groups design, comparisons of different levels of the IV are made between different groups."}, {"question": "To determine how well an examinee did on a test compared to other examinees, you would use:", "options": {"A": "criterion-referenced interpretation", "B": "domain-referenced interpretation", "C": "objectives-referenced interpretation", "D": "norm-referenced interpretation"}, "correct": "D", "explanation": "When using norm-referenced interpretation, an examinee's test performance is compared to the performance of members of the norm group\n(other people who have taken the test).\nAnswer A: When using criterion-referenced interpretation, an examinee's test performance is interpreted in terms of an external criterion (i.e., in\nterms of a speci\u0000c standard of performance).\nAnswer B: Domain-referenced interpretation is a type of criterion-referenced interpretation. It is used to determine how much of a speci\u0000c\nknowledge domain the examinee has mastered.\nAnswer C: Objectives-referenced interpretation is another type of criterion-referenced interpretation. It involves interpreting an examinee's\nperformance in terms of achievement of instructional objectives."}, {"question": "The kappa statistic is used as a measure of reliability when data are:", "options": {"A": "nominal or ordinal (discontinuous)", "B": "interval or ratio (continuous)", "C": "nonlinear", "D": "metric"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "The kappa statistic is used to evaluate inter-rater reliability. The kappa statistic is used to measure the consistency of ratings assigned by two\nraters when data are nominal or ordinal. Some authors use the term \"discontinuous\" to refer to nominal and other discrete data - i.e., data that\nrepresents noncontinuous categories.\nAnswers B, C, & D are incorrect."}, {"question": "The purpose of \"rotation\" in factor analysis is to:", "options": {"A": "obtain a pattern of factor loadings that is easier to interpret", "B": "reduce the impact of measurement error on the factor loadings", "C": "reduce the magnitude of the communalities", "D": "obtain a clearer pattern of communalities"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "For the exam, you don't need to understand what rotation involves, but you do want to be familiar with its purpose. The pattern of factor\nloadings in the initial factor matrix is often di\u0000cult to interpret, so the factors are rotated to obtain a pattern that's easier to interpret.\nAnswer B: Rotation does not reduce the effects of measurement error.\nAnswer C: Rotation alters the magnitude of the factor loadings but not the magnitude of the commonalities.\nAnswer D: This is not the purpose of rotation in factor analysis."}, {"question": "The point at which an item characteristic curve intercepts the vertical (Y) axis indicates which of the following?", "options": {"A": "The item's di\u0000culty level", "B": "The probability of answering the item correctly by guessing", "C": "The degree to which the item is affected by measurement error", "D": "The item's ability to distinguish between high and low achievers"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "An item characteristic curve usually provides three pieces of information - item di\u0000culty, item discrimination, and the probability of getting the\nitem correct by guessing alone. The point at which the curve intercepts the Y-axis indicates the probability of answering the item correctly by\nguessing.\nAnswer A: Item di\u0000culty is determined by identifying the ability level that is required for there to be a 50% chance of getting the item correct. In\nan item characteristic curve, the horizontal axis indicates examinees' ability level, and the vertical axis indicates the probability of getting the\nitem correct.\nAnswer C: The degree to which the item is affected by measurement error is not provided by an item characteristic curve.\nAnswer D: The slope of the curve indicates the item's ability to discriminate between low and high achievers."}, {"question": "According to classical test theory, X = T + E, where E refers to:", "options": {"A": "random error", "B": "equivalency", "C": "predictive error", "D": "estimated consistency"}, "correct": "A", "explanation": "According to classical test theory, an examinee's obtained test score (X) is composed of two components -- T and E. \"T\" refers to true score\nvariability, while \"E\" refers to random (measurement) error.\nAnswers B, C, & D are incorrect."}, {"question": "\"Consensual observer drift\" tends to __________ the size of the inter-rater reliability coe\u0000cient.", "options": {"A": "arti\u0000cially de\u0000ate", "B": "arti\u0000cially in\u0000ate", "C": "accurately de\u0000ate", "D": "accurately in\u0000ate"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "Consensual observer drift occurs when two or more raters work together and in\u0000uence each other's ratings. Consensual observer drift tends to\nmake the raters' ratings more similar, which arti\u0000cially in\u0000ates the inter-rater reliability coe\u0000cient.\nAnswers A, C, & D are incorrect."}, {"question": "The minimum and maximum values of the standard error of estimate are:", "options": {"A": "-1 and +1", "B": "0 and 1", "C": "0 and the standard deviation of the criterion", "D": "0 and the standard deviation of the predictor"}, "correct": "C", "explanation": "The standard error of estimate equals the standard deviation of Y (the criterion) times the square root of 1 minus the validity coe\u0000cient\nsquared. When the validity coe\u0000cient is 1, the standard error equals 0 (there is no error), and when the validity coe\u0000cient is 0, the standard\nerror equals the standard deviation of the criterion scores.\nAnswers A, B, & D are incorrect."}, {"question": "A test's convergent and divergent (discriminant) validity provide information about its:", "options": {"A": "incremental validity", "B": "construct validity", "C": "content validity", "D": "face validity"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "One way to evaluate a test's construct validity is to determine if it has high correlations with measures of the same or related traits (convergent\nvalidity) and low correlations with measures of unrelated traits (divergent validity).\nAnswer A: Incremental validity refers to the degree to which a test improves decision-making accuracy.\nAnswer C: Content validity is established primarily by having subject matter experts review test items to make sure they are a representative\nsample of the content domain(s) assessed by the test.\nAnswer D: Face validity refers to the extent that a test appears to be valid to test-takers and \"looks like\" it is measuring what it is supposed to\nbe measuring."}, {"question": "Of the various types of test validity, ________ validity has been described as the broadest category of validity because it overlaps and encompasses all other types.", "options": {"A": "content", "B": "construct", "C": "criterion-related", "D": "external"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The classical (tripartite) view of validity distinguishes between three major types -- content, criterion-related, and construct. From this\nperspective, construct validity refers to the degree to which a test measures the construct it was designed to measure. Due to changes in the\nconceptualization of validity, methods for evaluating validity, and methods of interpreting test scores, construct validity is now described by\nmany experts as a unifying concept of validity. See, M. E. Strauss and G. T. Smith, Construct validity: Advances in theory and methodology,\nAnnual Review of Clinical Psychology, 5, 1-25, 2009.\nAnswer A: A test has content validity to the extent that it adequately samples the content domain that it is designed to measure. The\nestablishment of content validity relies primarily on the judgment of subject matter experts.\nAnswer C: Criterion-related validity is relevant when test scores are used to draw conclusions about an examinee's performance on another\nmeasure.\nAnswer D: External validity focuses on the generalizability of one study to other conditions, individuals, and situations."}, {"question": "An examinee obtains a score of 70 on a test that has a mean of 80, a standard deviation of 15, and a standard error of measurement of 5. The 95% con\u0000dence interval for the examinee's score is:", "options": {"A": "65 to 75", "B": "60 to 80", "C": "55 to 85", "D": "50 to 90"}, "correct": "B", "explanation": "The 95% con\u0000dence interval for an obtained test score is constructed by multiplying the standard error of measurement by 1.96 and adding\nand subtracting the result to and from the examinee's obtained score. An interval of 60 to 80 is closest to the 95% con\u0000dence interval and is\nobtained by multiplying the standard error by 2.0 (instead of 1.96) and then adding and subtracting the result (10) to and from the examinee's\nscore of 70.\nAnswer A: This answer re\u0000ects a 68% con\u0000dence interval. The 68% con\u0000dence interval is constructed by adding and subtracting one standard\nerror to and from the examinee's obtained score. Adding and subtracting one standard error (5) to and from the examinee's score of 50 results\nin a con\u0000dence interval of 65 to 75.\nAnswer C: This answer re\u0000ects a 99% con\u0000dence interval. The 99% con\u0000dence interval is constructed by multiplying the standard error of\nmeasurement by 2.58 and adding and subtracting the result to and from the examinee's obtained score. An interval of 55 to 85 is closest to the\n99% con\u0000dence interval and is obtained by multiplying the standard error (5) by 3.0 (instead of 2.58) and then adding and subtracting the result\n(15) to and from the examinee's score of 70.\nAnswer D: A con\u0000dence interval of 50 to 90 would be the result of multiplying the standard error by 4 and then adding and subtracting the result\n(20) from the examinee's score of 70. This is not a meaningful calculation, as the vast majority of con\u0000dence intervals are constructed\nfollowing the 68-95-99 rule."}]};
